TY  - JOUR
TI  - BlinkChange: Active perception bi-temporal reliable representation for disaster impact understanding from remote sensing imagery
AU  - Chen H.
JO  - ISPRS Journal of Photogrammetry and Remote Sensing
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-12-01
VL  - 242
SP  - 566-584
DO  - 10.1016/j.isprsjprs.2026.09.014
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.isprsjprs.2026.09.014
AB  - Remote sensing disaster impact understanding aims to rapidly identify key affected objects, determine damage severity, and generate effective semantic representations from pre- and post-disaster imagery, thereby providing essential support for emergency response and post-disaster assessment. However, most existing methods are designed for a single task and generally rely on a static and uniform paradigm of global bi-temporal comparison, which limits their ability to effectively model the critical cues in disaster scenes. To address this issue, we propose BlinkChange, an active perception-driven framework for reliable bi-temporal representation learning toward remote sensing disaster impact understanding. Inspired by the human visual system, which understands complex scenes through a sequential, blink-like focus on salient regions, the proposed framework introduces learnable perception frames into a unified video-modeling backbone, transforming bi-temporal modeling from passive discrepancy comparison into a stage-wise representation optimization process driven by key disaster-affected regions, and providing a reliable representational foundation for downstream disaster understanding tasks. Specifically, we first design a change-aware discrepancy construction module (CADC) to generate change context with stronger disaster-semantic discriminability in a content-aware manner. On this basis, we further propose a saliency-guided dynamic perception enhancement strategy (SDPE), which converts spatial saliency into perception-level importance allocation. This is followed by a soft enhancement–suppression updating mechanism (SEU) that continuously strengthens critical disaster cues while suppressing redundant responses during progressive encoding, enabling the model to gradually shift its representational focus from global scene correspondence to the key disaster-affected regions that truly determine task decisions. Extensive experiments demonstrate that the proposed approach achieves state-of-the-art (SOTA) image captioning performance on two recent large-scale disaster understanding datasets, RSCC and DisasterM3, and also delivers strong performance on the widely used building damage assessment dataset xBD with relatively low model complexity. Further ablation studies, visualizations, and external-scene generalization experiments verify the effectiveness of each component and show that the proposed method learns bi-temporal representations that are more sensitive to fine-grained damage cues and more robust to complex background interference. The code is released at https://github.com/ChenHuyoo.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - SF-CFNet: Spatial-frequency collaborative fusion detection network for AI-generated satellite images
AU  - Tang Y.
JO  - Expert Systems with Applications
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-12-01
VL  - 330
DO  - 10.1016/j.eswa.2026.133006
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.eswa.2026.133006
AB  - With the rapid progress of generative models, such as generative adversarial networks (GANs) and diffusion models, it has become feasible to readily synthesize forged satellite images with high visual fidelity. This development presents substantial challenges to the existing forgery detection methods for satellite images. Furthermore, most current satellite image detection models are plagued by inherent limitations, including a lack of sufficient ability to capture high-frequency forgery cues, inadequate integration of multi-spectral features, and weak generalization performance under complex scenes and cross-domain data distributions. To address these challenges, a novel Spatial-Frequency Collaborative Fusion Network (SF-CFNet) is put forward for the detection of AI-generated satellite images. Specifically, a frequency-specific dual-attention (FDA) mechanism is devised to enhance the discrimination of crucial forgery-related features. Meanwhile, an adaptive multi-scale feature fusion module integrates hierarchical information across diverse scales. Additionally, a DCT-guided dynamic threshold strategy is incorporated to enhance the robustness of forgery detection under different satellite image qualities. Extensive experiments are conducted on a self-constructed SMTD dataset containing 20,000 samples, including 10,000 forged satellite images generated by GANs and diffusion models, as well as the public DeepFake_GeoSat dataset. Experimental results demonstrate that SF-CFNet consistently outperforms state-of-the-art methods under the main validated experimental setting, achieving an accuracy of 98.95%, precision of 99.75%, recall of 98.17%, and an F1-score of 98.96%.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - DescribeEarth: Detailed localized captioning for remote sensing images
AU  - Li K.
JO  - ISPRS Journal of Photogrammetry and Remote Sensing
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-12-01
VL  - 242
SP  - 860-872
DO  - 10.1016/j.isprsjprs.2026.09.019
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.isprsjprs.2026.09.019
AB  - Automated textual description of remote sensing images is crucial for unlocking their full potential in diverse applications, from environmental monitoring to urban planning and disaster management. However, existing studies in remote sensing image captioning primarily focus on the image level, lacking object-level fine-grained interpretation, which prevents the full utilization and transformation of the rich semantic and structural information contained in remote sensing images. Consequently, we propose Geo-DLC, a novel task for fine-grained object-level image captioning in remote sensing. To systematically establish and evaluate this new paradigm, we introduce a comprehensive suite encompassing a dataset, a benchmark, and a specialized model. Specifically, we construct DE-Dataset, a large-scale collection comprising 25 categories and 261,806 annotated instances with detailed descriptions of object attributes, relationships, and contexts. To provide a rigorous evaluation standard, we develop DE-Benchmark, an LLM-assisted evaluation protocol based on question answering, to accurately measure the capabilities of models on the Geo-DLC task. Furthermore, we present DescribeEarth, an MLLM architecture explicitly tailored for this task. It integrates a scale-adaptive focal strategy and a domain-guided fusion module, which leverage the features of remote sensing Vision-Language Model (VLM) to encode high-resolution details and category priors while maintaining the global context. Extensive evaluations show that DescribeEarth achieves the highest aggregate scores on the simple and complex subsets and matches GPT-4o on the out-of-distribution subset of DE-Benchmark. Its clearest gains occur in surrounding-context coverage and in categories whose interpretation depends strongly on overhead spatial layout and remote-sensing context. All data, code, and weights are publicly available at https://github.com/earth-insights/DescribeEarth.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - PVDINO: Extracting photovoltaic panels from high-resolution remote sensing imagery via vision foundation model and dual-stream edge mining
AU  - Zhang Z.
JO  - Applied Energy
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-12-01
VL  - 425
DO  - 10.1016/j.apenergy.2026.128571
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.apenergy.2026.128571
AB  - Accurate extraction of large-scale photovoltaic (PV) facilities from high-resolution satellite and Unmanned Aerial Vehicle (UAV) imagery is crucial for achieving global carbon neutrality. However, existing methods struggle to balance deep semantic representation with fine-grained edge localization amidst multi-scale morphological variations and complex background interferences. To overcome this limitation, this paper pioneers the introduction of the self-supervised vision foundation model (DINOv3) into remote sensing PV extraction by proposing PVDINO, a novel end-to-end framework. First, a DINOv3 Semantic Adaptation Encoder effectively adapts multi-level features to enhance deep global semantic representation. Second, an Edge Feature Mining Encoder introduces frequency-domain priors and flow field estimation for the fine-grained mining of shallow and deep features, respectively. Finally, a Cross-Guided Fusion Decoder synergizes these global semantic and edge-enhanced features for highly accurate extraction. Extensive experiments on the AIR-PV, PVP, and H-RPVS datasets demonstrate PVDINO's superiority. Notably, it yields an F1-score of 96.78% and an Intersection over Union (IoU) of 93.88% on the PVP dataset, outperforming state-of-the-art methods by up to 1.61% in IoU. Furthermore, PVDINO exhibits exceptional robustness in cross-scene generalization and large-scale mapping, providing a reliable automated monitoring solution for macro-energy infrastructure. The source code will be available at: https://github.com/whuzzzz/PVDINO.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Multi-temporal remote sensing and GIS-based geospatial assessment of iron ore mining dynamics and LULC impacts in Bailadila Range, Bastar Craton, Central India
AU  - Bansal R.
JO  - Results in Engineering
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-12-01
VL  - 32
DO  - 10.1016/j.rineng.2026.111660
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.rineng.2026.111660
AB  - The Bailadila iron ore range located in the Bastar Craton of southern Chhattisgarh represents one of India’s most important high-grade hematite mining provinces. Rapid expansion of open-cast mining activities in this region has significantly altered land cover, terrain morphology, and environmental conditions. This study applies an integrated Remote Sensing and Geographic Information System (RS–GIS) approach to evaluate spatial patterns of mining expansion, land use/land cover (LULC) dynamics, and environmental disturbances in the Bailadila range. Multi-temporal Sentinel-2B satellite imagery and ALOS PALSAR Digital Elevation Model (DEM) data were processed to generate vegetation, thermal, and terrain indicators including the normalized difference vegetation index (NDVI), land surface temperature (LST), and mining disturbance index (MDI). Supervised classification using machine learning techniques produced detailed thematic maps representing forest cover, mining areas, exposed rock, and other land cover classes. The results indicate that dense forest remains the dominant land cover type, occupying approximately 55.7% (184 km²) of the total mapped area, while moderately dense forest accounts for 29.66% (97.96 km²). Active mining and exposed rock surfaces cover around 7.05 km² (2.13%), reflecting localized but intensive landscape modification. NDVI analysis revealed values ranging from −0.29 to 0.87 in 2020 and −0.37 to 0.88 in 2025, indicating vegetation degradation near mining zones. Land Surface Temperature analysis showed maximum temperatures of 44.09 °C in 2020 and 38.01 °C in 2025, with higher thermal anomalies concentrated in disturbed mining areas. The Mining Disturbance Index increased from 0.06 to 2.41 in 2020 to 0.05–2.68 in 2025, highlighting progressive environmental stress associated with mining expansion. Accuracy assessment using 350 ground reference samples produced an overall classification accuracy of about 94% with a Kappa coefficient of 0.92, confirming the reliability of the classification results. This study advances the application of integrated multi-temporal RS-GIS techniques for assessing mining-induced landscape transformation and provides a reliable decision-support framework for environmental monitoring, sustainable mine management, and policy planning in mineral-rich mountainous regions.
ER  -

