TI  - Sketch and paint: A structural–temporal aware refinement network for fine-grained crop mapping on satellite image time series
AU  - Cui W.
JO  - ISPRS Journal of Photogrammetry and Remote Sensing
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-12-01
VL  - 242
SP  - 607-627
DO  - 10.1016/j.isprsjprs.2026.09.009
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.isprsjprs.2026.09.009
AB  - Crop type mapping based on Satellite Image Time Series (SITS) is a cornerstone of large-scale agricultural monitoring. However, existing methods often struggle in agricultural landscapes characterized by field fragmentation and crop phenological variability, where inter-class boundary ambiguity and intra-class phenological heterogeneity make it difficult to preserve sharp crop boundaries while maintaining spatial consistency. Inspired by the human cognitive process of “sketching outlines before filling in details”, we propose STAR, a sequential structural–temporal aware refinement framework for fine-grained crop mapping. STAR integrates three complementary components. The Structure Prior Injection (SPI) module explicitly extracts high-frequency geometric cues in shallow layers to anchor pixel-level boundary information and mitigate boundary degradation caused by deep feature diffusion. The Decoupled Phenology Encoder (DPE) module independently models pixel-wise growth trajectories, reducing spectral interference among phenologically similar crops and enhancing temporal discriminability. The Spatial Consistency Refinement (SCR) module further incorporates spatial context to enforce semantic coherence within structurally constrained regions without compromising boundary integrity. We evaluate STAR on four public crop mapping datasets covering study areas in France, Germany, and South Africa. Experimental results demonstrate that STAR consistently achieves state-of-the-art (SOTA) performance across multiple metrics. Qualitative analyses further confirm improved boundary delineation and field-level spatial coherence, highlighting the effectiveness of the proposed structural–temporal decoupling strategy. The source code is publicly available at https://github.com/cuiwei118/STAR.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - SaKD: Lightweight infrared small target detection via Spatial-Aware Knowledge Distillation for edge deployment
AU  - Ma Q.
JO  - ISPRS Journal of Photogrammetry and Remote Sensing
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-12-01
VL  - 242
SP  - 745-759
DO  - 10.1016/j.isprsjprs.2026.09.018
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.isprsjprs.2026.09.018
AB  - Accurate infrared small target detection (IRSTD) in remote sensing remains challenging because targets of interest typically occupy only a few pixels and provide limited spatial cues against complex backgrounds. Existing high-performance methods rely on complex networks to enhance weak target representations, limiting their use on resource-constrained edge devices. Lightweight models reduce computation but struggle to capture scarce spatial cues of small targets. To preserve these spatial cues in lightweight models for remote sensing applications, we propose SaKD, a Spatial-Aware Knowledge Distillation method for IRSTD. SaKD explicitly captures complementary spatial cues associated with target responses from teacher predictions, thereby providing richer spatial discriminative supervision for the student model. Without changing the student inference structure, SaKD introduces two training stage components. The Multi-Scale Self Guidance Mechanism first derives auxiliary supervision from the student’s own responses at multiple scales to strengthen target representation before teacher guidance is introduced. Spatial Prior Distillation then transfers the teacher’s spatial priors by enforcing region, position, and scale consistency, corresponding to target region coverage, center localization, and response scale control. Experiments on multiple IRSTD datasets containing real remote sensing imagery show that SaKD consistently improves the undistilled lightweight student and achieves competitive detection performance with low model complexity. The distilled student contains only 0.895M parameters and 1.999G FLOPs. Deployment verification on a Jetson platform further demonstrates its practical efficiency for edge deployable remote sensing applications, reaching 142.7 FPS for standalone TensorRT FP16 inference at an input resolution of 256 × 256. The code will be available at: https://github.com/Kingwin97/SaKD.
ER  -

