VL  - 242
SP  - 505-521
DO  - 10.1016/j.isprsjprs.2026.09.001
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.isprsjprs.2026.09.001
AB  - With the exponential improvement of earth observation capabilities, the primary challenge in remote sensing has shifted from massive data acquisition to efficient information extraction. Currently, user attention has increasingly turned to monitoring the dynamic changes of objects of interest. Traditional change detection paradigms usually require strict pixel-level registration and radiometric consistency correction for multi-temporal images. However, their high computational cost and sensitivity to registration accuracy make them difficult to meet the demands of time-critical applications. Inspired by the human visual system, which can perceive changes between images without requiring precise pixel alignment or radiometric normalization, namely spot-the-differences, this paper proposes a novel object-level change detection framework that reduces the dependence on strict pixel-level spatial registration. Instead of directly detecting “changes,” which are often difficult to define precisely, the proposed framework identifies and matches unchanged objects across image pairs, thereby indirectly inferring the change set. The core of this framework is a background-aware matching network based on deformable convolution. It incorporates a contrastive learning strategy and introduces a classification-oriented contrastive loss function. This enables the network to model elastic geometric correspondences and robustly associate the same semantic regions directly from misaligned image pairs without performing explicit pixel-level registration during inference. To verify the generality of the proposed method, we constructed a large-scale object-level change detection dataset. The method was integrated with multiple object detection frameworks, including Single-Stage, Two-Stage, Anchor-free, and DETR-based models, to evaluate its robustness and adaptability across different architectures. Experimental results show that, in challenging misaligned scenarios, the proposed method achieves a mean average precision (mAP) of 48.7% on the ATSS framework. This significantly outperforms image-difference-based methods. Moreover, it reduces the false alarm rate from approximately 90.0% in the CYWS method to 12.8%. These results provide a new perspective and exploration for relation learning on misaligned temporal data. The code and dataset for this work will be available on GitHub.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Introducing an automated avalanche debris detection framework from sentinel-2 imagery using multi-index spectral fusion and U-net optimisation
AU  - Sharma A.
JO  - Cold Regions Science and Technology
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-12-01
VL  - 252
DO  - 10.1016/j.coldregions.2026.105096
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.coldregions.2026.105096
AB  - Snow avalanche debris mapping is critical for hazard assessment in high-altitude terrain, yet remains challenging using satellite imagery due to spectral similarity with surrounding snow and terrain-induced illumination effects. This study presents the first framework for automated avalanche debris detection using Sentinel-2 imagery combining multi-index spectral fusion with a stepwise-optimised deep learning model having strong potential for practical application. An 8-channel input is constructed by combining multispectral bands with spectral indices (NDSI, NDWI, NDVI). A U-Net model is then trained using a structured, stepwise hyperparameter optimization strategy to isolate the effects of loss function, optimizer, batch size, and patch size. Class imbalance and heterogeneous scene conditions present significant challenges for avalanche debris segmentation. Tversky loss with asymmetric weighting best handled class imbalance, outperforming binary cross-entropy and Dice loss. AdamW was selected as the optimal optimizer based on both quantitative performance and qualitative robustness, demonstrating fewer false alarms than Nadam. A batch size of 16 and a patch size of 128 × 128 were found to be optimal, demonstrating superior generalization (Validation Dice = 0.658) compared to other configurations. On an independent test set with scenes spanning different dates, the final configuration achieved a mean Dice of 0.681 ± 0.054. Adding spectral indices improved performance over bands-only input across all metrics, Dice rose from 0.640 ± 0.040 to 0.681 ± 0.054, IoU from 0.473 ± 0.038 to 0.518 ± 0.059, precision from 0.653 to 0.707, recall from 0.643 to 0.663, and MCC from 0.637 to 0.676, confirming improved class separability with multi-index fusion. The optimised framework was validated on two temporally independent Sentinel-2 scenes, with cross-sensor validation against coincident PlanetScope imagery confirming spatial accuracy across sub-regions, terrain types, and deposit scales with an object-based overlap comparison yielding Precision = 0.61, Recall = 0.87, and F1 = 0.72. The proposed framework demonstrates consistent performance across tested scenes without scene-specific parameter tuning. The key contribution lies in the systematic combination of open-access Sentinel-2 imagery, multi-index spectral fusion, stepwise optimization, and cross-sensor validation, which together yield a robust, scalable framework for segmenting low-contrast cryospheric features in rugged, difficult terrain where limited field accessibility constrains conventional ground-based assessment.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Global Ecosystem Dynamics Investigation-enabled machine learning for aboveground blue-carbon mapping in the Cua Dai estuary, central Vietnam
AU  - Thu V.T.H.
JO  - Case Studies in Chemical and Environmental Engineering
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-12-01
VL  - 14
DO  - 10.1016/j.cscee.2026.101465
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cscee.2026.101465
AB  - Accurate blue-carbon assessment in fragmented estuarine landscapes remains challenging because multispectral optical data may saturate over dense canopies and plot observations are often sparse. This study developed a Google Earth Engine workflow integrating Global Ecosystem Dynamics Investigation (GEDI) spaceborne Light Detection and Ranging (LiDAR), Sentinel-1 C-band synthetic aperture radar (SAR), Sentinel-2 multispectral imagery, Shuttle Radar Topography Mission (SRTM) topography, principal component analysis (PCA), and machine-learning models to estimate aboveground blue-carbon stocks in the Cua Dai estuary, central Vietnam. After strict quality, sensitivity, and land-water screening, 2781 GEDI top-of-canopy height (TCH) footprints and 6711 GEDI aboveground biomass density (AGBD) footprints were retained as spaceborne reference observations. Random Forest (RF), Gradient Boosted Trees (GBT), Support Vector Machine (SVM), and Classification and Regression Trees (CART) were compared. RF produced the most stable performance, with root mean square error (RMSE) values of 17.32 Mg ha−1 for AGBD and 5.45 m for TCH. The resulting map estimated a mean aboveground carbon (AGC) stock of 13.659 Mg C ha−1 and a total AGC pool of 109,472.12 Mg C across the vegetated landscape. High-carbon hotspots were concentrated in mangrove and riparian alluvial zones in the southwestern sector. Because no local plot-based biomass inventory was available, the outputs should be interpreted as GEDI-calibrated, aboveground carbon estimates rather than fully field-validated total blue-carbon stocks. The workflow provides a rapid and scalable basis for restoration targeting, ecosystem-service valuation, and future measurement, reporting, and verification systems.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - A Hierarchical Context-Fusion and Synergistic Alignment Network for small object detection
AU  - Zhang B.
JO  - ISPRS Journal of Photogrammetry and Remote Sensing
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-12-01
VL  - 242
SP  - 19-35
DO  - 10.1016/j.isprsjprs.2026.08.034
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.isprsjprs.2026.08.034
AB  - Small-object detection in UAV imagery remains challenging because fine-grained target features progressively degrade, complex backgrounds cause substantial interference, and classification confidence is often misaligned with localization quality. To address these challenges, we propose a single-stage detector based on a Hierarchical Context-Fusion Network (HCFN). For feature preservation, Shallow-to-Head Feature Reorganization and Dynamic Soft-Weighted Interpolation (DSWI) retain high-resolution target cues and support stable cross-scale semantic interaction. For feature propagation, Expanded Receptive-Field Lightweight Convolution (ERL-Conv) transfers the preserved fine-grained details to deeper semantic representations while enlarging the receptive field. For feature refinement, the Adaptive Channel-Context Prioritization Module (ACCPM) combines asymmetric channel allocation, large-kernel context modeling, and frequency-domain gating to suppress background interference and enhance object-sensitive responses. Finally, the Synergistic Task-Alignment Head (STAH) improves consistency between classification and localization by combining shared task-interactive features, classification-aware modulation, and deformable regression alignment. Experiments on VisDrone2019, AI-TOD, AI-TODv2, SODA-D, and HIT-UAV demonstrate consistent improvements over the corresponding baselines.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Attribution-driven background modeling network with context-aware anomaly suppression for hyperspectral anomaly detection
AU  - Wang C.
JO  - Information Processing and Management
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-12-01
VL  - 63
IS  - 8
DO  - 10.1016/j.ipm.2026.104950
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ipm.2026.104950
AB  - Hyperspectral anomaly detection (HAD) aims to identify pixels whose spectral signatures deviate from the surrounding background without requiring prior knowledge. Although autoencoder-based methods achieve promising performance, they often suffer from insufficient separation between background and anomalous samples and inadequate modeling of heterogeneous background patterns. Moreover, the strong representation capability of neural networks may cause certain anomalies to be reconstructed, resulting in missed detections. To address these limitations, we propose an attribution-driven background modeling network with context-aware anomaly suppression, termed ADANet. The proposed framework adaptively mines sample attributions, explicitly models diverse background patterns, and suppresses anomaly reconstruction. Specifically, we develop a feature-guided sample attribution mining (FGSAM) strategy that leverages representative background prototypes to infer sample attributions, enabling effective separation of multiple background categories and reliable identification of anomalies. Building on this, a spectral-feature consistent discriminative (SFCD) loss is designed to enforce intra-class compactness and inter-class separability among background features, while maintaining structural consistency between the spectral and feature domains. In addition, we propose a context-aware anomaly suppression (CAAS) module that leverages background features from the spatial neighborhoods of anomalous pixels to constrain anomaly reconstruction, driving reconstructed anomalies toward the background distribution and thereby reducing missed detections. Quantitative comparisons with state-of-the-art methods show that our approach achieves consistently high (Formula presented) values across six hyperspectral image datasets, with the highest value reaching 99.95%, and outperforms the second-best method by up to 0.70%. Qualitative comparisons further support the superiority of the proposed method.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Synoptic-aware explainable artificial intelligence (SA-XAI) model for bias correction and regionalization of satellite precipitation over complex terrain
AU  - Tosan M.
JO  - Atmospheric Research
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-12-01
VL  - 342
DO  - 10.1016/j.atmosres.2026.109213
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.atmosres.2026.109213
AB  - A critical generalization challenge limits the utility of conventional machine learning (ML) models for correcting satellite precipitation products (SPPs), particularly over complex terrain. Models relying exclusively on static geographic predictors often suffer from spatial overfitting, achieving deceptively high skill under standard temporal validation while failing to generalize to ungauged locations. To address this limitation, a synoptic-aware explainable artificial intelligence (SA-XAI) framework was developed, integrating dynamic atmospheric drivers from the ERA5 reanalysis with multiple satellite inputs to enforce physically consistent learning. Evaluated over a 20-year period (2004–2023) across the topographically complex central Iran, a rigorous spatial cross-validation (Spatial CV) exposed the illusory skill of static models. An ablation study identified the 200 hPa geopotential height (Z200) and 850 hPa meridional moisture flux (vq850) as highly effective and parsimonious physical drivers for this regional circulation setting. To capture the magnitude of high-impact events more effectively, the architecture was further upgraded to a two-stage Hurdle model enriched with deep thermodynamic constraints (e.g., convective available potential energy and total column water vapour). The framework demonstrated substantial operational value during hydrologically significant wet events (>5.0 mm day−1). Compared to a traditional quantile mapping (QM) baseline, the SA-XAI model improved the probability of detection (POD) by 41.4%. Furthermore, Shapley Additive exPlanations (SHAP) was deployed as a rigorous internal diagnostic audit. The XAI analysis revealed an emergent synoptic importance shift, confirming that the model autonomously learns to dynamically prioritize large-scale synoptic forcing over error-prone satellite inputs during high-impact regional events. This framework bridges black-box ML and meteorological science, delivering the regionally transferable and reliable corrections essential for operational monitoring in data-scarce environments.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - HELIOS-Net: Hourly daytime estimation of land surface temperature with integration of observational solar-cloud-satellite geometry and deep neural network
AU  - Kim Y.
JO  - ISPRS Journal of Photogrammetry and Remote Sensing
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-12-01
VL  - 242
SP  - 760-777
DO  - 10.1016/j.isprsjprs.2026.09.028
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.isprsjprs.2026.09.028
AB  - Geostationary satellite-based land surface temperature (LST) is vital for monitoring diurnal thermal dynamics owing to its extensive spatial coverage and high temporal resolution. However, frequent cloud cover results in substantial data gaps, limiting its practical utility for reliable thermal monitoring applications. The solar-cloud-satellite geometry (SCSG) effect has recently been introduced to reflect direct cloud radiative effects in LST reconstruction, but its application has been largely limited to polar-orbiting satellites. To address this limitation, we propose HELIOS-Net, a deep learning (DL) framework for hourly daytime LST reconstruction using the GEO-KOMPSAT-2A satellite across South Korea, central Japan, and southeastern Australia. The framework combines SCSG-based regime partitioning with a generative adversarial network (GAN) to reconstruct spatially continuous all-sky LST by leveraging satellite observations and various meteorological and topographic variables. Validation against in-situ observations demonstrated that HELIOS-Net achieved high performance across all regions, with coefficient of determination (R2) values of 0.63–0.94 and root mean square error (RMSE) values of 2.45–7.65 °C under cloudy-sky conditions. Comparative evaluation further indicated an overall performance advantage over ERA5-Land and an existing all-sky LST product derived from satellite observations. Additional analyses confirmed that both the SCSG effect and the DL framework contribute to enhancing the fidelity of hourly daytime LST reconstruction. These results highlight the potential of the proposed framework to enable high-quality, spatially continuous all-sky LST monitoring from geostationary satellites, providing a promising approach mitigating cloud-induced data gaps under diverse viewing geometries and environmental conditions.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - A novel change homogenizing flood index and an expedited locally tuned Markov random field model for unsupervised rural floodwater detection in sentinel-1 data: An application to flood impact assessment
AU  - Mohsenifar A.
JO  - ISPRS Journal of Photogrammetry and Remote Sensing
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-12-01
VL  - 242
SP  - 455-489
DO  - 10.1016/j.isprsjprs.2026.08.021
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.isprsjprs.2026.08.021
AB  - Due to their all-weather, all-day data ingestion capability, interest in synthetic aperture radar (SAR) remote sensing satellites is growing for rural flood mapping in contemporary times. Current SAR flood detection change indices cannot correct for dependency on reference backscatter values when computing flood changes. To address this fundamental limitation, the present study proposes an unsupervised rural flood detection method that produces flood extent maps in two main steps. In the first step, a novel change homogenizing flood index (CHFI) was proposed to reduce the heterogeneity of flood-change patterns by highly mitigating the aforesaid dependency. In the second step, an expedited locally tuned Markov Random Field (ELTMRF) model was developed to partition the CHFI into flood and non-flood classes within a reasonable time while maintaining boundary accuracy. Finally, to evaluate flood impacts on different LULC classes, a land use/land cover (LULC) map for the flood-affected region was first generated using the random forest classifier and then intersected with the ELTMRF-based flood extent map. In this research, Sentinel-1 and −2 data for eight flood events were gathered to conduct experiments in two stages: (1) the evaluation of CHFI against existing change indices (CIs) and (2) the comparison of ELTMRF to state-of-the-art (SOTA) unsupervised flood detection approaches. According to the experimental results, the proposed CHFI enhanced the homogeneity of the flood-related changes while establishing a satisfactory contrast between non-flood and flood classes. Thereby, by yielding an average F-score value of around 88%, the proposed CI exhibited the most generalizable, superior performance compared to the conventional CIs. The ELTMRF model, preserving the boundaries of flood objects, also improved the prior MRF-based models by approximately 5.69% and 41.6% in accuracy and runtime, respectively. Furthermore, the proposed approach, which combines CHFI and ELTMRF, reached an average IoU of 84.98%, demonstrating a remarkable superiority of nearly 12% and 9% over unsupervised and supervised flood mapping methods, respectively. Regarding the impacts on the land use classes, the analyses revealed that farmlands and roads were the two classes most extensively affected by flooding in all cases. Accordingly, the proposed unsupervised rural flood detection approach, which combines CHFI and ELTMRF, provides an efficient framework for detecting flooded areas in bi-temporal SAR data, achieving high accuracy while requiring reasonable computational time. The datasets and the module of the proposed flood mapping method will be available on https://github.com/AminMohsenifar/CHFI-ELTMRF.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Enhancing identification of geothermal prospection in Yingshan geothermal field using machine learning and thermal infrared remote sensing
AU  - Zhang X.
JO  - Geothermics
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-12-01
VL  - 142
DO  - 10.1016/j.geothermics.2026.103818
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.geothermics.2026.103818
AB  - Improving the accuracy of land surface temperature (LST) retrieval from thermal infrared (TIR) remote sensing and inverting the properties of subsurface geothermal reservoirs remain significant challenges. This has particularly constrained the integration of TIR remote sensing with other exploration methodologies, such as geochemical surveys. To address this issue, we derived thermal infrared information from Landsat 8 and MODIS data, with Sentinel-2 and SRTM data providing auxiliary optical and topographic features in the Yingshan geothermal field. Subsequently, we adopted the eXtreme Gradient Boosting (XGBoost) machine learning model to perform spatial downscaling of LST. By establishing the optimal feature combination for temperature retrieval, we achieved high-precision surface temperature inversion and compared its performance with the traditional bilinear interpolation and DisTrad models. In parallel, we developed a processing workflow for altered mineral identification based on ASTER and Landsat 8 reflectance data. Finally, we conducted a comprehensive analysis integrating the downscaled LST, thermally altered minerals, and in-situ geochemical data including soil gas Rn, Hg, and geothermal gradient (GTG), to improve the accuracy of geothermal prospect targeting. The results show that the XGBoost model significantly enhanced the detail richness of the LST map, increasing the entropy value from 3.13 to 3.30, which outperforms the results of bilinear interpolation (3.12) and the DisTrad model (3.14). Validation against in-situ surface temperature measurements shows that the XGBoost model achieved a mean bias error (MBE) of -0.492 ± 0.2 °C and a root mean square error (RMSE) of 1.39 ± 0.0702 °C, which are significantly better than the results of the DisTrad model (MBE = 0.539 ± 0.2 °C; RMSE = 2.77 ± 0.0387 °C) and bilinear interpolation (MBE = 0.625 ± 0.2 °C; RMSE = 2.98 ± 0.0418 °C). Furthermore, the P-P plot analysis indicates that the XGBoost model significantly improved the identification accuracy for both the relatively high-temperature geothermal fields and the low-temperature vegetation-covered areas. Our TIR remote sensing analysis identified four types of hydrothermal alteration: propylitic, argillization, biotitization, and sericitization, all of which were validated by field investigations. In the focal research area, we identified five high-temperature anomalies, along with their corresponding thermally altered mineral anomalies. The results indicate that the geothermal reservoirs in the anomaly zones are dominated by medium-acidic and medium-low temperature environments. Moreover, through comprehensive integration with soil gas Rn, Hg, and geothermal gradient (GTG), we classified the thermal anomalies into three distinct types with complete quantitative signatures: (1) Type 1 (geothermal fields): showed LST anomalies of 23.3 ± 1.12–24.6 ± 1.22 °C, with the presence of surface hydrothermal alteration minerals, paired with elevated Hg and GTG; (2) Type 2 (hidden faults): had normal LST, with no detectable hydrothermal alteration minerals, but pronounced Rn anomalies; (3) Type 3 (heat island/conductive zones): exhibited extreme LST fluctuations, with no detectable hydrothermal alteration minerals, paired with elevated GTGs but weak geochemical anomalies. This classification result suggests that downscaled LST, when integrated with soil gas Rn, Hg, geothermal gradient, and structural information, can assist in distinguishing known geothermal fields from potential concealed structural or conductive zones. The XGBoost-based downscaling method, combined with hydrothermal altered mineral identification, can significantly improve the accuracy of geothermal exploration targeting, analyze the genesis of geothermal distribution at the initial exploration stage, and substantially reduce the cost of geothermal system exploration.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Integrated multisource remote sensing and geochemical constraints on the petrogenesis and geodynamic evolution of mineralized A-type granites in the Egyptian Nubian Shield
AU  - Abo Khashaba S.M.
JO  - Journal of Geochemical Exploration
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-12-01
VL  - 291
DO  - 10.1016/j.gexplo.2026.108196
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.gexplo.2026.108196
AB  - The Umm Hibal area, located in the westernmost part of the South Eastern Desert of Egypt, comprises quartz syenites and peralkaline granites that host abundant accessory and ore minerals, including Fe-Ti oxides, zircon, apatite, rutile, and allanite. In this study, for the first time, lithological discrimination was achieved using Support Vector Machine and Random Forest algorithms. Among the tested models, the SVM classifier applied to the combined Sentinel-1 and Sentinel-2 datasets achieved the highest performance, with an overall classification accuracy of 98.13%, a Kappa coefficient of 0.972, and an F1-score of 0.972. These results reflect the effectiveness of integrated radar-optical data for discriminating lithological variability in mineralized peralkaline granitoid suites. Spectral analysis of PRISMA imagery identified characteristic absorption features associated with various alteration types, including phyllic, argillic, hydroxyl-bearing, ferrous-silicate, and ferrugination assemblages. Field and laboratory validation confirmed the spatial distribution and intensity of these alterations, indicating that hydrothermal overprinting had occurred in the primary magmatic rocks. Sentinel-1 SAR revealed structural patterns in NW-SE, NE-SW, and N-S orientations, and lineament density mapping further identified critical fluid pathways that control mineralization. Geochemically, the quartz syenites display remarkably homogeneous chemical compositions, characterized by relatively low silica contents (SiO2 = 64.3–67.2 wt%), Rb (48.4–69.1 ppm), moderate K2O/Na2O ratios (0.75–1.02), and high FeOt (3.45–5.81 wt%) and Al₂O3 (14.6 to 16.9 wt%), compared with peralkaline granites, which show variable silica (SiO2 = 68–78.8 wt%), FeOt (0.88–7.87 wt%), Rb (52.1–129 ppm), and Ba (187–1611 ppm). Both granitic types are enriched in rare metals, including Zr (up to 1159 ppm), Nb (183 ppm), Y (190 ppm), and REEs (695 ppm), like mineralized peralkaline A-type granites in the ANS. They exhibit characteristics of anorogenic within-plate A1-type granites that crystallized via fractional crystallization of OIB-like mafic magma. A fuzzy-logic mineral prospectivity model integrating lithological, structural, and different alteration zones successfully delineates high-priority rare-metal targets in the Umm Hibal area. The integrated approach establishes a two-stage metallogenic model in which primary HFSE-REE enrichment during prolonged fractional crystallization of OIB-like peralkaline melts was subsequently upgraded by late-stage potassic metasomatism, silicification, and structurally focused hydrothermal fluid circulation. This framework provides a directly transferable predictive model for rare-metal exploration in analogous peralkaline ring-complex systems throughout the ANS and comparable Phanerozoic intraplate alkaline provinces globally.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Four decades of Landsat-based river turbidity reconstruction reveal spatially heterogeneous declines across U.S. rivers
AU  - Li Y.
JO  - ISPRS Journal of Photogrammetry and Remote Sensing
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-12-01
VL  - 242
SP  - 215-234
DO  - 10.1016/j.isprsjprs.2026.09.007
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.isprsjprs.2026.09.007
AB  - Turbidity, a fundamental water quality parameter, profoundly influences aquatic ecosystems by regulating light penetration, primary productivity, and the fate of nutrients and pollutants. Despite prior large-scale optical water-quality studies of U.S. rivers, systematic, multi-decadal analyses integrating Landsat surface reflectance, machine-learning retrieval, and ecoregion-scale driver attribution have remained limited. This study developed and validated a long-term Landsat-based turbidity reconstruction framework for major rivers of the conterminous United States from 1984 to 2025. By integrating harmonized Landsat 5/7/8/9 surface reflectance (Collection 2 Tier 1) with river geometry from the Surface Water and Ocean Topography Mission River Database (SWORD), named after the SWOT satellite mission, and in-situ turbidity measurements from the USGS, we established a robust Random Forest (RF) regression model (validation R2 = 0.95, RMSE = 21.25 NTU) for turbidity estimation, outperforming three other machine-learning algorithms. The RF model was further evaluated using strict spatiotemporal transferability tests, including chronological hold-out, shared-site, new-site, and leave-one-region-out (LORO) spatial cross-validation. Model interpretability was rigorously validated using SHAP (SHapley Additive exPlanations) analysis, confirming that the model learned physically meaningful relationships. Our study revealed distinct spatial patterns in river turbidity, with generally higher values in the Southern Plains (SPL) and Temperate Plains (TPL) ecoregions and lower values in the Western Mountains (WMT) and Xeric (XER) regions. Moreover, we detected a heterogeneous but overall declining trend in turbidity over time, particularly in historically high-turbidity areas. Model diagnostics indicate that predictive performance is higher in mid- to high-turbidity waters, and the reconstructed dataset should be interpreted primarily at regional and long-term scales. To explore the drivers of these changes, we integrated climatic, land cover, and dam data into the analysis. The results show that anthropogenic factors, including dams (∼33% contribution), forest cover (∼27%, reflecting the absence of disturbance), and urban/built-up areas (∼17%), are the primary contributors to turbidity variance at the national scale, far outweighing the influence of climatic variables (each generally < 5%). Further investigation of ten representative dammed rivers indicated that dam construction is associated with significant downstream reductions in turbidity, with the effect typically stronger for taller dams but modulated by local topography. As an exploratory application, framed as an illustrative case study rather than a primary finding, we further developed a turbidity-linked hydro-morphodynamic sensitivity indicator that combines turbidity decline and velocity-related channel proxies. This indicator is intended to provide a preliminary reference for identifying reaches where sediment reduction may coincide with enhanced hydraulic forcing; independent geomorphic validation is required before any hazard-related interpretations can be drawn.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Identifying diverse iron ores based on feature enhancement and deep learning classification with hyperspectral images
AU  - Xu Z.
JO  - Measurement Journal of the International Measurement Confederation
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-11-15
VL  - 290
DO  - 10.1016/j.measurement.2026.122716
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.measurement.2026.122716
AB  - Accurate determination of iron-ore grades enables optimized post-processing workflows, reducing energy consumption and minimizing pollution emissions. However, when identifying ore grades by relying on a limited number of features in hyperspectral images (HSI), existing methods suffered from poor separability between certain categories and did not account for adaptability across different mining areas. To better determine the grades of iron ores from HSI, this paper proposed a method to enhance grade separability of ores collected from different mines. First, spectral bands sensitive to ore grades were extracted through feature selection method of SPA, thereby reducing redundant information. The Gramian Angular Difference Field (GADF) transformation was then employed to convert the one-dimensional spectral data into two-dimensional image representations, leveraging more stable relationships between the different bands of the iron ores. To further capture spatial structures contained within the GADF features, a deep learning network structure was designed to classify and identify ore grades, which incorporates multi-scale convolution kernels and a feature layer weighting strategy. The experiment results indicated that the GADF transform with 15 representative bands improved the grade separability to 1.81 and deep learning further improved classification accuracy exceeding 2.7%. Classification accuracy of validation across four mines of Tangshan, Shougang, Handan and Yantai reached 95.88%, 84.65%, 85.18% and 92.12%, respectively. The proposed data processing framework demonstrated effective performance across a diverse range of ore samples, indicating its practical application potential in identification and production processes.
