TI  - Weakly supervised salient object detection in optical remote sensing images based on multi-directional nesting and edge scan fusion
AU  - Sun M.
JO  - Neurocomputing
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-11-07
VL  - 701
DO  - 10.1016/j.neucom.2026.134537
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.neucom.2026.134537
AB  - Recently, although fully-supervised salient object detection in optical remote sensing images (ORSI-SOD) has achieved remarkable performance improvements, the pixel-level annotations required by such supervision paradigm are extremely labor-intensive and resource-consuming. To alleviate this issue, weakly-supervised ORSI-SOD methods, especially those based on scribble annotations, have made considerable progress. Unfortunately, prevailing weakly-supervised ORSI-SOD methods lack explicit modeling of multi-directional features for salient objects, and neglect the effective utilization of edge information, particularly the cross-scale exchange of edge knowledge. To overcome these limitations, we present MNEFNet, a weakly-supervised salient object detection network based on multi-directional nesting and edge scan fusion. The model integrates two key modules: the Multi-directional Nested Mamba Module (MNMM) and the Cross-scale Edge Scanning Fusion Module (CESFM). MNMM adopts a bi-level nested architecture inspired by Vision Mamba, consisting of weight simulation generation and state equivalent update in the outer path, as well as explicit directional encoding and internal Mamba units in the inner path. It enables the network to estimate saliency by capturing and reasoning directional characteristics of objects without pixel-level supervision. As for the CESFM, its core objective is to enhance the localization of salient objects and refine their boundaries. This module mainly contains an edge information cache and a cross-scale Mamba fusion component. Through the joint operation of these two parts, the hidden edge cues at multiple levels and scales can be stored, mined, aggregated and utilized efficiently and accurately. Extensive experiments on benchmark datasets demonstrate that the proposed MNEFNet achieves state-of-the-art SOD performance.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Towards efficient multi-hazard detection from single satellite imagery via spectral-semantic graph reasoning
AU  - Ao W.
JO  - Remote Sensing of Environment
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-11-01
VL  - 345
DO  - 10.1016/j.rse.2026.115566
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.rse.2026.115566
AB  - Frequent natural and anthropogenic disasters occurring on Earth pose significant threats to human life and property. Timely and accurate responses to these disasters are crucial for mitigating the damage they cause. However, existing disaster detection methods are typically designed for a single category and rely on multi-temporal imagery, making them inadequate for real-time, multi-class disaster detection in on-orbit scenarios. In this study, we design a multi-hazard detection framework based on a single satellite image, aiming to ensure both high detection accuracy across multiple disaster categories and computational efficiency. Specifically, we investigate the advantages and disadvantages of traditional threshold methods primarily based on handcrafted spectral features and the currently popular deep learning approaches for disaster recognition tasks. Based on this, we propose a novel disaster detection framework called SSF-GNN. This method combines the strengths of both approaches by integrating predefined spectral features with semantic features extracted by deep learning models, and then using a graph neural network to reason over these features to determine whether a disaster has occurred in the remote sensing imagery. Moreover, due to the current lack of multi-hazard detection datasets based on multispectral imagery, we have constructed a new large-scale dataset containing six types of disasters, named S2MHD. Our method achieves an overall accuracy of 88.37% on this dataset, significantly outperforming existing state-of-the-art methods.
