PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-10-15
VL  - 78
IS  - 8
SP  - 8141-8158
DO  - 10.1016/j.asr.2026.08.035
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.asr.2026.08.035
AB  - Current Satellite Laser Ranging (SLR) data preprocessing mostly relies on manual screen processing, which exhibits significant efficiency bottlenecks under large data throughputs and yields subjective inconsistencies. To address these challenges, this paper proposes an automatic SLR data processing method based on a dual-layer Particle Swarm Optimization (PSO) and Back Propagation (BP) neural network. This method effectively captures the local density fluctuations and covariance geometric features of SLR data. The outer-layer PSO adaptively searches for the optimal network structure, while the inner-layer PSO automatically optimizes the weights and biases. This dual-layer architecture mitigates the risk of the traditional BP algorithm getting trapped in local optima. Experimental results demonstrate that on simulated satellite data across different orbits, the method achieves a recognition rate exceeding 99.89% and a misdetection rate below 11.11% (remaining under 1.84% under standard low-noise conditions), outperforming both the hand-crafted Vanilla Neural Network (Vnn) and the classical Graz algorithm. For actual observation data, the internal precision of normal points is improved by up to 37.35% and 18.28% compared to ILRS official website reference data and the Graz algorithm, respectively. Additionally, this method supports batch processing with an average efficiency of approximately 25 to 32 s per file. Ultimately, it provides an efficient near-real-time automated preprocessing solution in the standard normal point generation pipeline for future unmanned SLR systems.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Key challenges and a roadmap for earthquake forecasting using satellite remote sensing data
AU  - Akhoondzadeh M.
JO  - Advances in Space Research
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-10-15
VL  - 78
IS  - 8
SP  - 8328-8335
DO  - 10.1016/j.asr.2026.08.054
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.asr.2026.08.054
AB  - By reviewing the studies that have been conducted so far in the field of earthquake prediction using satellite data, it can be said that all phases related to the creation of an earthquake warning system have been almost completed. But why has this set of activities not yet led to earthquake prediction with acceptable uncertainty? The role of satellite remote sensing data in the development of earthquake prediction studies is undeniable. Although the improvement in spatial, temporal, spectral, and radiometric resolution and the diversity of satellite sensors have been significant, it does not seem sufficient for earthquake prediction, and the question that arises is whether the process of improving these satellite parameters can lead to earthquake prediction in the future? Regarding the detection of clear and sometimes hidden seismic anomalies in satellite data, existing classical and intelligent algorithms based on machine learning have been effective. But will it be possible to detect anomalies with unknown patterns related to seismic activity in the future by providing appropriate training data and resolving ambiguities related to the mechanisms of occurrence of seismic activity? Will deep learning-based intelligent algorithms be able to estimate earthquake parameters (time, location, and magnitude) with very low uncertainty in the future? In the case of integrating earthquake precursors, or in other words, earthquake parameters estimated by different precursors, existing simple and intelligent fusion algorithms are persuasive. Therefore, it seems that the biggest ambiguity and challenge that has been influential in the implementation process of the proposed earthquake warning systems is the lack of a precise definition of the LAIC (Lithospheric Atmospheric Ionospheric Coupling) mechanism. But the question arises whether effective and accurate earthquake warning systems will be provided in the future by resolving these existing ambiguities? This article reviews associated studies and challenges regarding the use of remote sensing data in earthquake prediction, and proposes a roadmap for the future of these studies.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - A novel framework for understanding the Seismo-LAIC mechanism using the Sequential Mann–Kendall anomaly detection method applied to satellite precursors
AU  - Akhoondzadeh M.
JO  - Advances in Space Research
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-10-15
VL  - 78
IS  - 8
SP  - 8239-8256
DO  - 10.1016/j.asr.2026.08.019
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.asr.2026.08.019
AB  - Most of the classical and intelligent methods developed for anomaly detection in earthquake precursor time series try to find points with abnormal sudden changes in the studied time interval. According to different unproven hypotheses that have been proposed for the LAIC (Lithospheric Atmospheric Ionospheric Coupling) mechanism, there is a time lag between the observed anomalies in different layers of the lithosphere, atmosphere, and ionosphere. Therefore, discovering the starting points of abnormal trends in the time series of earthquake precursors can be very effective in better understanding the LAIC mechanism. The Sequential Mann–Kendall (SMK) test is a widely used non-parametric approach for detecting structural changes, trend shifts, and regime transitions in time series. Unlike classical anomaly detection methods that focus on point anomalies, the SMK test identifies anomalies caused by changes in the underlying trend structure. In this study, using the SMK method and analyzing time series of different earthquake precursors obtained from satellite data, including electron density obtained from measurements of the Swarm (Alpha, Bravo, Charlie) and CSES-01 satellites, GPS-TEC (Total Electron Content), and products available on the GEE (Google Earth Engine) platform, including surface temperature, water vapor, and ozone, in a time periods of 170 (Swarm and GEE data), 102 (CSES-01 data) and 109 (GPS-TEC data) days, around the time and location of Turkey (February 06, 2023) earthquake, an attempt has been made to detect the onset time of possible LAI anomalies. The results emphasize the use of classical and even intelligent methods effective in detecting the exact time at which a structural change occurs in earthquake precursor time series for a better understanding of the LAIC mechanism.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - PVP-adapter: Dual-branch spatial-frequency adaptation for change detection of photovoltaic panels
AU  - Huang R.
JO  - ISPRS Journal of Photogrammetry and Remote Sensing
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-10-01
VL  - 240
SP  - 360-377
DO  - 10.1016/j.isprsjprs.2026.07.032
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.isprsjprs.2026.07.032
AB  - Solar photovoltaic (PV) power generation is a core clean energy source for addressing the energy crisis and climate change. Accurate monitoring of large-scale solar PV deployments is crucial. Existing work often reports on PV installation monitoring or growth assessments in developed regions, overlooking underdeveloped areas. This study leverages high-resolution bi-temporal satellite imagery to construct the first publicly available dataset for PV installation change detection, PVPanel-CD. This dataset contains bi-temporal remote sensing images covering representative African landscapes, along with pixel-level change annotations. We propose PVP-Adapter, a dual-branch spatial-frequency adaptive method for PV panel change detection, to achieve accurate monitoring of solar PV deployments across Africa. Specifically, we introduce a parameter-efficient adaptation scheme that fine-tunes frozen features from Vision Foundation Models (VFMs), facilitating knowledge transfer of parametric features and reducing dependence on a large number of training samples. Furthermore, we decouple the adaptive spatial features into low-frequency and high-frequency branches to perform differential change detection separately. To explore the transferability of the proposed PVP-Adapter, we also release six geographically diverse test domain datasets. We found that: (1) PVP-Adapter shows significant advantages in complex interference environments, surpassing existing change detection methods with an overall accuracy of 97.91%. (2) We use PVP-Adapter to calculate the growth of PV panel installations in Africa by 25.98 square kilometers from 2006 to 2024, and then discuss changes in environmental elements and the potential of solar energy in Africa. Our work makes it possible to automatically monitor changes in PV panel installations and human environment using bi-temporal RS images. The source code of the project is available at https://github.com/DonMuv/PVP-Adapter.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Edge-preserving and uncertainty-guided domain adaptation for weakly supervised rooftop photovoltaic mapping
AU  - Wang S.
JO  - ISPRS Journal of Photogrammetry and Remote Sensing
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-10-01
VL  - 240
SP  - 31-53
DO  - 10.1016/j.isprsjprs.2026.07.008
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.isprsjprs.2026.07.008
AB  - Accurate, large-scale mapping of rooftop photovoltaic (PV) systems from high-resolution remote sensing imagery is critical for urban energy planning and grid management. However, existing deep learning methods suffer from high annotation costs associated with pixel-level labeling and significant performance degradation due to cross-domain shifts in geography and sensors. To address these challenges, this paper presents an integrated edge-preserving and uncertainty-guided domain adaptation framework (EPUDA) for weakly supervised rooftop PV mapping. By transferring knowledge from densely annotated source domains to target domains with only sparse point annotations, EPUDA achieves low-cost cross-domain segmentation. First, a semantic-structural dual-path decoding network is employed to model regional semantics and edge structures separately. Subsequently, an edge-preserving adversarial adaptation mechanism aligns features by dynamically focusing on structurally informative boundaries and hard regions with inconsistent semantic predictions, thereby enhancing boundary stability across domains. Furthermore, to tackle the sparsity of point supervision, an uncertainty-guided pseudo-label consistency learning module is incorporated to generate more reliable pseudo-labels via probabilistic consistency evaluation and confidence-aware selection. To support this study, we construct the China Rooftop Photovoltaic Segmentation Dataset (CRPVS) for weakly supervised learning, covering 49 major cities. Experiments across four cross-domain scenarios demonstrate that EPUDA outperforms 15 representative comparative methods, improving the Intersection over Union (IoU) by 1.75%–2.65% over the strongest competing method in each scenario. This study offers a practical, low-cost solution for large-scale rooftop PV mapping.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - MapGlue: Multimodal remote sensing image matching
AU  - Wu P.
JO  - ISPRS Journal of Photogrammetry and Remote Sensing
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-10-01
VL  - 240
SP  - 166-183
DO  - 10.1016/j.isprsjprs.2026.07.007
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.isprsjprs.2026.07.007
AB  - Multimodal remote sensing image (MRSI) matching is pivotal for cross-modal fusion, localization, and object detection, but it faces severe challenges due to geometric, radiometric, and viewpoint discrepancies across imaging modalities. Existing unimodal datasets lack scale and diversity, limiting deep learning solutions. This paper proposes MapGlue, a universal MRSI matching framework, and MapData, a large-scale multimodal dataset addressing these gaps. Our contributions are threefold. First, MapData, a globally diverse dataset spanning 233 sampling points, offers original images (7000 × 5,000 to 20,000 × 15,000 pixels). After rigorous cleaning, it provides 121,781 aligned electronic map–visible image pairs (512 × 512 pixels) with hybrid manual-automated ground truth, addressing the scarcity of scalable multimodal benchmarks. Second, the proposed MapGlue method constructs a saliency feature distribution map for keypoint extraction and integrates semantic information to accurately capture common invariant features. Furthermore, it utilizes a dual graph-guided mechanism to enable efficient global-to-local information interaction and robust feature enhancement for multimodal descriptors. Finally, extensive evaluations on MapData and five public datasets demonstrate MapGlue’s superiority in matching accuracy under complex conditions, outperforming state-of-the-art methods. Notably, MapGlue generalizes effectively to unseen modalities without retraining, highlighting its adaptability. This work addresses longstanding challenges in MRSI matching by combining scalable dataset construction with a robust, semantics-driven framework. The dataset and demo code are available at https://github.com/PeihaoWu/MapGlue.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - ASG-TFANet: Adaptive skip gate enhanced temporal-frequency aggregation network for multi-scale remote sensing image segmentation
AU  - Wang L.
JO  - Signal Processing Image Communication
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-10-01
VL  - 148
DO  - 10.1016/j.image.2026.117644
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.image.2026.117644
AB  - Semantic segmentation of remote sensing images is a core task in earth observation, yet existing convolutional neural networks based methods suffer from inefficient feature extraction via skip connections and the loss of small-target features during downsampling. This paper proposes ASG-TFANet, a hybrid network integrated with the IFDTM and HCASG modules. The IFDTM fuses Fourier and wavelet transforms to realize cross-domain interaction between global frequency-domain and local spatial-domain features, suppressing the loss of small-target features and enhancing high-frequency details. The HCASG replaces traditional skip connections and fuses multiple types of convolutions to mine valid multi-scale features and eliminate redundant information. A combined loss function integrating Dice loss, cross-entropy loss, and auxiliary cross-entropy loss is also designed to optimize inter-class discrimination. Experiments on the LoveDA and Vaihingen datasets show that the method achieves mIoU/OA of 80.20%/86.30% and 85.94%/93.95%. Visualization and ablation experiments verify that ASG-TFANet performs excellently in boundary localization, small-target segmentation, and inter-class discrimination in complex remote sensing scenes, and its modules effectively strengthen the capabilities of global-local feature interaction and multi-scale feature mining. It provides an effective solution for fine-grained remote sensing image segmentation and has broad application prospects in fields such as land use monitoring.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Morphological edge detection steganography for quantum remote sensing images based on quantum walks and truncated octahedron in unit cube
AU  - Xu T.t.
JO  - Journal of the Franklin Institute
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-10-01
VL  - 363
IS  - 15
DO  - 10.1016/j.jfranklin.2026.109024
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jfranklin.2026.109024
AB  - This paper proposes a novel steganographic scheme for quantum remote sensing images. First, to enhance the invisibility and robustness of the secret information, an image encryption approach is developed by integrating quantum walks (QWs), a chaotic system, and the particle swarm optimization (PSO) algorithm. Subsequently, morphological edge detection is employed to extract and label comprehensive edge points within the quantum remote sensing images. Guided by this detection, a data hiding method based on a truncated octahedron in a unit cube (TO-UC) is designed for non-edge regions. This approach enables the highly effective concealment of the secret image while concurrently improving visual quality and security. Furthermore, an efficient and secure quantum steganographic circuit is constructed to facilitate its implementation. Experimental results demonstrate that the proposed scheme achieves a PSNR of up to 56.7797 dB and a SSIM index approaching 1. The secret image can be perfectly recovered in the absence of attacks; moreover, even under various levels of noise and clipping attacks, the NC and BER remain highly satisfactory. These results confirm the strong invisibility, high fidelity, and robust security of the proposed steganographic scheme.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - A systematic approach to assess fused drone-satellite data for vertical water quality profile estimation in a deep reservoir
AU  - Mishmast Nehi S.
JO  - Journal of Hydrology
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-10-01
VL  - 679
DO  - 10.1016/j.jhydrol.2026.136131
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jhydrol.2026.136131
AB  - In-situ sampling for deep-reservoir water quality monitoring is accurate but often impractical for routine, high-resolution vertical monitoring. Remote sensing (RS) provides an alternative for estimating multiple water quality variables (WQVs), including both optically active and non-optically active variables. However, most existing studies have focused on surface-level estimations, which are inadequate for deep reservoirs where WQVs’ profiles vary significantly with depth. While previous studies have compared remote sensing data sources for estimating water quality, this work addresses a critical gap by providing one of the first systematic evaluations of different data sources specifically for assessing WQVs at lower levels in deep water bodies. To achieve this, we introduce a novel multi-platform systematic framework that evaluates multiple RS data sources (including Sentinel-2 and Landsat 8/9 satellite imagery as well as unmanned aerial vehicle (UAV) observations), alongside fused datasets and machine learning models to estimate vertical profiles of WQVs. The framework was applied to the Wadi Dayqah Dam (Oman), where seven WQVs (temperature, chlorophyll-a, turbidity, EC25, pH, oxidation–reduction potential, and dissolved oxygen) were modeled across depth zones using coincident in-situ measurements for training and validation. Results show that RS-based models can reproduce vertical WQV patterns beyond the surface layer, and that multi-source fusion improves estimation for several non-optically active variables compared with single-source models. Key contributions are: (1) a reproducible evaluation process for selecting RS sources and models for vertical profiling, (2) evidence that multi-source RS improves estimation of depth-varying WQVs in a deep reservoir, and (3) a demonstration on a large reservoir that the approach can extend operational RS monitoring below the surface. This framework can guide future vertical water-quality monitoring and multi-source RS integration.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Multi-modal dual-target spatio-temporal graph learning for regional evapotranspiration estimation
AU  - Gyamfi K.A.
JO  - Journal of Hydrology
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-10-01
VL  - 678
DO  - 10.1016/j.jhydrol.2026.136014
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jhydrol.2026.136014
AB  - Accurate evapotranspiration (ET) estimation remains challenging because physically based formulas rely on simplifying assumptions, whereas satellite products contain retrieval uncertainty. We develop a multi-modal dual-encoder, dual-target spatio-temporal graph neural network for regional ET estimation over the Korean Peninsula. The framework treats meteorological forcing and MODIS-derived surface descriptors as heterogeneous but complementary modes. A hybrid graph represents geographic-distance and inter-station-correlation structure, modality-specific graph encoders and a recurrent temporal encoder capture spatio-temporal dependence, and a learnable gate fuses climate and satellite representations while quantifying their relative contributions. The model is trained jointly against MODIS ET and FAO-56 Penman–Monteith reference ET (PM ET0), combining satellite-observed actual ET with meteorological evaporative demand. On the holdout test set, the model achieved MAE/RMSE/R2 of 2.197/3.503/0.883 for MODIS ET and 0.473/0.615/0.999 for PM ET0. Relative to a graph-free CNN + LSTM dual-head baseline, MAE decreased by 33.3% for MODIS ET and 39.0% for PM ET0. Component ablation showed that the full multi-modal dual-target configuration reduced joint MAE by 40.0% and RMSE by 38.6% relative to the single-encoder MODIS variant. Gate analysis showed that climate and satellite contributions vary by season and input position, making the fusion interpretable rather than fixed. MC-dropout uncertainty with isotonic calibration achieved PICP90 of 0.895 and 0.899 for MODIS ET and PM ET0, respectively. Multi-horizon tests retained seasonal predictability up to 12 months.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Aerial Image Gravity Direction Estimation (AGE): Robust single-image estimation by fusing foundation model features with camera geometry
AU  - Zhu C.
JO  - ISPRS Journal of Photogrammetry and Remote Sensing
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-10-01
VL  - 240
SP  - 396-409
DO  - 10.1016/j.isprsjprs.2026.07.019
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.isprsjprs.2026.07.019
AB  - Estimating the gravity direction from a single aerial image provides an absolute vertical prior, which is valuable for spatial reasoning when multi-view stereo or external spatial references are unavailable. Although onboard inertial measurement units (IMUs) provide platform attitude, transferring these measurements to the camera coordinate frame requires precise synchronization and accurate extrinsic calibration, which are not always satisfied for low-cost aerial platforms. Moreover, such calibration information is often unavailable for crowdsourced or historical aerial imagery. These limitations make vision-based approaches a practical alternative for estimating gravity direction directly from images. However, reliable estimation from a single aerial image remains challenging due to the wide range of aerial viewing angles, which often leads to ambiguous geometric cues. In this work, we propose Aerial Image Gravity Direction Estimation (AGE), a framework for estimating gravity direction from a single aerial image. To resolve geometric ambiguity under diverse viewing configurations, AGE combines semantically rich features from a frozen DINOv2 backbone with camera-aware geometric features derived from known camera intrinsics. Geometric reasoning is guided by supervision from an intermediate Perspective Field, while gravity is predicted as a 3D unit vector for stable learning across aerial viewpoints. To facilitate training and evaluation, we construct AGE-Pano, a large-scale synthetic dataset containing 28,500 images with accurate ground-truth gravity directions and diverse camera viewpoints and intrinsics. Experiments on both synthetic and real-world aerial imagery demonstrate state-of-the-art accuracy and robustness under aerial viewing conditions.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Monitoring wetland variations using Sentinel-2: Case study of Keta Lagoon complex ramsar site
AU  - Dzomeku M.E.
JO  - Journal of Atmospheric and Solar Terrestrial Physics
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-10-01
VL  - 287
DO  - 10.1016/j.jastp.2026.106955
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jastp.2026.106955
AB  - Anthropogenic pressures increasingly threaten coastal wetlands across West Africa, yet systematic assessments of their degradation remain scarce. This study quantified spatiotemporal land use and land cover (LULC) dynamics within Ghana's Keta Lagoon Complex Ramsar Site (KLCRS) from 2015 to 2023, using dense time-series Sentinel-2 imagery, Random Forest machine learning, and the Google Earth Engine platform. Classification achieved high accuracy (Overall Accuracy >89%; Kappa >0.84) across all periods. Two critical findings emerged: natural vegetation declined by 122.05 km2 and flooded vegetation by 25.17 km2, driven primarily by rapid settlement expansion (+82.98 km2) and salt mining operations, which exhibited the highest proportional growth (131% increase). The simultaneous expansion of saltpan areas (17.48 km2, 131%) and contraction of farmland (18.6%) between 2020 and 2023 in the same areas reveals a spatial association that warrants further investigation. While this pattern is consistent with salinization impacts reported elsewhere, establishing causality would require field-based soil and water quality measurements not available in this study. These findings confirm the KLCRS's unsustainable trajectory and underscore the urgent need for evidence-based policy interventions, including strategic zoning, promotion of sustainable livelihoods, and integrated coastal zone management, to conserve this ecologically significant Ramsar site. The findings of the study are essential for monitoring threatened coastal wetlands, with potential applicability to similar systems across West Africa.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Spatio-temporal variability of land cover dynamics and land degradation vulnerability using cellular automata and Artificial Neural Networks
AU  - Thakur T.K.
JO  - Physics and Chemistry of the Earth
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-10-01
VL  - 144
DO  - 10.1016/j.pce.2026.104634
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.pce.2026.104634
AB  - In Central India, land-use and land-cover (LULC) transitions have intensified over the last three decades, driven by agricultural expansion, mining activity, and peri-urban growth. This study evaluates spatio-temporal LULC change dynamics (1994-2023) and projects future scenarios (2040-2060). In this study, we used a predictor-screened Cellular Automata (CA) integrated with the Artificial Neural Network (ANN) (CA-ANN) framework. Landsat-derived LULCC maps achieved high classification reliability (κ = 0.87-0.97). Between 1994 and 2023, forest cover declined by 0.91%, primarily transitioning to agricultural and scrubland classes, while built-up and barren land expanded steadily. Model validation yielded a κ value of 0.87, indicating strong agreement between simulated and observed LULC patterns. Future projections indicate continued contraction of scrubland (−1.94%) and forest (−1.19%) by 2060, accompanied by an increase in built-up (+1.32%), agricultural (+0.58%), and barren land (+1.63%). Land degradation vulnerability index (LDVI) mapping revealed that over half of the study area falls within moderate to high vulnerability zones, particularly across forest-agriculture transition belts and mining corridors. This integrated modelling framework provides spatially explicit evidence to guide restoration prioritization, landscape planning, and monitoring efforts aligned with land degradation neutrality targets.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Improving water pixel detection in SWOT Pixel Cloud (PIXC) data over small reservoirs using machine learning methods
AU  - Shirafkan S.
JO  - Journal of Hydrology
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-10-01
VL  - 679
DO  - 10.1016/j.jhydrol.2026.136247
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jhydrol.2026.136247
AB  - This study presents a machine learning-based framework to improve water pixel detection in SWOT Pixel Cloud (PIXC) data for reservoir monitoring applications. Unlike general land-cover classification, the primary objective is to reduce false positive and false negative water detections in PIXC products, thereby improving the accuracy of derived reservoir surface area and volume estimates.The proposed framework integrates PIXC-derived features with an external long-term water occurrence prior (based on Landsat 5–8 observations) to refine pixel-level water detection. Three supervised learning algorithms, namely K-Nearest Neighbors (KNN), Random Forest (RF), and Extreme Gradient Boosting (XGBoost), are used not only as predictive models but also as diagnostic tools to investigate the behavior of PIXC-derived inputs within the feature space. These inputs include radar backscatter observables, interferometric coherence, detection quality indicators, and geometry-dependent parameters.The models are trained and evaluated across three reservoirs in Iran (Dez, Shahid Rajaei, and Agh Chay), selected to represent distinct hydro-climatic and geomorphological conditions, and are subsequently applied to eight reservoirs to assess their spatial transferability. Performance is quantified using pixel-level Intersection-over-Union (IoU), together with hydrological consistency metrics including correlation coefficient, Normalized Root Mean Square Error (NRMSE), and Kling–Gupta Efficiency (KGE) against in-situ observations.Beyond predictive performance, the results provide interpretability insights into PIXC input features. In particular, features related to detection uncertainty, interferometric coherence, and imaging geometry consistently show strong influence on water detection performance, suggesting that these components of the PIXC processing chain play a critical role in misclassification patterns. This finding highlights potential directions for improving uncertainty representation and quality control within future SWOT PIXC products.Results indicate that all machine learning models improve agreement with both SWOT PIXC and LakeSP products in terms of water detection performance and consistency with in-situ-derived reservoir area estimates.Overall, the findings suggest that incorporating physically meaningful PIXC features together with external hydrological priors can significantly enhance water pixel detection reliability. This approach provides practical guidance for improving downstream SWOT-derived reservoir products and offers insights that may support future refinements of the PIXC processing chain without requiring modifications to the original satellite data stream.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Monitoring land-centric business processes using remote sensing and satellite data
AU  - Chan V.
JO  - Information Systems
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-10-01
VL  - 141
DO  - 10.1016/j.is.2026.102739
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.is.2026.102739
AB  - Process mining has been intensively used for business processes that are extensively supported by information systems. The tight integration of information processing and process execution, as leveraged in the service sector, is, however, often absent in land-centric processes such as farming. Land-centric processes exhibit some challenging characteristics that make it difficult to monitor them in real-time: they unfold continuously over time, yet with clearly identifiable states. In this paper, we address the challenge of monitoring land-centric processes. We introduce a framework to generate event logs of land-centric processes by utilizing remote sensing systems such as satellites. We demonstrate the feasibility of our approach using publicly available data on agricultural processes in the United States.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Hcropland30: A hybrid 30-m global cropland map generated by leveraging global land cover products and Landsat data via deep learning
AU  - Hu Q.
JO  - Remote Sensing of Environment
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-10-01
VL  - 344
DO  - 10.1016/j.rse.2026.115552
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.rse.2026.115552
AB  - While global cropland mapping has seen notable improvements over the past decade, with several 10–30 m global land use and land cover (LULC) products now available, these products often suffer from considerable classification errors and spatial inconsistencies in cropland information. In this study, we developed a hybrid 30 m global cropland map for 2020, namely Hcropland30, to advance cropland mapping at a global scale. We started our approach with a hierarchical sampling strategy that used the simulated annealing method to select representative 1° × 1° grids globally and identify point-level samples within these grids, considering factors like cropland heterogeneity, spectral feature, climate, and topography. We then employed an ensemble learning technique that integrated multiple classifiers, including support vector machine (SVM), random forest (RF), multi-layer perceptron (MLP), and gradient boosted decision tree (GBDT), to expand these sparse point-level samples into area-level pseudo-labels. These pseudo-labels were used to train a U-Net model for predicting global cropland, using Landsat-derived seasonal NDVI composites and six LULC datasets as inputs. Our results revealed considerable spatial discrepancies among existing 10–30 m LULC products, with areas exhibiting ‘high’ to ‘very high’ inconsistency significantly outnumbering those of ‘medium’ to ‘low’ inconsistency. Our Hcropland30 map achieved an overall accuracy of 89.8% and a cropland F1-score of 0.85, higher than other LULC datasets, especially in regions with high landscape heterogeneity. Although LULC datasets provide useful prior information, the proposed framework is robust to variations in the number, type, and combination of LULC inputs. Furthermore, given the availability of historic Landsat data and LULC datasets, this methodology can be readily extended to create multi-temporal Hcropland30 maps and adapted for global mapping of other land cover types. Overall, this study not only generated a high-accuracy global cropland dataset but also introduced a robust methodology for diverse global mapping applications by leveraging deep learning and varied prior knowledge.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - LSTM-based weighted approach for TMY generation in extreme Antarctic climate
AU  - Zhang G.
JO  - Energy
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-10-01
VL  - 361
DO  - 10.1016/j.energy.2026.142034
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.energy.2026.142034
AB  - Under Antarctica's highly complex climatic conditions, characterizing building-relevant meteorological variability and constructing typical meteorological year (TMY) datasets for distinct climate zones are essential for climate-adaptive research stations. However, existing TMY methods rely on fixed weighting schemes and cannot capture the nonstationary spatiotemporal variability of Antarctic meteorological parameters or the nonlinear coupling among climatic factors affecting building loads, leading to deviations in building performance simulations. To address this issue, a continuous meteorological dataset was developed from PANDA observations supplemented with satellite and reanalysis products. Three building climate zones are identified, including plateau extreme cold, steep-slope extreme wind, and coastal severe cold zones, and five representative stations are selected. A building-response-informed weighting scheme based on long short-term memory (LSTM) is introduced to replace the fixed empirical weights in the Sandia method. Results show that temperature is the most influential meteorological parameter. Compared with the fixed temperature weight of 0.2 in the conventional Sandia method, its weight increases to approximately 0.6 in the plateau extreme cold zone, reducing the mean absolute error (MAE) of building energy simulation by 0.2 kWh/m2. Compared with conventional methods used in low- and mid-latitude regions, the proposed approach yields TMY results that better match long-term meteorological records and multi-year average building energy consumption, with maximum root mean square error (RMSE) reductions of 3.08 °C and 0.96 kWh/m2. The Sandia-LSTM method provides a building-response-informed approach to weighting-factor determination for TMY generation in extreme climates and supplies representative annual weather inputs for Antarctic building-energy assessment.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Integrating remote sensing, machine learning, and stable isotopes to diagnose hydrological drivers and water sources of a semi-arid wetland under coal mining disturbance
AU  - Yin Z.
JO  - Journal of Hydrology Regional Studies
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-10-01
VL  - 67
DO  - 10.1016/j.ejrh.2026.103825
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ejrh.2026.103825
AB  - Study region: The Tangsige Marsh Wetland is situated in the Yanjiga River Basin, Inner Mongolia, China, a semi-arid area under climatic pressure and coal mining disturbance. Study focus: We designed an integrated framework that synergistically combines multi-decadal Landsat time-series analysis (Random Forest), XGBoost-SHAP driver attribution, and stable isotope tracers (δD, δ18O, d-excess) with a Bayesian mixing model (MixSIAR) to diagnose hydrological drivers and water sources of the wetland. New hydrological insights: Wetland area declined by 35.5% from 2000 to 2022, exhibiting four distinct stages of decline and recovery. DEM and NDVI dominate wetland distribution. Key drivers—NDVI, population density, and precipitation—exhibit nonlinear thresholds, and synchronous changes in their SHAP values correspond to the different evolutionary stages. Precipitation (47.9%) and groundwater (35.3%) are the dominant water sources, with coal mine drainage contributing 7.6% on average but reaching nearly 10% locally. A south-to-north gradient from groundwater-dominated to strongly evaporated precipitation-dominated explains the northern wetland's greater drought sensitivity. This macro-micro framework bridges long-term dynamics and process-scale hydrology, revealing that wetland evolution arises from the temporal alignment or misalignment of multiple drivers.
