TY  - JOUR
TI  - Satellite observations coupled with a buffer-zone framework for regional methane mapping
AU  - Burak D.N.
JO  - Advances in Space Research
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-10-01
VL  - 78
IS  - 7
SP  - 6852-6882
DO  - 10.1016/j.asr.2026.07.073
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.asr.2026.07.073
AB  - This study evaluates the long-term spatial and temporal distribution of methane emissions from rice-growing regions and their source-centered dispersion using a wind-aware buffer-zone approach. Areas with buffer zone radii ranging from 0 to 125 km around identified rice fields were established using multi-source satellite data. Monthly average CH4 data from the Sentinel-5P TROPOMI sensor were acquired for the years 2019 to 2024, and a paddy-fraction weighted retrieval was applied to enable sub-pixel attribution. Methane concentrations in the analyzed region were investigated together with prevailing wind direction (downwind/upwind sectors) and ERA5-Land climate variables. Spatiotemporal trends and statistical significance were comprehensively evaluated. The findings indicate that CH4 emissions from rice paddies progressively diminish with downwind distance, with three independent statistical tests on yearly anomalies confirming a significant decay (linear slope = −0.095 ppb/km, p < 10−7; Spearman ρ = −0.383, p < 10−7; ANOVA F = 5.62, p < 10−4). Six-year mean CH4 concentrations declined from 1891.58 ppb in weighted paddy areas to 1879.17 ppb in the 100–125 km buffer ring. CH4 concentration in rice fields decreases to a minimum in February (non-cultivation period) and peaks in September (late-stage ripening and harvest), indicating a strong correlation between CH4 emissions and the agricultural activity cycle. Long-term trend analysis using the Yue–Wang modified Mann–Kendall test revealed statistically significant increasing CH4 trends in all regions (p < 0.001), with paddy areas exhibiting a higher increase rate than the Türkiye national average yet remaining below the global rate. This study contributes to the literature by employing remote sensing to identify the emission source (rice fields), apply wind-aware sectoral and statistical analyses, and evaluate long-term CH4 dynamics in regional and global context. The results demonstrate the importance of incorporating rice cultivation into regional planning to prevent high-emission zones and to contribute to climate change mitigation.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - DADFENet: A dual-branch adaptive and dynamic feature enhancement network for hyperspectral change detection
AU  - Sheng M.
JO  - Expert Systems with Applications
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-09-25
VL  - 327
DO  - 10.1016/j.eswa.2026.132851
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.eswa.2026.132851
AB  - Hyperspectral Change Detection, a core technology in remote sensing image processing, aims to accurately identify subtle land surface changes by leveraging the abundant spatial-spectral information of bi-temporal hyperspectral images. However, existing mainstream methods rely on CNNs or Transformers with fixed architectures, which not only lack efficient attention mechanisms to enhance the representation of differential features but also fail to fully explore the deep correlations between bi-temporal features due to their fixed convolution kernels and network structures. To address these issues, this paper proposes a novel Dual-branch Adaptive Dynamic Feature Enhancement Network (DADFENet), adaptively focusing on the spatial regions and spectral channels most relevant to change detection while suppressing irrelevant information. Its dual-branch architecture realizes an end-to-end mapping from bi-temporal images to change detection results, consisting of three blocks: the multiple Local-Global Feature Adaptive Enhancement (LGFAE) block, Difference Feature Learning Capability Enhancement (DFLCE) block, and Dual-branch Change Feature Dynamic Fusion (DCFDF) block. Specifically, the LGFAE block integrates Deformable Convolution (DC) and Adaptive Spatial-Spectral Hybrid Attention (AS2HA) to achieve cross-branch feature interaction and dynamic adjustment of spatial-spectral attention weights. The DFLCE block fuses DC, Deformable Spatial-Channel Attention (DSCA) and Wavelet Multiscale Attention (WMSA) to focus on core change regions and improve feature discriminability. The DCFDF block introduces a Spatial-Spectral Vision Transformer (SSViT) for global context modeling, realizes adaptive and efficient fusion of dual-branch features via a Dynamic Fusion (DF) module. Experimental results on four public hyperspectral datasets demonstrate that DADFENet outperforms state-of-the-art methods. The code will be open-sourced at https://github.com/SMingshuai/DADFENet.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Integrating remote sensing and deep learning models to assess construction area vulnerability in Ho Chi Minh City after the 2025 provincial merger
AU  - Thi Do A.N.
JO  - Advances in Space Research
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-09-15
VL  - 78
IS  - 6
SP  - 5846-5866
DO  - 10.1016/j.asr.2026.07.013
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.asr.2026.07.013
AB  - The swift urbanization and administrative amalgamation among regions in Vietnam have intensified the demands on the construction area, requiring advanced vulnerability assessment tools that can capture inter-regional interactions and ecological integration. This research employed 10 m Sentinel-2 multispectral imagery alongside 317 field survey points to effectively classify land use land cover (LULC) through the application of Convolutional Neural Networks (CNN). The resultant LULC data was subsequently integrated with 16 terrain, natural, and anthropogenic variables within an Adaptive Neuro Fuzzy Inference System enhanced by an Artificial Bee Colony (ABC-ANFIS) framework, where the ABC algorithm optimized fuzzy membership functions and rule parameters to improve nonlinear predictions of vulnerability in urban development zones. The CNN model achieved remarkable classification performance, with Overall Accuracy (OA) exceeding 86% and a Kappa coefficient greater than 0.83, demonstrating its proficiency in distinguishing various cover types, especially constructed land, water features, and green areas. This investigation integrated both natural and anthropogenic terrain variables to create a vulnerability map of the construction area, attaining commendable results (R2adj = 0.83; RMSE = 5.8; and MAE = 3.72). The findings indicated that Ho Chi Minh City, the Thu Duc – Di An – Thuan An urban corridor, and Ba Ria city emerged as resilience hotspots, with values surpassing 0.8, indicative of a high concentration of infrastructure coupled with a limited capacity for ecological buffering. Conversely, peri-urban ecological areas such as Can Gio, Phu Giao, and Xuyen Moc exhibited low vulnerability levels due to their extensive forest cover, water bodies, and agricultural landscapes which collectively contribute to environmental regulation. While the 10 m spatial resolution of Sentinel-2 satellite imagery is adequate for regional-scale analyses, it may constrain the detection of micro-scale infrastructure vulnerabilities in densely constructed urban environments. Overall, this research proposed a cohesive framework that integrates remote sensing and machine learning methodologies, aimed at enhancing resilience assessments in regions that have undergone administrative consolidation, thereby providing scientific guidance for spatial planning focused on sustainability and urban resilience.
ER  -

