TI  - Knowledge-driven random forest models for identifying soybean planting areas from Sentinel-1/2 satellite imagery: An interpretable mapping approach
AU  - Wan X.
JO  - Advances in Space Research
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-09-15
VL  - 78
IS  - 6
SP  - 5826-5845
DO  - 10.1016/j.asr.2026.07.012
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.asr.2026.07.012
AB  - Black-box algorithms, such as machine learning and deep learning, dominate soybean remote sensing mapping, their opaque decision-making processes often obscure valuable insights. The rise of Explainable Artificial Intelligence (XAI) has highlighted the importance of interpretability in such methods. However, traditional knowledge-based approaches for soybean mapping rely heavily on extensive experimentation and suffer from slow knowledge updates. To bridge this gap, this study introduces a novel knowledge extraction technique that interprets trained RF models, integrates the insights into the ensemble process to reconstruct decision rules, and selects the most effective rules as the target. Utilizing Sentinel-1/2 satellite imagery, 31 features were generated, and an interpretable soybean mapping approach (ISMA) was developed, comprising four key feature rules: B11 > 0.194, EVI > 0.811, GNDVI > 0.755, and NDre > 0.602. The evaluation revealed that ISMA achieved accuracy levels between 89.47% and 96.02%, with F1-scores ranging from 76.52% to 89.01%, demonstrating robust classification performance. Although ISMA’s extraction accuracy was marginally lower than that of the original RF model, it utilized only four features, reducing data volume by 87.1% while maintaining high interpretability. ISMA represents a viable alternative to black-box algorithms in the era of XAI, offering a balance between precision, interpretability, and efficiency.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Hyperspectral image classification using depth-wise separable convolution-based Swin Retentive Network
AU  - Arya R.K.
JO  - Advances in Space Research
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-09-15
VL  - 78
IS  - 6
SP  - 5719-5737
DO  - 10.1016/j.asr.2026.07.003
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.asr.2026.07.003
AB  - Transformer-based models and Convolutional neural networks (CNNs) have been widely used for hyperspectral image classification (HSIC) due to their ability to extract global and local features; however, transformer-based models are not well-suited for fine-grained local spatial patterns and incur high computational cost, whereas CNNs struggle to model global context and long-range dependencies. Recently, Retentive Networks (RetNet) have been proposed and shown good performance in HSIC with low inference cost and good training parallelism; however, RetNet still struggles to capture local information. To address these challenges, this study proposes a Depth-wise Separable Convolution-based Swin Retentive Network (DSCSRN) for HSIC, which consists of three modules: 1) a depth-wise separable 3D convolution with a squeeze-and-excitation (DS3DC-SE) module to capture rich local spectral–spatial relationships at low computational cost, 2) a data augmentation module to enhance the model’s learning capacity and robustness, and 3) a Swin Retentive Network (SRN) module using a shifted-window mechanism to model hierarchical and complex global spectral-spatial structures and long-term dependencies efficiently. Together, these modules enable DSCSRN to learn intrinsic spectral-spatial relationships from both local and global perspectives while maintaining a relatively low computational cost. Furthermore, tensor singular spectrum analysis (TSSA) is used to denoise the data and extract robust, low-rank, and representative features, which further improve classification performance. Experiments on four widely used HSI datasets, i.e., Indian Pines, Pavia University, Salinas, and HyRANK-Loukia, demonstrate that DSCSRN achieves OAs of 99.47%, 99.92%, 99.98%, and 93.25%, respectively, outperforms state-of-the-art methods, and provides a competitive and parameter-efficient solution for HSIC.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - A novel deep learning-based method for adaptive building extraction from high-resolution remote sensing imagery using independent external probabilistic priors
AU  - Naanjam R.
JO  - Advances in Space Research
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-09-15
VL  - 78
IS  - 6
SP  - 6002-6020
DO  - 10.1016/j.asr.2026.07.029
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.asr.2026.07.029
AB  - Building extraction from high-resolution remote sensing imagery remains challenging despite advances in deep learning, particularly when labeled data are limited and models need to generalize across unseen scenes and geographic regions. Therefore, this research proposes a novel prior-conditioned segmentation framework that leverages external, independently generated probabilistic building maps as a reliability-aware guidance signal within an adaptive training strategy. The method consists of three main stages: (1) an external probabilistic building prior is learned independently and used as an additional source of spatial confidence; (2) a U-Net-style encoder–decoder is trained with a prior-conditioned adaptive gating module that embeds the prior into feature space and modulates shallow and mid-level encoder representations using a soft range-based gating function with learnable parameters, followed by channel–spatial attention to selectively enhance high-confidence building evidence while suppressing confounding background responses; (3) training optimization is improved by introducing a new confidence-aware loss function that implements adaptive supervision through dynamic pixel-wise reweighting based on the joint reliability of the external prior and the model’s predictive uncertainty, further complemented by region-consistency and boundary-sensitive terms. Experiments on three benchmarks (WHU, Massachusetts, and Inria) show that with only 30% of the labeled WHU training data, the proposed approach achieves 96.83% F1-score and 94.57% IoU. In cross-domain evaluations, it improves precision by reducing false positives by over 12% compared with a standard U-Net, while also delivering consistent gains over both CNN– and Transformer-based baselines. These results suggest that the proposed framework is well suited for practical building mapping scenarios where annotations are scarce and domain shifts are unavoidable.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Estimating daily air temperature in Zanjan Province using MODIS data and Random Forest: a seasonal and spatial analysis (2018–2022)
AU  - Angouti R.
JO  - Advances in Space Research
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-09-15
VL  - 78
IS  - 6
SP  - 5808-5825
DO  - 10.1016/j.asr.2026.07.011
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.asr.2026.07.011
AB  - Accurate estimation of daily air temperature is vital for climate monitoring, agricultural planning, and environmental management, particularly in regions with limited meteorological data. This study presents a modeling framework that integrates satellite-derived land surface temperature (LST) from MODIS sensors with environmental variables from the ERA5 reanalysis to estimate daily maximum, minimum, and mean air temperatures across Zanjan Province, Iran, for the period 2018–2022. A Random Forest (RF) machine learning model was developed and compared with a Multiple Linear Regression (MLR) model using ground-based station observations for validation. After model optimization and data quality control, the RF achieved root-mean-square errors (RMSE) of 2.99 °C, 1.88 °C, and 1.85 °C for maximum, minimum, and mean air temperatures, respectively, outperforming the MLR model in all cases. Feature importance analysis identified MODIS LST as the most influential predictor, followed by soil moisture. Seasonal evaluation showed the highest model accuracy in autumn and spring, while reduced performance was mainly observed for maximum air temperature during summer. As an independent case study, the RF model trained over the 2018–2022 period was applied to a representative summer day (10 July 2023), producing realistic spatial temperature patterns at 1-km resolution. Validation against station observations demonstrated strong agreement for minimum and mean temperatures (R > 0.90) and moderate agreement for maximum temperature (R = 0.65). These findings highlight the effectiveness of integrating satellite observations with machine learning techniques for high-resolution air temperature estimation in regions with limited observational coverage.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Enhancing financial market prediction through multi-stage signal decomposition and hybrid machine learning
AU  - Kamwele Mutinda J.
JO  - Expert Systems with Applications
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-09-15
VL  - 326
DO  - 10.1016/j.eswa.2026.132670
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.eswa.2026.132670
AB  - Forecasting stock indices remains challenging due to nonlinearity, nonstationarity, and multiscale dynamics in financial time series. This study proposes the CEEMDAN-SE-VMD-BiGRU-Ridge framework, a novel hybrid model that integrates hierarchical decomposition and adaptive prediction to improve accuracy. The method applies complete ensemble empirical mode decomposition with adaptive noise (CEEMDAN) for initial decomposition into intrinsic mode functions. Sample entropy and K-means clustering classify modes into high-frequency, medium-frequency, low-frequency, and residual groups. Variational mode decomposition (VMD) is then applied specifically to further decompose the high-frequency group, suppressing noise and achieving precise noise separation. Bidirectional gated recurrent units (BiGRU) model the high-frequency decomposed IMFs from VMD and the medium-frequency components, while ridge regression captures stable trends in the low-frequency and residual components. Final forecasts are obtained by adding all component predictions linearly. Out-of-sample evaluation on six major global indices—Financial Times Stock Exchange 100 (FTSE), Shanghai Stock Exchange Composite (SSEC), Standard & Poor’s 500 (S&P 500), Deutscher Aktienindex (DAX), New York Stock Exchange Composite (NYSE), and Hang Seng Index (HSI)—using 70-30 hold-out shows excellent performance. The framework achieves R2 values of 0.9958 (FTSE), 0.9944 (SSEC), 0.9994 (S&P 500), 0.9992 (DAX), 0.9986 (NYSE), and 0.9975 (HSI), with substantially lower root mean square error (RMSE), mean absolute error (MAE), symmetric mean absolute percentage error (SMAPE), and higher R2 than thirteen benchmark models. Modified Diebold-Mariano tests (M-DM) and Model Confidence Set (MCS) analysis confirm statistical superiority. Backtesting yields higher Sharpe ratios, with robustness validated across splits, three business cycles (pre-pandemic expansion, COVID-19 recession, post-crisis recovery), and two contrasting market regimes (COVID-19 stimulus bullish phase, Russia-Ukraine conflict bearish period) as well as varying temporal lookback window. These results highlight the effectiveness of hierarchical decomposition and adaptive deep learning for precise financial forecasting and investment applications.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - LandslideSegFormer: A non-local context-aware dual-branch network for accurate small-scale landslide segmentation from remote sensing imagery
AU  - Lee X.
JO  - ISPRS Journal of Photogrammetry and Remote Sensing
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-09-01
VL  - 239
SP  - 54-75
DO  - 10.1016/j.isprsjprs.2026.05.047
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.isprsjprs.2026.05.047
AB  - Small-scale landslide segmentation remains a classic yet challenging task in remote sensing image analysis. Current transformer-based landslide segmentation models suffer from two main limitations: difficulty in detecting small targets and the loss of boundary details. To address these issues, we propose LandslideSegFormer, a semantic segmentation network inspired by the SegFormer framework but redesigned in both encoder and decoder modules. LandslideSegFormer jointly leverages transformers and convolutional structures to capture both global and local information, thereby enhancing the representation of small-scale landslide features. More importantly, we introduce a non-local block as a global feature interaction mechanism, which performs similarity-driven aggregation across feature maps. By computing pairwise similarities in the feature space, the mechanism adaptively controls information exchange such that semantically similar locations directly “share” features. With a single-layer operation, it establishes long-range dependencies whose strength is determined by feature content rather than spatial distance, enabling the modeling of complex relationships between landslides and environmental factors. Furthermore, we design a gated hybrid-attention decoder tailored for LandslideSegFormer’s encoder. Through gated propagation, the decoder selectively controls multi-scale feature transmission, preserves high-resolution details, suppresses background noise, and accurately localizes small-scale landslide regions. Extensive experiments on the large-scale CAS Landslide dataset, which includes both satellite and UAV imagery, systematically evaluate LandslideSegFormer from the perspectives of small-object segmentation and complex boundary delineation. LandslideSegFormer, with a lightweight B0-based design, outperforms SegFormer-B0 and B2, improving landslide IoU by 5.34 percentage points on the complete test set and 8.16 percentage points on small-scale landslides (with boundary IoU improved by 9.08 percentage points), demonstrating superior capability in detecting small-scale landslides and complex boundaries.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - FreqChange: Frequency-aware spatiotemporal learning with prior guidance for semantic change detection in remote sensing images
AU  - Huang H.
JO  - ISPRS Journal of Photogrammetry and Remote Sensing
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-09-01
VL  - 239
SP  - 124-146
DO  - 10.1016/j.isprsjprs.2026.05.042
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.isprsjprs.2026.05.042
AB  - Semantic change detection plays a critical role in monitoring Earth’s surface dynamics by identifying the locations and semantic categories of changes from bi-temporal remote sensing images. Existing methods primarily employ a shared-weight image encoder coupled with a change extractor to detect changes in the spatial domain, while lacking sufficient temporal modeling and effective exploitation of semantic priors. As a result, they struggle to accurately localize changed regions and maintain intra-class semantic consistency. To address these limitations, we propose FreqChange, a frequency-aware spatiotemporal learning framework with semantic prior guidance. Specifically, a frequency change-aware module (FCAM) is integrated into the spatiotemporal learning framework to accurately perceive and localize changed regions by comprehensively analyzing both low- and high-frequency components between bi-temporal features. Additionally, to enhance the intra-class semantic consistency of bi-temporal images, we propose a semantic consistency enhancement module (SCEM), which leverages semantic priors derived from large-scale pre-trained vision foundation models, combined with semantic consistency upsampling, to effectively facilitate spatiotemporal learning. Through their collaborative integration, these modules collectively enable the precise detection of land cover changes from bi-temporal images while preserving intra-class semantic consistency. Extensive experiments on four well-recognized datasets (i.e., HRSCD, SECOND, HiUCD-mini, and Landsat-SCD) demonstrate that FreqChange consistently outperforms state-of-the-art methods in both quantitative metrics and qualitative visualizations. By uniting frequency-domain analysis and semantic priors, FreqChange establishes a new paradigm for accurate and reliable semantic change detection in remote sensing.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Agricultural parcel polygon reconstruction using boundary line segment assembly
AU  - Zuo X.
JO  - ISPRS Journal of Photogrammetry and Remote Sensing
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-09-01
VL  - 239
SP  - 203-222
DO  - 10.1016/j.isprsjprs.2026.05.049
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.isprsjprs.2026.05.049
AB  - Vectorized extraction of agricultural parcels is essential for large-area cropland monitoring and management. Existing approaches either predict raster masks followed by heuristic vectorization or directly regress field contours using dense vertex sequences along the boundary. However, these pipelines often result in limited boundary regularity, redundant polygon vertices, and incomplete reconstruction of parcels that span multiple image patches. To address these issues, we propose the Boundary-to-Polygon Network (B2PNet), a Boundary Line Segment-based (BLS-based) reconstruction pipeline for generating agricultural field polygons. Instead of relying on dense pixel boundaries or vertex-based contour regression, B2PNet represents each parcel as a set of oriented and ordered Boundary Line Segments (BLSs) and predicts their pairwise connectivity to assemble closed polygonal geometries. This representation explicitly encodes structural boundary information, leading to more accurate boundary localization as well as significantly improved polygon regularity and vertex efficiency. To support large-area mapping, we further develop an image-level post-processing strategy that leverages the boundary adherence property of the BLS-based pipeline, ensuring geometric alignment across patch borders and enabling complete reconstruction of parcels that span multiple patches. Extensive experiments on the AI4SmallFarms and iFLYTEK datasets show that B2PNet achieves state-of-the-art performance across both region-level and boundary-level evaluations. On AI4SmallFarms, B2PNet improves the F1-scores for parcel extraction and boundary delineation to 54.39% and 53.54%, respectively, substantially outperforming the strong contour-based baseline E2EVAP (48.91% and 48.78%). Moreover, B2PNet achieves the best polygon regularity (PoLiS, MTA) and vertex efficiency (N ratio, C-IoU), confirming its advantages in geometric fidelity and polygon compactness. Cross-dataset experiments further demonstrate its strong robustness and generalization capability across diverse agricultural regions.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - SARU: A Shadow-Aware and Removal Unified Framework for remote sensing images with new benchmarks
AU  - Bo Z.Y.
JO  - ISPRS Journal of Photogrammetry and Remote Sensing
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-09-01
VL  - 239
SP  - 39-53
DO  - 10.1016/j.isprsjprs.2026.05.022
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.isprsjprs.2026.05.022
AB  - Shadows are a prevalent problem in remote sensing imagery (RSI), degrading visual quality and severely limiting the performance of downstream tasks like object detection and semantic segmentation. Most prior works treat shadow detection and removal as separate, cascaded tasks, which can lead to cumbersome process and error accumulation. Furthermore, many deep learning methods rely on paired shadow and non-shadow images for training, which are often unavailable in practice. To address these challenges, we propose Shadow-Aware and Removal Unified (SARU) Framework, a cohesive two-stage framework. First, its dual-branch detection module (DBCSF-Net) fuses multi-color space and semantic features to generate high-fidelity shadow masks, effectively distinguishing shadows from dark objects. Then, leveraging these masks, a novel, training-free physical algorithm (N2[jls-end-space/]SGSR) restores illumination by transferring properties from adjacent non-shadow regions within the single input image. To facilitate rigorous evaluation and foster future work, we also introduce two new benchmark datasets: the RSI Shadow Detection (RSISD) dataset and the Single-image Shadow Removal Benchmark (SiSRB). Extensive experiments on the AISD and RSISD datasets demonstrate that SARU achieves state-of-the-art shadow detection performance with IoU scores of 86.35% and 89.71%, respectively, providing a performance gain of over 3.5% compared to recent methods like CADDN and SDDNet. For shadow removal, our training-free N2[jls-end-space/]SGSR algorithm attains an average processing speed of approximately 1.3s, which is over 10 times faster than the state-of-the-art MAOSD while maintains an SRI value close to 0.9 on both the AISD and SiSRB datasets, a level comparable to the advanced RS-GSSR method. By holistically integrating shadow detection and removal to mitigate error propagation and eliminating the dependency on paired training data, SARU establishes a robust, practical framework for real-world RSI analysis. The source code and datasets are publicly available at: https://github.com/AeroVILab-AHU/SARU.
ER  -

