TY  - JOUR
TI  - An end-to-end framework for semi-automated and cost-effective urban drainage system design: integrating deep learning, topology generation, and multi-objective optimization
AU  - Zhou Q.
JO  - Journal of Hydrology
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-09-01
VL  - 677
DO  - 10.1016/j.jhydrol.2026.135844
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jhydrol.2026.135844
AB  - Driven by climate change and urbanization, extreme rainfall events are becoming more frequent. Traditional experience-based drainage design methods lack global optimization and efficiency, making them increasingly unable to meet cost-effectiveness demands. This study introduces a novel framework comprising four key steps: (1) land-use identification and hydrological characterization via an enhanced DeepLabv3+ model applied to satellite imagery, facilitating subcatchment delineation and runoff coefficient estimation; (2) development of the dual-criterion impact factor system considering both construction and layout rationalities, along with a weight matrix to examine the influence of these factors on design schemes; (3) automated generation of network topology and capacity calculation using the improved shortest path algorithm within a directed weighted network; and (4) multi-objective optimization based on SWMM simulations under various rainfall scenarios to evaluate Expected Annual Damage (EAD) and construction costs, thereby deriving Pareto-optimal solutions. Results indicate that the segmentation model, enhanced by incorporating a prior knowledge mask, effectively identified major urban land-use types, particularly buildings, roads, and water. Prioritizing construction-related impacts guided the model toward design schemes with more favorable slopes and lower costs. Under comparable flood risk conditions, the optimized design achieved a 9.4% reduction in the maximum pipe diameter, a 21.9% decrease in the average burial depth in localized regions, and an approximate 25.0% reduction in total cost. Applications to the districts in Lanzhou and Guangzhou demonstrated the framework’s robust applicability across diverse urban environments. The proposed framework presents a systematic, data-driven approach to generating cost-effective urban drainage designs with reduced manual effort.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - UAV-based temporal synergistic estimation of multiple alfalfa qualities integrating physics-informed network and 3D allometric operator
AU  - Cheng T.
JO  - Plant Phenomics
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-09-01
VL  - 8
IS  - 3
DO  - 10.1016/j.plaphe.2026.100244
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.plaphe.2026.100244
AB  - Accurately monitoring alfalfa nutritional quality is essential for optimal pasture management. Yet, current UAV remote sensing methods rely on single-temporal imagery and empirical indices, limiting their ability to handle multi-stage growth dynamics, canopy spectral saturation, and canopy-to-whole-plant scale differences. Furthermore, small sample sizes often cause purely data-driven models to overfit correlations, yielding biologically unrealistic results. Overcoming these challenges, we designed a comprehensive quality estimation framework using 127 alfalfa core germplasms, combining high-dimensional spectral mining, a physics-informed network, and a 3D allometric transfer operator. After screening 14,960 spectral operators across original and log-transformed spaces, we applied a dual dimensionality reduction strategy to isolate optimal features. Four-band dual-difference structures proved highly sensitive to fiber components (ADF/NDF, |r| = 0.896), while logarithmic decoupling operators accurately isolated protein and nitrogen signals (CP/N, |r| = 0.868). We then engineered a Physics-Informed Sparse Shallow Network (PI-SSN). By leveraging temporal attention decoupling, it adaptively assigns growth-stage weights to different components and uses carbon-nitrogen metabolic constraints to maintain biological accuracy during multi-task retrieval. Multi-stage temporal data significantly boosted accuracy over single-period spectra. PI-SSN delivered exceptional test set coefficients of determination ((Formula presented) ) of 0.812–0.848 and RPDs >2.0 for N, CP, ADF, and NDF, easily outperforming standard baselines. To bridge the canopy-only observation gap, we introduced a 3D allometric transfer operator that incorporates canopy coverage and plant height. This effectively corrected vertical stem-leaf observation biases, enhancing Relative Feed Value (RFV) predictions. Ultimately, this approach offers a powerful new framework for high-throughput forage phenotyping.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - UMS-DETR: A lightweight Transformer-based framework for real-time ship detection in complex remote sensing scenes
AU  - Nguyen T.N.
JO  - Ocean Engineering
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-09-01
VL  - 365
IS  - P2
DO  - 10.1016/j.oceaneng.2026.127311
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.oceaneng.2026.127311
AB  - Real-time ship detection in optical remote sensing images remains challenging due to large scale variation, elongated target geometry, dense ship distribution, weak object boundaries, and complex maritime backgrounds. Although CNN-based detectors provide fast inference, they often have limited capability in modeling long-range contextual dependencies, whereas Transformer-based detectors usually introduce higher computational cost. To address these issues, this paper proposes UMS-DETR, a lightweight end-to-end Transformer-based detector for real-time ship detection in remote sensing images. Built upon the RT-DETR framework, UMS-DETR introduces two task-oriented designs. First, a Multi-Scale Large-Small (MSLS) convolution is designed to enhance ship feature extraction by combining multi-scale large-kernel perception with efficient small-kernel aggregation. Based on this convolutional operator, a lightweight Multi-Scale Large-Small Network (MSLSNet) backbone is developed to extract discriminative multi-scale features while preserving local geometric details of ship targets. Second, a Ship-Aware Dual-Path Agent Fusion (SDAF) module is proposed to enhance high-level semantic representations by integrating efficient global agent-based interaction and local context refinement. In addition, UMS-DETR adopts the uncertainty-minimal query selection strategy from RT-DETR to initialize object queries from reliable and object-relevant feature tokens, thereby improving decoder prediction stability. Extensive experiments are conducted on two public optical remote sensing ship detection benchmarks, ShipRSImageNet and SCCOS. On ShipRSImageNet, UMS-DETR achieves 57.4% mAP, 88.3% mAP50, and 53.4% mAP75 with only 18.1M parameters, 44.5G FLOPs, and 19.1 ms latency. On SCCOS, it obtains 47.5% mAP, 73.4% mAP50, and 41.6% mAP75, outperforming representative CNN-based and Transformer-based detectors. Qualitative visualization and ablation studies further demonstrate that the proposed MSLSNet backbone and SDAF module improve small ship sensitivity, localization accuracy, feature discrimination, and robustness in cluttered maritime scenes.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Technological advances, challenges and future prospects for remote sensing-driven climate change monitoring
AU  - Kirubakaran D.
JO  - Journal of Trace Elements and Minerals
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-09-01
VL  - 17
DO  - 10.1016/j.jtemin.2026.100318
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jtemin.2026.100318
AB  - Background: Climate change poses existential threats to Environ. ironmental sustainability, resource management, and global ecosystem stability. Remote sensing technologies have become indispensable for monitoring these climate-related processes, providing continuous, large-scale and high-resolution observations of the Earth surface and atmosphere. Purpose: This review examines recent advancements in remote sensing technologies and their expanding applications in climate change monitoring, Environ. ironmental sustainability and the study of trace elements and minerals. Furthermore, it evaluates the integration of artificial intelligence (AI) to enhance Environ. ironmental assessment and strategic decision-making. Methods: A comprehensive literature review was conducted, focusing on satellite platforms, unmanned aerial vehicles (UAVs), LiDAR, hyperspectral imaging, Synthetic Aperture Radar (SAR), and AI-driven analytics. We critically analyzed their applications across climate indicators, disaster management, agriculture, water resources, and geochemical risk assessments. Results: Significant progress has been made in tracking vital climate variables, including temperature anomalies, vegetation dynamics, land-cover shifts, and atmospheric composition. Advanced AI techniques such as machine learning, large language models (LLMs), vision-language models (VLMs) and geospatial foundation models (GFMs) have revolutionized data processing, prediction accuracy, and automated decision-support. Remote sensing also demonstrates profound potential in mapping soil geochemistry, heavy-metal contamination, mineral exploration and dust transport. However, challenges such as cloud interference, massive data processing requirements and limited ground-truthing remain significant constraints. Conclusions: Remote sensing continues to transform Earth system sciences by enabling timely, synoptic observations. The seamless integration of AI and advanced geospatial analytics further amplifies our capacity to build climate resilience and manage natural resources sustainably. Continued sensor innovation and rigorous validation frameworks are critical to maximizing the future utility of these technologies.
ER  -

