TI  - Satellite water quality monitoring of China’s coastal inland waters based on Google Earth Engine
AU  - Zhao Y.
JO  - Journal of Hydrology
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-09-01
VL  - 677
DO  - 10.1016/j.jhydrol.2026.135882
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jhydrol.2026.135882
AB  - Monitoring water quality parameters (WQPs) in coastal inland waters remains challenging because pronounced spatiotemporal variability and complex pollutant sources limit the robustness of conventional retrieval methods. High-resolution Earth-observation satellites improve spatial detail for fine-scale monitoring, but their application over large-area is constrained by substantial computational demands. Moreover, in optically complex waters, spectral information alone is often insufficient, highlighting the need to incorporate geographic and environmental predictors into WQP retrieval models. In this study, we developed a Google Earth Engine (GEE)-based workflow to retrieve four management-relevant WQPs across China’s coastal inland waters: permanganate index (CODMn), total nitrogen (TN), total phosphorus (TP), and turbidity (TUB). A large matched dataset was constructed by linking Sentinel-2 remote sensing reflectance (Rrs) with observations from automatic monitoring-stations, as well as geographic and environmental covariates. Based on this dataset, lightweight multimodal machine-learning models were developed to integrate optical, spatial, and environmental predictors within a computationally efficient, scalable framework. The models showed good predictive performance, with validation R2 values of 0.759–0.827 and independent test R2 values of 0.664–0.734, indicating satisfactory accuracy and transferability. Application to 2024 imagery in China’s coastal inland waters revealed clear spatial gradients, with CODMn, TN, and TP generally higher in northern regions, particularly in estuarine and nearshore waters. Seasonal patterns showed summer–autumn maxima for CODMn, TP, and TUB, whereas TN peaked in winter–spring. The proposed GEE-based framework provides a practical and transferable approach for WQP monitoring and water-environment assessment.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Multi-image super-resolution in remote sensing: A review of artificial intelligence techniques and challenges
AU  - Rubin I.
JO  - Acta Astronautica
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-09-01
VL  - 246
SP  - 643-663
DO  - 10.1016/j.actaastro.2026.04.043
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.actaastro.2026.04.043
AB  - The development of remote sensing technologies in recent years has led to significant advances in a range of tasks previously solved using more time- and labor-intensive approaches. Rapid monitoring and precise analysis are key goals that are pursued by companies and researchers, driving the progress in geospatial monitoring. In this field, artificial intelligence (AI) technologies serve as a vital instrument to address limitations caused by satellite payload constraints and various sources of signal distortion. AI algorithms can be used to artificially enhance images through denoising, deblurring, and detail improvement. A popular computer vision task for image enhancement is image super-resolution (SR), where an algorithm reconstructs fine-grained details from a lower-resolution image to produce an output equivalent to a higher spatial resolution. The goal of such approaches is to delineate objects and structures that cannot be identified directly in the original imagery. The most common approach in both remote sensing and general computer vision is single-image super-resolution (SISR). In contrast, multi-image super-resolution (MISR) — which utilizes several images from different dates or observation points — has been less thoroughly studied. MISR algorithms strive to capture multiple significant features both in temporal and spatial contexts. Despite the potential of the MISR technique, comprehensive analysis of its methodologies, limitations, and real-world efficacy in the remote sensing domain have not yet been well-studied in the literature. Therefore, this review aims to provide in-depth analysis of MISR techniques, including generative adversarial networks (GANs), visual transformers, and diffusion-based models, applied to remote sensing data. This review proposes a taxonomy and discuss the current main limitations and future development directions in this field with specific respect to satellite imaging, effective evaluation metrics, and downstream tasks.
ER  -

