DO  - 10.1016/j.jnc.2026.127361
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jnc.2026.127361
AB  - This study presents an integrated spatio-temporal assessment of ecological restoration in the Jharia Coalfield using multi-temporal dataset acquired between 2005 and 2024. A comprehensive remote sensing framework integrating spectral indices, including NDVI, NDMI, NDWI, Bare Soil Index (BSI), Land Surface Temperature (LST), and Land Use and Land Cover (LULC) analysis, was developed to evaluate long-term ecological transformations in one of India's most intensively mined coalfields. Temporal trends were quantified using the Mann–Kendall test and Sen's slope estimator, while an Analytical Hierarchy Process (AHP)-based Ecological Restoration Index (ERI) was formulated with high decision consistency (CR = 0.04). The findings reveal substantial mining-induced landscape modification over the past two decades. Mining areas increased from 24.90 km2 to 54.86 km2, while built-up land expanded from 31.85 km2 to 44.82 km2, reflecting rapid industrial and urban growth. In contrast, vegetation cover declined from 100.91 km2 to 94.50 km2, although localized recovery was observed in reclaimed sectors. Barren land decreased from 145.77 km2 in 2015 to 112.71 km2 in 2024, indicating measurable reclamation progress. Spatial analysis further highlighted heterogeneity in restoration patterns, with severe vegetation degradation concentrated in the western mining zone (−12.4%) and ecological recovery identified in eastern agricultural regions (+18.7%). ERI classification indicates that 50.94% of the study area falls within high to very high restoration categories, whereas 18.39% remains under low restoration conditions. A statistically significant positive relationship between ERI values and distance from mining sites (R2 = 0.67, p < 0.001) confirms the dominant influence of anthropogenic disturbance on ecological recovery. Persistent thermal anomalies associated with underground coal-fire zones, reaching maximum LST values of 94.35°C, continue to impede restoration effectiveness. The proposed ERI framework provides a scalable and transferable approach for monitoring ecological restoration in mining-impacted landscapes worldwide.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - A physics-guided and interpretable machine learning framework for rapid flood inundation mapping in complex flood retention areas
AU  - Zhu C.
JO  - Journal of Hydrology
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-09-01
VL  - 677
DO  - 10.1016/j.jhydrol.2026.135747
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jhydrol.2026.135747
AB  - Rapid and accurate flood mapping using Synthetic Aperture Radar (SAR) imagery is a critical tool for emergency response. While machine learning (ML) models have significantly enhanced the accuracy and efficiency of flood mapping, their “black box” nature limits their trustworthiness for critical emergency response applications. To address this issue, we developed an interpretable flood mapping framework based on Gaofen-3 (GF-3) SAR imagery that fuses remote sensing physics with data-driven analysis. The framework comprises three core components: (1) a novel physics-guided feature, the Generalized Normalized Difference Polarization Index (GNDPI), which transforms skewed backscatter data into a more separable bimodal distribution; (2) a Random Forest (RF) classification model incorporating multi-source features, including Gray-Level Co-occurrence Matrix (GLCM), Local Binary Pattern (LBP), and Haar Wavelet; and (3) the SHapley Additive exPlanations (SHAP) method, utilized to interpret the model’s decisions. The framework was successfully applied to the Dongdian flood retention area (DFRA) during China’s “23·7″ major flood event, yielding high accuracy (Overall Accuracy (OA) = 97.72%, Kappa = 0.953). In model comparisons, the RF model demonstrated over four times the computational efficiency of XGBoost, with a minimal 0.11% loss in accuracy, proving its practical value for emergency scenarios. Crucially, SHAP analysis revealed that the model learned a physically consistent decision logic, prioritizing features for macroscopic smoothness and microscopic uniformity, and leveraging complex feature interactions to differentiate water from non-water pixels. This study’s framework not only improves flood mapping performance but also demystifies the model’s decision logic via SHAP, offering a transparent and robust tool for rapid emergency response in data-scarce basins.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - A novel fusion framework for generating 30 m daily seamless soil moisture with reliable performance
AU  - Liu Y.
JO  - Journal of Hydrology
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-09-01
VL  - 677
DO  - 10.1016/j.jhydrol.2026.135791
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jhydrol.2026.135791
AB  - High-resolution (30 m) daily soil moisture (SM) dataset is critical for precision agriculture, drought monitoring, and water resource management. However, such dataset remains challenging to obtain due to the limited spatial and temporal resolutions in existing satellite products. To address this issue, this study proposes a novel two-step deep learning framework to produce a high-resolution (30 m) daily seamless SM with reliable accuracy, leveraging strengths from open-access remotely sensed products, terrain, and soil property datasets. The framework is independent from in-situ measurements. First, a convolutional neural network-based super-resolution model (MSRNet) downscales 500 m MODIS spectral bands (8 bands including visible, near-infrared, and thermal infrared) to 30 m resolution using Harmonized Landsat-Sentinel (HLS) data of the corresponding 8 bands. Second, a SM simulation network (SMSNet) learns the relationship between SM and 16 explanatory variables, including the downscaled MODIS bands, terrain features, soil properties, and 1 km SMAP/Sentinel SM at an intermediate resolution of 125 m. Third, the trained SMSNet is applied to 30 m inputs to generate seamless daily high-resolution SM estimations. Validated against in-situ measurements in Naqu (central Tibet Plateau) with significance test (p < 0.01), the 30 m SM achieves significant accuracy improvements over the original SMAP/Sentinel product, with increased correlation (R = 0.70 vs. 0.68) and reduced errors (RMSE = 0.09 m3/m3 vs. 0.12 m3/m3, ubRMSE = 0.07 m3/m3 vs. 0.10 m3/m3). Moreover, the cross-regional validation is conducted at the Maqu (northeast Tibet Plateau) for generalizability and transferability test of the established SMSNet, and achieves reasonable performances. This research provides a feasible solution for large spatial–temporal scale improvement of SM simulation through the combination of MSRNet and SMSNet, enabling field-scale SM applications, as demonstrated in the Tibetan Plateau region, without reliance on ground-based networks.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Collaborative discrimination of Cadmium and Lead contamination types and levels in Bok Choy based on multi-temporal hyperspectral data and RCBA-HMTL
AU  - Zou Z.
JO  - Journal of Hazardous Materials
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-09-01
VL  - 515
DO  - 10.1016/j.jhazmat.2026.143022
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jhazmat.2026.143022
AB  - The type and degree of heavy metal contamination in green vegetables are key factors for evaluating food safety. Current evaluation methods based on Hyperspectral Imaging (HSI) are mostly constrained by the single-task learning paradigm. Not only is it difficult to mine the correlated information between tasks, but it also faces the challenges of jointly modeling the high-dimensional redundancy, non-linear features, and dynamic responses of HSI data. To address this deficiency, this study proposes a novel RCBA hybrid deep network, which integrates Residual Convolutional Neural Network (Res-CNN), Bidirectional Long Short-Term Memory (BiLSTM), and Multi-Head Self-Attention (MHSA) mechanisms within the Hierarchical Multi-Task Learning (HMTL) framework. Through a cascaded approach, the RCBA-HMTL architecture effectively degrades high-dimensional spectral redundancy while achieving the synergistic discrimination of contamination types and degrees. The model further introduces Competitive Adaptive Reweighted Sampling (CARS) feature optimization and multi-temporal scale fusion strategies, overcoming the challenges of temporal dynamics. Experimental results demonstrate that on the test set, the model combining CARS feature optimization and the multi-temporal scale strategy achieved classification accuracies (Accuracy) of 0.962, 0.987, 0.985, and 0.981 in identifying the contamination type and the contamination levels of Cadmium (Cd), lead (Pb), and composite contamination, respectively, significantly outperforming traditional machine learning. This provides a highly efficient and reliable new paradigm for the fine-grained characterization and high-precision dynamic monitoring of heavy metal contamination in crops throughout their entire growth cycle.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - A review of machine learning applications in inland and nearshore water color remote sensing: from preprocessing to object detection and parameter estimation
AU  - Shi J.
JO  - Journal of Hydrology
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-09-01
VL  - 677
DO  - 10.1016/j.jhydrol.2026.135950
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jhydrol.2026.135950
AB  - A combination of Machine Learning (ML) techniques with satellite remote sensing has brought target objects and water-quality research into a new era. These emerging techniques and unprecedented opportunities have improved the accuracy of satellite data products, facilitated knowledge discovery, expanded the spatial domain of target objects and water-quality research to the globe, and extended the social, economic, and scientific impacts of water-quality research. This review provides the first systematic integration of machine learning (ML) across the full-chain workflow for inland and nearshore watercolor remote sensing, we examine recent progress in the application of ML across the full remote sensing workflow for inland and nearshore water color studies—from image preprocessing (including atmospheric correction, water body extraction, spectral simulation, spatiotemporal fusion and water extent extraction) to object detection and water quality parameter estimation. We also present a curated selection of valuable open-source resources and highlight key challenges—including data scarcity and label bias, limited model interpretability, insufficient uncertainty quantification, and the difficulties of multimodal data integration, long-term, large-scale, and multi-scale analysis, as well as the need for effective spatial relationship modeling—and discuss emerging trends such as multi-temporal data integration, and the incorporation of physical constraints. By providing a comprehensive overview of ML applications in this field, this review serves as a valuable reference for researchers and practitioners seeking to enhance water quality monitoring and assessment. The insights presented here are expected to foster the development of more accurate, scalable, and interpretable ML models, ultimately contributing to improved environmental management and decision-making.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Flood depth mapping with SWOT-derived training data: evaluating the potential of open-source datasets
AU  - Li Z.
JO  - Journal of Hydrology
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-09-01
VL  - 677
DO  - 10.1016/j.jhydrol.2026.136000
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jhydrol.2026.136000
AB  - Flood depth is a critical parameter for disaster relief and damage assessment, yet pixel-level ground-truth data remain scarce. Traditional approaches, including digital elevation model (DEM) based methods and hydrodynamic simulations, are constrained by data accuracy, cost, and limited generalization. This study proposes machine learning (ML) and deep learning (DL) frameworks for flood depth estimation using only open-source datasets. Surface Water and Ocean Topography (SWOT) satellite observations were used to construct a pixel-level flood depth reference dataset for model training. Multi-channel inputs include DEM, slope, aspect, SAR/optical-derived flood extent, and AlphaEarth data, with channel- and self- attention mechanisms enhancing feature representation. Results from two test datasets show that both ML and DL outperform the baselines. Ablation experiments further confirm the value of attention mechanisms and Squeeze-and-Excitation (SE) in handling high-dimensional features. Validation with in situ measurements from the Haihe River Basin indicates that different flood extent extraction methods affect results, though differences are not statistically significant. The DL framework further demonstrates improved robustness to DEM uncertainties, reducing RMSE by 10–50 % across three test datasets. Overall, the results highlight the potential of combining SWOT with open-source data for flood depth mapping, while noting limitations related to SWOT event availability and DEM accuracy.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - A two-stage data fusion framework for multi-source precipitation estimates integrating gauge, radar, and satellite observations via deep learning and Bayesian model averaging
AU  - Chao L.
JO  - Journal of Hydrology
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-09-01
VL  - 677
DO  - 10.1016/j.jhydrol.2026.135764
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jhydrol.2026.135764
AB  - Reliable precipitation estimation over complex terrain requires the integration of complementary information from rain gauges, weather radar, and satellite products. Effective data fusion remains challenging due to scale inconsistencies, spatial heterogeneity, and source-dependent uncertainties. We propose a transparent two-stage deep-learning–Bayesian multi-source precipitation fusion framework (DBMPF) integrating spatiotemporal deep-learning (DL) correction with probabilistic ensemble calibration. The first stage employs a hybrid 3-dimensional convolutional neural network (3D-CNN) and convolutional long short-term memory (ConvLSTM) architecture to extract station-centered spatiotemporal features from multi-source data cubes and to produce corrected member ensemble members corresponding to different source combinations. The second stage applies Bayesian model averaging (BMA) to merge these members into a calibrated predictive distribution through the expectation–maximization (EM) algorithm. A Box-Cox transformation is introduced to better satisfy Gaussian residual assumptions, facilitating uncertainty quantification through prediction intervals and probabilistic evaluation metrics. The DBMPF framework is assessed in the Tunxi River basin (Anhui, China) during May–September, trained on 2019–2020, and validated in 2022 for validation at daily, hourly, and half-hourly temporal resolutions. Across all scales, DBMPF surpasses both the source products and benchmark approaches. At the daily scale, the correlation coefficient (CC) increases from 0.942 for radar to 0.994,root mean square error (RMSE) decreases from 5.231 to 1.685 mm/day, and Kling-Gupta efficiency (KGE) rises from 0.750 to 0.972, accompanied by negligible bias. At sub-daily resolutions, improvements are observed in event detection performance as measured by critical success index (CSI) and false alarm ratio (FAR), and the framework achieves superior probabilistic performance for 95% prediction intervals, attaining the lowest continuous ranked probability score (CRPS), PICP values closest to the nominal 0.95 level, and the smallest absolute coverage error (ACE) across precipitation intensity categories while maintaining relatively narrow mean prediction interval width (MPIW). A progressive gauge-density experiment involving 18–45 stations reveals substantial performance gains under sparse observational networks and diminishing returns at moderate to high densities, offering guidance for cost-efficient network configuration. The proposed DBMPF framework provides a modular approach for generating high-resolution deterministic and probabilistic precipitation estimates, with particular potential for flood-focused hydrological applications requiring uncertainty-aware precipitation inputs.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Machine learning and AHP approaches for evaluating land degradation in coal mining landscape
AU  - Mishra A.
JO  - Journal of Geochemical Exploration
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-09-01
VL  - 288
DO  - 10.1016/j.gexplo.2026.108117
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.gexplo.2026.108117
AB  - The global surge in demand for thermal power generation has driven rapid growth in coal mining, triggering landscape disturbances that inadvertently lead to vegetation loss, land degradation, ecosystem destabilization, habitat loss, climate anomalies, and an alarming threat to food security and livelihoods. Effective monitoring of coal mining sites and the extent of their expansion is essential to quantify the nature and scope of damage to native ecosystems, assess vulnerability, and develop strategies to prioritize future restoration efforts. Therefore, we investigated to evaluate the impact of coal mining on land cover destruction using Sentinel-2 data, employing machine learning (ML) algorithms and assessing degradation vulnerability through the Analytical Hierarchy Process (AHP) model. The findings reveal that the Support Vector Machine (SVM) outperformed the Random Forest (RF) and Gaussian Maximum Likelihood Classifier (MLC) algorithms, which were found to be suitable in characterizing land cover and vegetation. Over the past thirty years, coal mining has proliferated in forest and agricultural lands, leading to varying levels of degradation. AHP identified strong correlations among soil bulk density, organic carbon, the Normalized Difference Vegetation Index, and the Normalized Difference Moisture Index with degradation patterns; hence, these variables received higher weights in the analysis. The Land Degradation Vulnerability Index map, derived by integrating AHP variables within a Geographic Information System (GIS), illustrates the highest vulnerability in mined areas, followed by barren areas, agricultural lands, and forests. The study highlighted the effectiveness of the ML algorithm in precise land cover classification and the robustness of AHP techniques in analysing degradation. It further explores the implications of successful ecological restoration strategies that foster resilient ecosystems, aiming to reverse land degradation in coal-mined areas in alignment with the Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs) for 2030.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - MiMNet-STASF: a spatiotemporal decoupled network for sub-pixel canopy structure retrieval of urban forests with multi-source long-term remote sensing time series
AU  - Qin J.
JO  - ISPRS Journal of Photogrammetry and Remote Sensing
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-09-01
VL  - 239
SP  - 309-333
DO  - 10.1016/j.isprsjprs.2026.06.013
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.isprsjprs.2026.06.013
AB  - Forest canopy height (FCH) and vertical structural composition are essential for urban ecological assessment and precision forest management, yet reliable retrieval in fragmented urban–rural transition landscapes remains challenging due to multi-source sensor heterogeneity, seasonal dynamics, and mixed canopy conditions within 10 m pixels. We propose MiMNet-STASF, a spatiotemporal fusion framework for grid-level canopy-structure inversion from 24-month Sentinel-1/2 time series. The framework integrates a nested Mamba architecture (Inner VMamba for multi-scale spatial encoding and Outer bidirectional Mamba for long-range temporal dependency modeling), an adaptive Spatiotemporal Arbiter Gated Fusion (STASF) module (SG/TG/STAG), and a physics-informed hierarchical regression head that jointly estimates FCH and vertical structural fractions (<10 m, 10–20 m, and ≥20 m). Using UAV LiDAR-derived supervision, validated in Xiong’an New Area against 13 deep learning models spanning four architectural paradigms (RNNs, CNNs, Transformers, SSMs), MiMNet-STASF outperforms all comparatives, achieving an R2 of 0.8963 and an RMSE of 1.0903 m for FCH retrieval. Ablation results indicate that the nested Mamba and STASF components provide clearer gains on structurally imbalanced targets. From a data perspective, Sentinel-1 provides enhanced structural sensitivity, Sentinel-2 offers stable spectral and phenological context, and vegetation indices (VI) further improve vegetation contrast; their fusion enhances prediction consistency across canopy-height gradients and vertical fractional components. Moreover, MiMNet-STASF retains robust structural representations under seasonal vegetation variation. Together with cross-regional GEDI-referenced evaluation, these results support MiMNet-STASF as an effective and scalable solution for management-oriented, fine-grained urban forest canopy-structure mapping. The implementation of the proposed method is publicly available at https://github.com/qinjs123/MiMNet-STASF.
ER  -

