TI  - Short-term ionospheric modeling over the Indian region using long short-term memory networks
AU  - Prajapati P.
JO  - Advances in Space Research
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-09-01
VL  - 78
IS  - 5
SP  - 4972-4982
DO  - 10.1016/j.asr.2026.06.081
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.asr.2026.06.081
AB  - Precision in regional short-term ionospheric modeling is essential for the reliability of satellite navigation, trans-ionospheric communication, and space weather forecasting. Due to the highly non-linear variability driven by solar activity, traditional empirical and statistical models often struggle to maintain accuracy during localized perturbations. This study develops a predictive framework using Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) networks to simulate short-term ionospheric dynamics across the Indian region. The model utilizes high-resolution observational data from the Meteorological and Oceanographic Satellite Data Archival Centre (MOSDAC) of the Indian Space Research Organisation (ISRO). Focused on Total Electron Content (TEC) derived from ionospheric delay and frequency measurements, the LSTM architecture was designed to capture complex temporal dependencies within the diurnal cycle. Performance was rigorously evaluated against multiple baselines, including the International Reference Ionosphere (IRI-2016) and simple RNN models. The proposed LSTM model achieved a Root Mean Square Error (RMSE) of 2.10 TECU and a Mean Squared Error (MSE) of 4.41, demonstrating significantly higher accuracy and a stronger correlation (R2 = 0.95) compared to traditional empirical methods. These results establish the LSTM framework as an effective tool for short-term regional modeling and provide a scalable foundation for future data-driven research encompassing broader geomagnetic conditions and expanded spatial scales.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Hemispheric-scale mapping of thaw slumps using a cloud-native and transferable deep learning framework
AU  - Lou P.
JO  - ISPRS Journal of Photogrammetry and Remote Sensing
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-09-01
VL  - 239
SP  - 105-123
DO  - 10.1016/j.isprsjprs.2026.06.002
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.isprsjprs.2026.06.002
AB  - Accelerating permafrost degradation is driving the rapid expansion of thaw slumps (TS) across the Northern Hemisphere, posing increasing risks to ecosystems, infrastructure, and the global carbon cycle. However, reliable TS identification at continental to hemispheric scales remains challenging due to strong environmental heterogeneity, fragmented remote sensing workflows, and limited cross-regional transferability of existing methods. Here, we address the cross-regional transferability gap in hemispheric TS identification by developing a reproducible framework that couples cloud-native multi-source data fusion in Google Earth Engine (GEE) with a modular PyTorch-based deep learning pipeline. The framework operationalizes transferability through three coordinated components: (1) a physically interpretable channel design that integrates Sentinel-1, Sentinel-2, and topographic data to encode TS-relevant processes; (2) a structure-preserving feature representation using PCA-guided dimensionality reduction and SNIC-based superpixel segmentation to suppress noise while maintaining geomorphic consistency; and (3) a standardized cross-regional training and ablation strategy that identifies model configurations maximizing generalization rather than region-specific accuracy. Evaluations across the Qinghai-Tibet Plateau (QTP), the Arctic, and the broader Northern Hemisphere demonstrate that late-summer observations and fused optical–SAR channels consistently outperform single-sensor inputs. The LinkNet-EfficientNet-B7 configuration achieves intersection-over-union (IOU) scores of 0.802 in the QTP, 0.770 in the Arctic, and 0.776 at hemispheric scale. Cross-regional transfer experiments further confirm robust generalization, with the hemispheric model maintaining IOU values exceeding 0.63 across distinct permafrost domains without region-specific retraining. These results indicate that transferable TS mapping is not primarily determined by model complexity, but by the consistency between process-informed feature representation and cross-regional training design. The proposed GEE-PyTorch framework provides a computationally efficient and reproducible foundation for operational monitoring of thaw-induced geomorphic disturbances.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Assessing deep learning architectures for land cover and crop type mapping over complex regions using small spatial contexts
AU  - Naboureh A.
JO  - Advances in Space Research
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-09-01
VL  - 78
IS  - 5
SP  - 4519-4528
DO  - 10.1016/j.asr.2026.06.076
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.asr.2026.06.076
AB  - Deep learning (DL) has become the foundation of contemporary Land Cover and Crop Type (LCCT) classification practices, yet its performance largely depends on the availability of large spatial patches (e.g., 512 × 512 or 256 × 256 pixels) that offer rich contextual information. However, in practice, obtaining such large spatial contexts in topographically complex regions is often challenging, as it is both costly and labor-intensive. At the same time, valuable freely available pixel-point reference data, collected through field surveys or expert interpretation, remain underutilized to train modern DL models because they lack rich contextual information. To address this gap, this study employs a simple yet effective method that centers small image patches on reference sample pixels, producing 9 × 9 pixel inputs. We then, utilizing Sentinel-1 and Sentinel-2 data as input data, systematically evaluated the performance of five widely used DL architectures in LCCT classification (U-Net, U-Net++, Res-U-Net++, DeepLabV3+, and DCNN) when trained on these very small patches across a topographically complex region. Our assessment showed that, across the 14 LCCT classes, U-Net++ achieved the highest classification performance (75.45% overall accuracy), followed by DeepLabV3+ (70.86%), while DCNN produced the lowest accuracy (62.12%). We found that models with nested skip connections and enhanced feature reuse exhibited greater robustness to the loss of spatial context and better extraction of fine-scale patterns. This research discusses the limits and potential of DL architectures under small patch-size constraints and offers practical guidance for data-efficient LCCT mapping in data-limited or heterogeneous regions.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - GICL-Net: A graph-interactive collaborative learning network for joint classification of HSI and LiDAR
AU  - Zhu H.
JO  - International Journal of Applied Earth Observation and Geoinformation
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-09-01
VL  - 153
DO  - 10.1016/j.jag.2026.105525
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jag.2026.105525
AB  - Hyperspectral and LiDAR images, as a class of multi-source remote sensing data, have their own advantageous features. HSI contains rich spectral information, compared to LiDAR, which is more capable of reflecting the height information of the target area. However, determining the intrinsic correlation characteristics between HSI channels and fully exploiting the complementary characteristics of HSI and LiDAR to improve classification accuracy remains a challenging issue. In this paper, we propose a graph-interactive collaborative learning network (GICL-Net) for joint classification of HSI and LiDAR, which consists of a multi-scale spectral-spatial graph feature extractor (MSGE) and a cross-modality adjacency matrix fusion (CAMF) module. Specifically, MSGE is divided into two branches. One branch starts from the spectral dimension, defining each channel of HSI as a node to construct a graph network and mine potential correlations at the channel level, thereby capturing the intrinsic spectral correlation features of the target region. The other branch focuses on the height information of the target area, constructing a graph network at the geometric spatial level to extract representative height features. The CAMF module leverages the fused adjacency matrix to establish a cross-modal graph interaction mechanism, enabling the spectral features and the spatial features to achieve deep synergy through iterative updates in graph convolution. This generates cross-modal fusion features with fine spectral discrimination capability and spatial dimensional structural integrity. This method has been validated for effectiveness across multiple HSI and LiDAR datasets. In particular, on the Houston2013 dataset, the overall accuracy (OA) reached 98.91%, with a Kappa coefficient of 0.9882. Furthermore, compared with the baseline network (i.e., the conventional two-branch graph network, the overall accuracy improved by 6.58%. Our code is available at the link: https://github.com/Xidian-AIGroup190726/GICLNet.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - STaPL: Scale Transfer with Pseudo-Labelling for satellite-based mapping of agricultural practices
AU  - Ma Y.
JO  - Remote Sensing of Environment
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-09-01
VL  - 343
DO  - 10.1016/j.rse.2026.115500
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.rse.2026.115500
AB  - Improved data on conservation practices in agriculture would help to boost understanding of their impacts on crop productivity, environmental outcomes, and climate resilience. Despite ongoing adoption of several practices in many regions, public information is typically limited to very coarse estimates of practice prevalence at a frequency of once or twice per decade. Remote sensing has shown potential value for mapping many practices, but existing methods have relied on calibration with field-scale practice data which are unavailable in most locations and years. In this study, we present a Scale Transfer with Pseudo-Labelling (STaPL) framework for mapping agricultural practices without field-scale data. Using conservation tillage as a case study, we combine Landsat earth observations, elevation data, and county-level tillage census data. We first generate pseudo-labels for tillage types in each county and year using publicly available county-level tillage statistics. These labels are then iteratively updated using predictions from a deep multilayer perceptron (MLP) model. The final pseudo-labels are used to train a random forest classifier for tillage mapping. Comparison of STaPL predictions with a ground-based tillage dataset that spans eight years and more than 25,000 field-level observations demonstrates that STaPL has comparable overall performance (Accuracy = 75%) and stronger temporal generalizability than reference models trained using field-level tillage data, although some temporal variability is still observed in STaPL-based models. Class-wise evaluation shows that STaPL performs better on capturing low-intensity tillage class (F1 = 80.29.%), while underperforming the field-supervised baseline by about 3% on the high-intensity class (F1 = 65.82%). Using the STaPL framework, we generate a dataset of annual 30-m tillage maps for the twelve U.S. Midwestern states in 2001–2023. These maps exhibit strong agreement with official statistics on tillage practices at both the county (r2 = 0.65) and state level (r2 = 0.83). The STaPL tillage maps provide a useful proxy for studying conservation tillage adoption in the U.S. and the STaPL framework can be readily extended to other agricultural practices and regions to support decision making in agroecosystems. The 30-m STaPL tillage maps are available publicly at https://github.com/yuchima8/STaPL.
