TY  - JOUR
TI  - Precise waterbody mapping at the city scale using a novel connectivity preservation and boundary refinement network based on medium-resolution remote sensing imagery
AU  - Zhai H.
JO  - Journal of Hydrology
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-08-01
VL  - 676
DO  - 10.1016/j.jhydrol.2026.135669
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jhydrol.2026.135669
AB  - Waterbody mapping has always been a research hotspot, with utmost significance in water resource management. Although deep learning approaches have achieved remarkable accomplishments, most of them are designed for high-resolution images, with limited adaptability to the city scale, and suffered from serious misjudgments, frequent breakages, and rough morphologies in complex scenarios. To overcome these obstacles, this paper proposes an innovative connectivity preservation and boundary refinement network (CB-Net), to precisely delineate waterbodies at the city scale using medium-resolution remote sensing images, which explicitly focuses on geometrical characteristic mining of waterbodies. Specifically, with regard to frequent breakages and rough morphologies, an effective dense dynamic snake convolution module is developed, to deeply learn felicitous geometrical features for various waterbodies and aggregate hierarchical expressions to preserve connectivity, which enables continuous and detailed waterbody extraction. In addition, to improve boundary localization and morphology expressivity, a valid high-frequency multiscale feature fusion module is designed as skip connections, to learn multidirectional high-frequency information and leverage multiscale directional expressions to generate structure-and-position-aware features, thus facilitating fine-grained segmentations. A challenging waterbody dataset based on Sentinel-2 images (S2-Water) is constructed for performance validation, which compensates for the scarcity of specialized datasets and advances future researches. Extensive experimental results illustrate that CB-Net significantly outperformed the other state-of-the-art alternatives to yield promising results for Wuhan City and Jinan City, indicating its good adaptability and generalizability to various waterbodies in complex scenarios. Generally, CB-Net achieved a mean F1-sore of 88.69% and IoU (Intersection over union) of 79.79%, which well satisfies applicational requirements.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Daily inference of reservoir surface water area at large scales via a systematic deep learning framework synergistically integrating multisource remote sensing imagery
AU  - Feng Y.
JO  - Journal of Hydrology
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-08-01
VL  - 676
DO  - 10.1016/j.jhydrol.2026.135712
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jhydrol.2026.135712
AB  - Reservoir surface water area (SWA) serves as a pivotal indicator for characterizing storage changes and water balance, with important implications for assessing the effects of anthropogenic regulation and protecting aquatic ecosystems. Although satellite observations offer indispensable data support for reservoir SWA monitoring, the trade-off between spatial and temporal resolutions in single-source satellite datasets severely impedes the comprehensive quantification of rapid SWA dynamics. To address this challenge, we develop a systematic deep learning framework for daily SWA inference that synergistically combines cloud-free optical image reconstruction and surface water extraction. The reconstruction module generates daily cloud-free Landsat-like optical images by fusing heterogeneous data from Sentinel-1, MODIS, and Landsat-derived NDVI via a multi-branch feature fusion mechanism. Using the reconstructed imagery as input, the surface water extraction module accurately delineates reservoir SWA. Comparative experiments demonstrate that the proposed framework achieves robust performance in cloud-free image reconstruction (RMSE = 0.021, SSIM = 0.737) and high accuracy in water extraction, with both user’s and producer’s accuracies exceeding 0.970. The framework is applied to 288 large reservoirs across the Yangtze River Basin, and its scalability for large-scale inference is further validated using in situ water elevation measurements from 77 reservoirs. Overall, this framework provides a robust and scalable solution for daily large-scale reservoir SWA inference, laying a solid foundation for hydrological analysis, optimal reservoir operation, and sustainable water resource management.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Exploiting foundational spatial semantic prior for accurate few-shot hyperspectral image classification
AU  - Zhao X.
JO  - Expert Systems with Applications
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-08-01
VL  - 322
DO  - 10.1016/j.eswa.2026.132252
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.eswa.2026.132252
AB  - Extensive deep neural networks with various architectures have been developed for hyperspectral image (HSI) classification, i.e., identifying class category of each pixel based on their spatial-spectral characteristics. However, due to lack of a large-scaled annotated HSI dataset (e.g., ImageNet) for sufficient pre-training, these networks struggle to comprehensively exploit the complicated spatial semantic knowledge (e.g., context, global and local similarity etc.) of input HSI and thus shows limited generalization capacity, especially in the challenging few-shot scenarios. To mitigate this problem, we propose a novel spatial semantic knowledge transfer framework (SSPF), which attempts to borrow the powerful representation capacity of foundation models (e.g., SAM, DINO and CLIP etc.) in terms of capturing the complicated spatial semantic knowledge in natural images to enhance the feature representation of HSI during few-shot learning. In a specific, the framework comprises of a patch-free HSI classification (HSIC) network and a spatial semantic knowledge transfer network. The former utilizes a U-shape network to predict the pixel-wise class category without patch sampling, while the latter first learns to pixel-wise dynamically compress the input HSI into a three-band pseudo-color image and feeds it into the following foundation model to extract the spatial semantic knowledge. Such a pixel-wise image compression scheme enables efficiently collecting the useful spatial semantic knowledge distributed across different spectral bands into the optimal three-band image so that it is suitable for the foundation model for better spatial semantic knowledge extraction. Moreover, the extracted semantic knowledge is enhanced by channel-wise attention and integrated into the feature representation of the input HSI for classification in a coarse-to-fine manner. By doing these, the spatial semantic knowledge extracted by the foundation models pretrained on large-scale nature images can be appropriately adapted and injected into the cross-domain HSI, thus enhancing the generalization performance in few-shot learning. Experiments on six benchmark HSI datasets demonstrate the superiority of the proposed method over existing state-of-the-art baselines in terms of few-shot classification. The code will be available at https://github.com/zhaoxb2025/SSPF-main.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - A novel deep learning semi-observational analog framework for diagnosing convection over Morocco using satellite imagery and ERA5-reanalysis
AU  - Alaoui B.
JO  - Atmospheric Research
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-08-01
VL  - 338
DO  - 10.1016/j.atmosres.2026.108969
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.atmosres.2026.108969
AB  - This study investigates a new semi-observational analog approach (AnOb) for diagnosing convection related parameters. The new developed approach inherits from traditional analog forecasting but leverages deep learning computer vision algorithms for assessing weather state's similarity. The system integrates high-resolution RGB convection satellite imagery and ERA5 reanalysis data to enhance the characterization of convective processes. Three pre-trained DL architectures—VGG16, Xception, and Inception-ResNetV2—are fine-tuned and employed to extract salient convective features from satellite imagery. The model was trained over a four-year period (2020−2023) for lead times of 12, 15, and 18 h, with 2024 reserved for testing, covering a study region spanning 40°N–20°N and 20°E–4°W. The set of parameters includes Convective Available Potential Energy (CAPE), Total Column Water Vapor (TCWV), Vertical Velocity (W600), and Lifted Index (LI). Hence for a zero‑lead-time t, the DL algorithms are trained to create feature vectors for current and all past images. The similarity between the feature vectors is computed using cosine distance and the dates of the most similar past satellite images are selected. The ERA5-Reanalysis of the best analogs mean is then used as diagnosis of the convection parameters. This method mitigates some limitations in conventional analog systems, such as being mostly a point-wise approaches. To assess the system performance a two steps verification process has been achieved. First the mean hourly bias over the full test period 2024 is computed, then the mean bias is evaluated over a sample of 100 events equally distributed between 50 extreme convection events and 50 no convection events of 2024. For both full test period and extreme convection sample, the system exhibits enhanced performances compared to climatology and persistence, with hourly mean biases spanning between −200 J/kg and 200 J/Kg for CAPE, 0 °C to 1 °C for LI, and − 0.3 Pa/s to 0.3 Pa/s for W at 600 hPa. To assess the capability of the system to detect convection, a fuzzy logic was implemented based on operational thresholds. The system displays a Probability of Detection (POD) of 0.6, a False Alarm Ratio (FAR) of 0.4, a Brier Score (BS) of 0.4, and a Continuous Ranked Probability Score (CRPS) of 0.4. These metrics indicate a promising potential to detect severe convective events. This innovative AnOb system offers a cost-effective, high-performance complementary tool to traditional forecasting models, with significant potential for operational meteorological applications.
