TY  - JOUR
TI  - A novel hybrid knowledge distillation model for high-resolution image semantic segmentation
AU  - Liu B.
JO  - Advances in Space Research
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-08-01
VL  - 78
IS  - 3
SP  - 2426-2441
DO  - 10.1016/j.asr.2026.05.066
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.asr.2026.05.066
AB  - As deep learning technology continues to evolve, deep neural networks have achieved significant breakthroughs in the domain of semantic segmentation of remote sensing images (RSI). However, the deepening of network architectures inevitably leads to a substantial increase in the consumption of computational resources, which is highly detrimental in scenarios where resources are limited. To tackle this issue, this paper presents a novel knowledge distillation method based on features and pixels (FPKD), which aims to improve the real-time performance of model prediction while maintaining a high level of segmentation effect. Initially, a triplet probability map distillation (TPMD) module is proposed. This model can effectively guide the student network to focus on the key processing areas of the teacher network, and significantly improve the learning efficiency of the student network by simulating the probability distribution map of the teacher network. Subsequently, a similarity distillation (SD) module is presented, enabling the student network to assimilate the interactions and connections between pixels. Finally, in order to better adjust the bias and variance in the learning process, an adaptive loss function is designed to optimize the training process by dynamically adjusting the loss weight, thereby enhancing the model’s generalization capabilities. Extensive experiments were conducted on the Potsdam, Vaihingen, and LoveDA datasets. Experimental results show that compared with the current leading baseline model, the proposed method demonstrates better performance on multiple key performance indicators, fully verifying the efficacy and practicability of the proposed approach.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Navigating Hierarchies in Hyperbolic Space: A Knowledge-Driven Graph Network for Semantic Segmentation of Remote Sensing Imagery
AU  - Cui W.
JO  - International Journal of Applied Earth Observation and Geoinformation
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-08-01
VL  - 152
DO  - 10.1016/j.jag.2026.105435
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jag.2026.105435
AB  - Semantic segmentation of multi-scale remote sensing objects faces two key challenges: representing complex relationships and effectively integrating geographic knowledge. These relationships generate hierarchical patterns embedded with eigen properties, forming a nested system that exhibits exponential growth, a characteristic poorly captured by Euclidean-based deep learning models limited to polynomial expansion. Hyperbolic space offers a geometrically faithful alternative, but current hyperbolic neural networks rely on tangent space projections for computation, introducing significant approximation errors that degrade network performance. To address these challenges, we introduce a knowledge-driven framework founded on fully hyperbolic neural network operators. These operators are designed to be manifold-preserving, enabling all computations to be performed directly within hyperbolic space and thereby eliminating tangent space approximation errors. Building on this foundation, we propose a novel Fully Hyperbolic Graph Attention Network (FHGAT) that operates on a multi-relational heterogeneous graph representation of remote sensing objects. Our model effectively mines and integrates embedded knowledge from complex, multi-scale relationships in a bottom-up fashion. Extensive experiments validate its superiority; compared to a standard Euclidean baseline, our model increases object-level accuracy and Mean Intersection over Union (MIoU) by 7.2% and 7.6%, respectively. Notably, it also surpasses a state-of-the-art tangent-space approximation model by 5.4% in accuracy, confirming the benefits of its end-to-end hyperbolic design for resolving spectral confusion. This research marks a significant step in advancing remote sensing analysis from a data-driven to a more robust and interpretable knowledge-driven paradigm.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Improved causal attention mechanism and semi-supervised learning integrated deep learning model for blueberry ripeness detection
AU  - Zhang R.
JO  - Computers and Electronics in Agriculture
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-08-01
VL  - 250
DO  - 10.1016/j.compag.2026.111910
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.compag.2026.111910
AB  - The high nutritional value of blueberry has driven production scale expansion, with efficient harvesting and precision management becoming key for industrial upgrading—blueberry ripeness grading is its core link. Mainstream fruit ripeness detection methods use ordinary or hyperspectral images as data foundations, relying on machine learning-based computer vision. However, they have two critical limitations: only focusing on data correlation while overlooking causality, failing to unearth the intrinsic links between fruit growth and ripeness and thus resulting in misjudgments in complex scenarios; and poor cost and deployment adaptability, relying on large-scale manual annotations and high equipment costs, which cannot meet low-cost portable field needs. To address these, this study takes YOLO11 as the baseline and proposes YOLO11-WBO, a semi-supervised lightweight model for blueberry ripeness grading. Structurally, it integrates three customized modules: C2PSA_BCA includes causal attention and blueberry-specific features to enhance key information representation; C3k2_WT adopts wavelet convolution for accurate multi-scale feature extraction; C3k2_OREPA uses “multi-branch optimization during training and single convolution deployment during inference” to improve feature efficiency. A semi-supervised learning framework reduces reliance on large annotated data. Experiments on a blueberry dataset show YOLO11-WBO outperforms the original YOLO11n by 3.3 % in Precision and 4.6 % in mAP@50:95, maintains 8.33 ms per-image inference speed, and cuts annotation effort by 63 %. This work provides a low-annotation, low-cost, high-robustness solution for blueberry ripeness grading, with significant practical value and broad promotion prospects.
ER  -

