TI  - Assessment of vulnerability to mitigate risks in aquaculture areas across 28 coastal provinces of Vietnam: facilitating the selection of optimal farming locations
AU  - Le T.Q.
JO  - Advances in Space Research
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-08-01
VL  - 78
IS  - 3
SP  - 2312-2338
DO  - 10.1016/j.asr.2026.05.057
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.asr.2026.05.057
AB  - Aquaculture is integral to both food security and the economic framework of Vietnam; however, the sustainability of this sector is increasingly jeopardized by environmental degradation and climate change, necessitating the implementation of sophisticated monitoring and risk assessment methodologies. This research utilizes Sentinel-2 satellite imagery, Artificial Neural Network (ANN) models, and a hybrid Genetic Algorithm-Extreme Gradient Boosting (GA-XGBoost) model to delineate aquaculture zones and evaluate environmental susceptibility across four significant socio-economic regions. The classification outcomes indicated that the nationwide aquaculture area totals 1,204,600 ha, with the Mekong River Delta (MRD) accounting for a substantial 77.44%, predominantly attributed to intensive shrimp aquaculture practices. Nonetheless, the MRD has also been identified as highly vulnerable in its aquaculture zones, primarily due to the compounded effects of saline intrusion during dry periods coupled with abrupt environmental shifts in the rainy season. In contrast, the North Central and Central Coastal (NCCC) region displayed a consistent vulnerability pattern along waterways, influenced by flooding and storm activities. Notably, vulnerability hotspots in the Red River Delta (RRD) and South East (SE) regions were concentrated around major coastal urban and industrial hubs, largely driven by wastewater contamination and urban heat island phenomena. The GA-XGBoost model demonstrated remarkable accuracy (R2train > 0.83) in forecasting vulnerability in aquaculture areas by incorporating both natural and anthropogenic variables and pinpointed optimal aquaculture sites, including the freshwater-brackish transition zones in Tien Giang and Tra Vinh, as well as the stable lagoon areas in Thua Thien Hue, which maintain consistent environmental conditions throughout the year. These insights offer a vital scientific basis for the strategic planning of sustainable aquaculture zones, fostering aquaculture practices that are both effective and resilient in the face of escalating environmental challenges.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Evaluating deep learning model for multispectral feature mapping of fecal coliform contamination in major rivers of South Korea
AU  - Suh S.M.
JO  - Remote Sensing of Environment
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-08-01
VL  - 341
DO  - 10.1016/j.rse.2026.115458
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.rse.2026.115458
AB  - Fecal coliform bacteria are key microbial indicators of water quality and public health risk, yet conventional monitoring methods relying on site-specific sampling and laboratory analysis are spatially limited and lack real-time responsiveness. This study develops a novel framework for large-scale estimation of fecal coliform concentrations by integrating Sentinel-2 multispectral imagery with a convolutional neural network (CNN) model across South Korea's four major river basins: Han, Nakdong, Geum, and Yeongsan. To enhance spectral sensitivity to microbial pollution, backscattering albedo (uT) was derived from reflectance data and incorporated alongside first- and second-order spectral differentiation. A ResNet-18-based CNN model was trained using Sentinel-2 reflectance, backscattering albedo, first and second differentiation combined dataset and in-situ fecal coliform data from 2017 to 2022, achieving performance with R2 values of 0.922 and 0.566 for training and validation datasets, respectively. The model captured low-concentration patterns more consistently, while prediction errors increased in moderate-to-high concentration ranges. Spatial prediction maps revealed contamination hotspots in urban and agricultural zones, particularly downstream of tributary confluences and near regulated flow structures. Model uncertainty was quantified using Maximum Likelihood Estimation, and SHapley Additive exPlanations (SHAP) analysis identified near-infrared and shortwave-infrared backscattering bands as the most influential features, providing a transparent interpretation of the model's behavior. This remote sensing-based approach enables robust, scalable, and explainable estimation of fecal coliform distributions over broad geographic areas, surpassing the spatial and temporal limitations of traditional field-based monitoring. This study presents the large-scale fecal coliform estimation model that directly incorporates backscattering albedo and spectral derivatives, offering a novel remote sensing-based solution that captures microbial contamination dynamics with unprecedented spatial precision and model interpretability.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Beyond single and earthbound: Exploring multi-image grounding in remote sensing with Large Vision-Language Models
AU  - An X.
JO  - ISPRS Journal of Photogrammetry and Remote Sensing
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-08-01
VL  - 238
SP  - 589-605
DO  - 10.1016/j.isprsjprs.2026.05.023
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.isprsjprs.2026.05.023
AB  - The rapid advancement of Large Vision-Language Models (LVLMs) has showcased impressive perception and reasoning capabilities on visual-linguistic content. However, their full potential remains largely confined to an earthbound perspective, leading to unsatisfactory performance when confronted with fine-grained remote sensing image (RSI) interpretation due to the inherent domain gap. Although recent research has been dedicated to fine-tuning LVLMs with domain-specific datasets, yielding notable improvement in tasks such as visual grounding, the restriction of supporting only single-image input or the limited diversity of task categories continues to impede their practicality in remote sensing applications. To address these challenges, we propose to explore and advance the multi-image grounding (MIG) capability of LVLMs from a bird’s-eye view, enabling more robust support for tasks such as change detection, object tracking, referring expression comprehension, and cross-view retrieval within the field of remote sensing. Therefore, we introduce MIGRANT, the first LVLM tailored for diverse remote sensing tasks requiring MIG to enable fine-grained interpretation across RSIs. To achieve this, we employ a two-stage training paradigm on a hierarchically constructed instruction-following dataset called MIG-RS-Instruct, which consists of two primary categories: Implicit Grounding and Explicit Grounding, and is divided into five leaf tasks in total. Given the absence of an established remote sensing MIG evaluation mechanism, we also propose the first benchmark, MIG-RS-Bench, consisting of 4000 cases. Extensive experiments demonstrate that our MIGRANT achieves the best performance across all tasks in MIG-RS-Bench, and the additional fine-tuning on MIG further enhances the visual grounding capability and generalizability of the model, yielding approximately 15% improvement on mainstream single-image grounding benchmarks. We expect MIGRANT to serve as a valuable resource and provide insights for the future development of remote sensing MIG. Code and dataset are available at this https URL.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Forward temporal forecasting of land-cover transitions from multispectral observations in mining-influenced zones of the Pra River Basin, Ghana: A spatial panel learning approach
AU  - Essel-Yorke K.A.
JO  - Remote Sensing Applications Society and Environment
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-08-01
VL  - 43
DO  - 10.1016/j.rsase.2026.102240
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.rsase.2026.102240
AB  - Mining expansion across Ghana’s Pra River Basin has resulted in rapid and heterogeneous landscape transformation, creating an increasing need for methods capable of anticipating land-cover transitions rather than merely documenting changes after they occur. However, short-term forecasting remains constrained by spatially inconsistent observations, limited integration of predictive uncertainty, and insufficiently interpretable modelling approaches. To address these challenges, this study develops a forward temporal modelling framework that integrates spatial panel alignment, spectral interaction analysis, and probabilistic machine learning for land-cover transition forecasting in mining-influenced zones of the basin. Grid aggregation of Landsat 8 imagery acquired between 2022 and 2024 yielded a spatially consistent panel comprising 2100 temporally matched grid cells. The spectral structure of the land-cover classes was subsequently examined using Pearson correlation matrices and Graphical Lasso partial-correlation networks, providing a basis for assessing the stability of spectral relationships across classes and observation periods. Building on this representation, a Random Forest classifier was used to predict subsequent land-cover states from spectral features and prior class information. Strict chronological validation, with training on the 2022–2023 transition and testing on the independent 2023–2024 transition, provided an assessment of forward forecasting performance under temporally separated conditions. The model achieved a mean prediction confidence of 0.892 for 2024, while entropy-based uncertainty analysis indicated low ambiguity for most predictions (mean entropy = 0.176), with only 1.8% of observations exceeding the high-uncertainty threshold. Shapley Additive Explanations analysis identified vegetation-sensitive indices and shortwave infrared bands as influential predictors, while spectral interaction analysis revealed persistent coupling between Band 6 and Band 7 in mining areas. Applying the model to the subsequent 2025 period resulted in a moderate increase in predictive entropy (mean = 0.218; high-uncertainty proportion = 3.61%), indicating greater uncertainty in the one-year-ahead forecast. The findings demonstrate that a spatially consistent temporal representation of land-cover observations can support reliable short-term transition forecasting while preserving information on prediction confidence and feature contributions. By coupling chronological validation with interpretable and uncertainty-aware predictions, the framework provides a more informative basis for distinguishing confidently forecast transitions from areas requiring further investigation. This capability strengthens the use of medium-resolution satellite observations for anticipatory land-cover assessment and offers a practical basis for prioritising environmental monitoring in mining-influenced tropical landscapes.
ER  -

