TI  - xLSTM for multi-source cotton yield estimation and temporal interpretability across agro-ecological regions in Türkiye
AU  - Yardımcı F.
JO  - Advances in Space Research
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-08-01
VL  - 78
IS  - 3
SP  - 2139-2153
DO  - 10.1016/j.asr.2026.05.025
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.asr.2026.05.025
AB  - The increasing availability of complementary Earth Observation (EO) data, particularly from spaceborne optical and SAR sensors such as Sentinel-1 and Sentinel-2, has made crop yield estimation more data-driven. However, the relationship between EO features and yield can vary significantly throughout the crop growth cycle, depending on geographical, physiological, and agronomic conditions. Robust deep learning (DL) models for yield estimation must achieve not only high accuracy, but also temporally coherent relationships among features and modalities. This study addresses these challenges using extended Long Short-Term Memory (xLSTM), which extends and stabilizes traditional LSTM, a widely accepted approach to handling long time-series data in yield estimation, by improving memory retention and gradient flow over time steps. Using a multivariate time-series (MTS) dataset consisting of spaceborne EO data (Sentinel-1 SAR and Sentinel-2 optical), satellite-informed reanalysis variables (ERA5-Land), and soil data (SoilGrids), for cotton yield estimation in Türkiye, encompassing diverse environmental conditions and agricultural practices, xLSTM is compared with standard LSTM, Bidirectional LSTM (BiLSTM), and the Transformer-based Informer model. The results show that xLSTM not only achieves comparable predictive performance but also provides highly consistent temporal importance profiles across diverse sub-regions with varying agricultural practices and climatic conditions. It demonstrates strong temporal coherence and broad alignment with key agronomic stages, particularly highlighting late summer as a critical period for cotton yield. In contrast, LSTM, BiLSTM, and Informer models exhibit less consistent temporal importance patterns. This work highlights the potential of xLSTM for robust and interpretable crop yield estimation across diverse agroecological contexts. By leveraging MTS, the proposed approach supports evaluation of model robustness across heterogeneous agro-ecological settings within the study area, offering a basis for data-driven agricultural decision-making. The study contributes to the development of scalable methods for crop yield estimation based on satellite EO data, supporting regional agricultural monitoring from space. The availability of both the dataset and source code aims to support reproducibility and foster further research in yield estimation. All resources can be accessed at:https://github.com/Feanor1021/Cotton-Yield-Forecast-2025.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Multi-source remote sensing fusion for European tree species classification: Explore the role of GEE-derived indices by explainable AI
AU  - Ganjirad M.
JO  - Advances in Space Research
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-08-01
VL  - 78
IS  - 3
SP  - 2442-2468
DO  - 10.1016/j.asr.2026.05.067
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.asr.2026.05.067
AB  - This research investigates the role of spectral, polarimetric and vertical structure indices derived from freely available satellite data by utilizing ensemble machine learning and Explainable AI (XAI) techniques. Accordingly, by employing Google Earth Engine, the effective indices were extracted for warm and cold seasons from Sentinel-1, Sentinel-2, and the global GEDI-derived Canopy Height Model (CHM). Then various ensemble tree based models including Random Forest, Extra Trees, AdaBoost, XGBoost and LightGBM were employed to classify European tree species at various levels (L0 to L3) based on the collected dataset. The result demonstrated that LightGBM obtained the best performance across all four levels. At the higher levels (L2 and L3), the classification become more complex by increasing the number of tree species and intra-class variation. However, the model could successfully identify 20 tree species at the L3 level with a satisfactory F1-score of 67.2%, using only free satellite data and sensor fusion techniques. By adding spectral and vertical structure indices to polarimetric indices, the F1-score at the L2 level improved by 26.36%. This study also investigated the effects of seasonal variations on classification. The results indicated that evergreen tree species, due to their higher tolerance thresholds and stable vegetation characteristics, exhibited similar spectral and polaremetric pattern in both warm and cold periods, resulting in consistent accuracy. In contrast, deciduous species showed more distinctive spectral reflectance in the warm period due to physiological changes. Also, considering the diverse range of indices, the model’s behavior was systematically analyzed using Feature Importance and SHapley Additive exPlanations (SHAP) across three levels: (1) exploring the contributions of different sensors (Sentinel-1, Sentinel-2, CHM) to classification performance, (2) understanding how feature importance varied between different tree species groups, and (3) assessing how changes in individual feature values influenced the model’s output. Based on feature importance analysis, the indices DPSVI, DPSVIo, Cross Ratio, SWIR2/SWIR1, and SWIR2/Red ranked higher than others. From the perspective of SHAP analysis, the indices MIN, DPSVIo, and Cross Ratio were identified as the most influential features. Finally the implementation of proposed seasonal fusion technique realized the generation of species classification maps at various level with desirable accuracy, facilitating regular forest monitoring.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Motion consistency-guided spatiotemporal learning: a robust framework for low-altitude infrared object detection
AU  - Zhong L.
JO  - ISPRS Journal of Photogrammetry and Remote Sensing
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-08-01
VL  - 238
SP  - 712-735
DO  - 10.1016/j.isprsjprs.2026.05.026
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.isprsjprs.2026.05.026
AB  - Low-altitude infrared object detection is a fundamental technology for airspace surveillance and wide-area monitoring. However, accurately detecting dim and tiny targets remains highly challenging in complex scenes, where targets are often captured under low signal-to-noise ratio conditions and appear as weak responses embedded in drifting clouds, swaying vegetation, and other background clutter. Although multi-frame methods can exploit temporal information to enhance target perception, distinguishing target-related motion from clutter-induced motion remains difficult when both targets and dynamic background vary across frames. To address this challenge, we propose a Motion Consistency-Guided Fusion Network (MCFNet) for low-altitude infrared object detection. Specifically, we first develop a bio-inspired motion perception mechanism that combines ON/OFF pathway modeling with fractional-order temporal integration to improve sensitivity to weak motion. Building on this, we further design a motion consistency encoding strategy that estimates motion intensity and adaptively adjusts directional offsets to alleviate motion-scale mismatch while capturing motion directionality and trajectory coherence. These encoded motion cues are then introduced into an adaptive fusion process through a consistency-aware cross-attention mechanism, which retrieves and reinforces target-relevant spatial features while suppressing contamination caused by background-induced pseudo-motion. Comprehensive experiments on the Anti-UAV410, ITSDT-15 K, and IRDST benchmarks demonstrate that the proposed method achieves competitive performance, reaching an AP50 of 82.59% on the dynamic Anti-UAV410 dataset and improving AP50 by 11.43% on IRDST. Moreover, the model shows favorable robustness in complex environments characterized by intense clutter and scale variations. The source code is available at https://github.com/SZUUI/MCFNet.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Mitigating terrain-induced scattering and speckle bias in SAR-based biomass estimation: a causal analysis and machine learning approach
AU  - Raj A.
JO  - Advances in Space Research
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-08-01
VL  - 78
IS  - 3
SP  - 1936-1954
DO  - 10.1016/j.asr.2026.05.011
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.asr.2026.05.011
AB  - Accurate estimation of aboveground biomass (AGB) in mountainous landscapes remains challenging because of severe speckle contamination in Synthetic Aperture Radar (SAR) data and complex terrain-induced scattering mechanisms. The current study presents a novel framework integrating advanced speckle filtering, Decision-Making Trial and Evaluation Laboratory (DEMETEL), and Innovative Trend Analysis (ITA) to enhance biomass retrieval over the Kumbhalgarh Wildlife Sanctuary (KWLS). Multi-sensor data, including Sentinel-1C-band SAR, ALOS-2 PALSAR-2 L-band SAR, Sentinel-2 optical imagery, Landsat-9 surface reflectance, and GEDI LiDAR footprints were employed. Eight speckle filters (Boxcar, Lee, Refined Lee, IDAN, Frost, Median, Gamma, and Lee Sigma) were evaluated systematically. DEMETEL was introduced in SAR filtering to establish causal relationships among filters and objectively rank their influence on biomass prediction. Furthermore, ITA was applied to validate monotonic and non-linear trends between filtered backscatter and field-measured canopy height, overcoming the limitations of conventional correlation analysis. Machine learning models (Random Forest, XGBoost, and Multi-Layer Perceptron [MLP]) were trained using multi-sensor predictors. The results showed that the IDAN and Gamma filters consistently emerged as dominant causal drivers, improving the model performance (R2 up to 0.93, RMSE = 11.37 Mg/ha). The MLP model outperformed XGBoost and Random Forest in this heterogeneous landscape. Spatial biomass maps revealed a strong correspondence between terrain gradients and vegetation density. This study demonstrated that the DEMETEL and ITA significantly advanced SAR-based biomass modelling in heterogeneous mountainous environments, offering a transferable framework for carbon monitoring and forest management.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - DLGCNet: Multimodal remote sensing semantic segmentation via dual diagonal low-rank adaptation and graph convolutional feature fusion
AU  - Zeng J.
JO  - Knowledge Based Systems
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-07-19
VL  - 347
DO  - 10.1016/j.knosys.2026.116299
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.knosys.2026.116299
AB  - In recent years, multimodal remote sensing images (MRSI) have demonstrated complementary cross-modal information owing to their heterogeneous characteristics, enabling more detailed and effective scene interpretation than single-modality data. To address challenges in existing multimodal fusion methods, this paper proposes DLGCNet, a multimodal segmentation network that jointly optimizes a dual diagonal low-rank adaptation (D2LoRA) training framework for visual foundation models (VFMs) and a graph convolutional feature fusion (GCFF) module. To better adapt to the data distributions of MRSI, D2LoRA introduces two trainable diagonal matrices that perform row-wise and column-wise transformations on the low-rank weight matrix, thereby improving the VFM's adaptability and feature extraction performance for MRSI. To overcome the limited cross-modal modeling capacity of convolutional neural network-based fusion and the excessive complexity of transformer-based fusion, GCFF dynamically adjusts the graph Laplacian according to modal information and establishes long-range cross-modal dependencies with lower computational complexity than transformer-based fusion methods. Experimental results demonstrate that, compared to current state-of-the-art multimodal data fusion methods, the proposed DLGCNet achieves optimal segmentation results on three datasets: Potsdam, Vaihingen, and WHU-OPT-SAR. The source code is accessible at https://github.com/2023xjh2023/DLGCNet.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Detection of tea heat stress in UAV multi-spectral remote sensing images based on spatial-frequency two stream network
AU  - Wei Z.
JO  - Computers and Electronics in Agriculture
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-07-15
VL  - 249
DO  - 10.1016/j.compag.2026.111846
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.compag.2026.111846
AB  - Accurate detection of tea heat stress (THS) using uncrewed aerial vehicle (UAV) multi-spectral remote sensing images combined with deep learning techniques is crucial for THS management. However, most existing multi-spectral image fusion detection networks lack robustness in complex scenes and perform poorly in small-target feature extraction, leading to low detection accuracy. To address these issues, a spatial-frequency two-stream network (SFTNet) is proposed. It employs spatial feature extraction (SFE) and frequency feature extraction (FFE) branches to extract high-resolution spatial details from RGB images and rich frequency-domain features from NIR images, respectively. A feature enhancement structure with multiple attention mechanisms and channel interaction was designed at the output end of the FFE branch to effectively aggregate shallow and deep semantic information from the hierarchically aggregated feature maps. For multi-scale feature fusion, a multi-spectral spatial-frequency feature fusion (MSFF) module is developed to achieve tight alignment and integration of RGB and NIR features. To improve small target detection, a multiscale cross-attention aggregation (MCA) module has been proposed. By extracting fine-grained horizontal and vertical features while incorporating a cross-fusion strategy, the MCA module enhances SFTNet's learning of small target THS features, thereby improving detection accuracy for small target THS. Experimental results demonstrate that the proposed SFTNet outperforms state-of-the-art networks across 4 m, 6 m, and 8 m datasets, achieving mAP@50 improvements of 5.9%, 7.7%, and 4.6% respectively. Furthermore, when evaluated on terminal devices in a non-deployment testing setup, SFTNet achieves an inference speed of 15.3 ms per RGB-NIR image pair, suggesting high computational efficiency under these specific conditions.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - HyperKANODE: Kolmogorov-Arnold network with neural ordinary differential equations for hyperspectral image classification
AU  - Zhang Z.
JO  - Expert Systems with Applications
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-07-15
VL  - 320
DO  - 10.1016/j.eswa.2026.132148
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.eswa.2026.132148
AB  - The emerging Kolmogorov-Arnold Network (KAN) is an efficient nonlinear mapping method that works through univariate function decomposition. This capacity helps mitigate challenges in hyperspectral image (HSI) classification, such as high dimensional data redundancy and difficulty in capturing intricate nonlinear features. However, KAN’s discrete gradient update mechanism limits its ability to characterize the continuous variation patterns of features and lacks explicit modeling of the dynamic spectral-spatial interplay. To address these limitations, we propose a novel framework, named Kolmogorov-Arnold Network with Neural Ordinary Differential Equations (HyperKANODE), which deeply integrates KAN with Neural Ordinary Differential Equations (Neural ODEs) to achieve both parameter efficiency and dynamic modeling capability. Specifically, HyperKANODE introduces a tri-branch spectral-spatial dynamic modeling module named TriKAN, which incorporates learnable one-dimensional B-spline basis functions along the spectral, spatial width, and spatial height dimensions. By adaptively adjusting the grid points, the model accurately captures spectral absorption peaks and spatial texture heterogeneity, surpassing the coarse modeling of high-dimensional features typically imposed by conventional linear weights. The core innovation lies in the proposed KANODE layer, which reformulates discrete KAN transformations as a Neural ODE-driven continuous dynamical system, where B-spline nonlinear mappings explicitly parameterize the ODE vector field to govern feature evolution trajectories. Taking advantage of Neural ODEs, the model continuously tracks feature variation trajectories, enabling efficient end-to-end training and enhancing generalization to complex spectral-spatial patterns. Experiments on five publicly available HSI datasets demonstrate that HyperKANODE outperforms mainstream methods by 0.02%-5.85% in overall accuracy. Ablation studies further demonstrate the superiority of the KANODE layer in modeling dynamic learning processes, highlighting the potential of continuous dynamic modeling in advancing HSI classification.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Hyperspectral image classification using an optimized multi-layer perceptron-autoencoder bidirectional long short-term memory with Bhattacharyya wingsuit flying search optimization
AU  - Arya R.K.
JO  - Advances in Space Research
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-07-15
VL  - 78
IS  - 2
SP  - 888-900
DO  - 10.1016/j.asr.2026.04.086
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.asr.2026.04.086
AB  - Convolutional neural networks (CNNs) and recurrent neural networks (RNNs) have adequately performed the hyperspectral image classification (HSIC) task. However, CNNs struggle to capture global features, and RNNs struggle to capture local features and long-range dependencies. This study proposes a novel HSIC method, called optimized multi-layer perceptron autoencoder bidirectional long short-term memory (MLP-AE-BiLSTM), to overcome these challenges. The proposed method integrates the MLP-AE to prevent overfitting and preserve spatial and spectral correlations, and the BiLSTM to model backward and forward spectral dependencies. First, the morphological operation is used to preprocess the HSI dataset, emphasizing the objects within it. We developed an Improved SIFT (I-SIFT) to capture more effective spatial features and utilize HOG and effective spectral feature extraction methods to obtain rich, fused spectral and spatial features. To improve the training efficiency and generalization, we developed a Bhattacharyya wingsuit flying search (BWFS) optimization to optimize the parameters of BiLSTM. The proposed optimized MLP-AE-BiLSTM model has been evaluated on the publicly available HACARUS dataset, achieving better accuracy (97.89%) than state-of-the-art methods. The source code for this study is available at https://github.com/RajatArya22/MLP-AE-BiLSTM.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Precision-aware adaptive suboptimality suppression for pose recovery of non-cooperative on-orbit targets
AU  - Peng L.
JO  - Advances in Space Research
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-07-15
VL  - 78
IS  - 2
SP  - 1627-1643
DO  - 10.1016/j.asr.2026.04.101
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.asr.2026.04.101
AB  - In the context of orbital spacecraft maintenance, estimating the pose of non-cooperative targets is essential for accurate control and capture operations. However, space-borne imagery is subject to significant interference, compounded by environmental complexities such as the Earth background, which challenges the accuracy and robustness of deep learning models. This work primarily addresses Earth background interference, a critical yet underexplored challenge in orbital pose estimation. To address this issue, we propose a non-optimal suppression strategy designed to filter out interfering features while preserving effective ones. By leveraging a masked self-attention mechanism and the adaptive suppression of non-optimal keypoints, our approach extracts reliable and pertinent features to mitigate interference in space imagery. Building on this strategy, we develop the MGKSNet model for estimating the pose of non-cooperative targets; this model encompasses object detection, keypoint localization, suboptimal keypoint suppression, and pose resolution. Experimental results on the SPEED + dataset demonstrate that our model achieves a 56% improvement in rotational accuracy (0.4092°) and an 82% improvement in translational accuracy (0.0107 m) compared to existing state-of-the-art models. These findings validate the superiority of our proposed method and affirm its capability to meet the precision requirements for on-orbit space missions.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Physically interpretable machine learning for robust façade bio-colonisation detection: Comparative insights from hyperspectral and RGB imaging under hard visual interference
AU  - Hu X.
JO  - Building and Environment
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-07-15
VL  - 300
DO  - 10.1016/j.buildenv.2026.114712
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.buildenv.2026.114712
AB  - Reliable detection of bio-colonisation on heritage building façades is essential for preventive conservation but remains challenging due to complex visual interferences, such as shadows, moisture stains, and pollution. While RGB-based approaches and deep learning are increasingly adopted, their reliance on colour features often limits robustness in variable illumination conditions. This study proposes a physically interpretable machine learning framework combining Hyperspectral Imaging (HSI) and eXtreme Gradient Boosting (XGBoost) for automated bio-colonisation mapping. Using a medieval castle in Belgium as a case study, a 3D spectral façade pointcloud model is constructed, and RGB, HSI, and fused RGB–HSI approaches are systematically compared. Results show that RGB models attain a high F1-score on clean surfaces (F1 ≈ 97%), yet suffer from a high pixel-level False Positive Rate (FPR ≈ 73%) on visually unclean areas, where colour-based features overlap with mineral backgrounds. In contrast, HSI models maintain robust discrimination (FPR ≈ 2.2%) by exploiting the chlorophyll-related red-edge spectral signature. A spectral efficiency analysis identifies a critical performance leap at the 4-band multispectral (MSI) level, where the FPR drops from 8.8% to 2.6%, while full 31-band HSI provides the ultimate refinement for high-precision diagnostics. Furthermore, incorporating hard negatives during training proves decisive, reducing the fusion model's FPR from 60% to 1.9%. Sensitivity analysis reveals that exhaustive annotation is unnecessary; a small, representative dataset (e.g., ≈20,000 pixels) ensures stable detection and minimises manual effort. The proposed framework enables quantitative, spectrally grounded condition assessment for heritage façades.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - CSSH-Mamba: coupled spectral-spatial heterogeneous Mamba features for unsound maize seeds identification
AU  - Zhao K.
JO  - Expert Systems with Applications
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-07-15
VL  - 320
DO  - 10.1016/j.eswa.2026.132253
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.eswa.2026.132253
AB  - AbstractAccurate identification of unsound maize seeds is essential for ensuring grain storage safety and reliable quality assessment. Traditional seed identification methods typically rely on manual expertise and struggle to cope with the substantial variability and complexity of seed types. Although deep neural networks exhibit strong nonlinear feature extraction capabilities in hyperspectral imaging (HSI) classification, their performance in identifying unsound seeds is hindered by the high dimensionality of spectral data and the poor cross-source consistency. To overcome these challenges, we propose a Mamba-based model, termed CSSH-Mamba, which couples heterogeneous spectral-spatial features to enhance the identification accuracy of unsound maize seeds. In the spectral branch, the model incorporates a Self-adaptive Tensor-Regularization module (STM), which dynamically adjusts tensor rank while jointly modeling local consistency and global constraints to strengthen spectral feature representation. For the spatial branch, a Self-adaptive Grouped Dilated Convolution (SGDC) module is designed to capture multi-scale texture structures and compensate for the limitations of conventional convolutions in modeling spatial details. Subsequently, the model performs deep fusion of the heterogeneous spectral-spatial features and leverages the Mamba architecture to achieve efficient global semantic modeling, thereby generating more discriminative integrated feature representations. On the constructed SS-M−Dataset V1, CSSH-Mamba achieves 99.6% OA, 99.2% mAP and 99.4% Kappa, outperforming the second-best methods by 0.9%, 0.8%, and 0.6%, respectively. These results demonstrate the significant advantages and strong potential of the proposed model for precise grain seed identification.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Quantifying drought impacts on wetlands in arid regions using a change-based drought severity index (WCDI) and Sentinel-1/2 data fusion within Google Earth Engine: evidence from two wetlands in central Iran
AU  - Sarvari Z.
JO  - Advances in Space Research
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-07-15
VL  - 78
IS  - 2
SP  - 973-999
DO  - 10.1016/j.asr.2026.04.096
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.asr.2026.04.096
AB  - This study presents a comprehensive analysis of land use and land cover (LULC) changes in Gavkhouni and Mighan wetlands––two critical Ramsar sites in central Iran–during the period 2017 to 2023. By leveraging the cloud-computing capabilities of Google Earth Engine (GEE), we developed a robust classification workflow that integrates multi-source remote sensing data, including Sentinel-1 SAR, Sentinel-2 optical imagery, key spectral indices (NDVI, MNDWI, SAVI, BSI, WRI), and a digital elevation model (SRTM DEM). A comparative evaluation of three machine learning algorithms—support vector machine (SVM), decision tree (DT), and random forest (RF)—identified RF as the most accurate classifier, achieving exceptional overall accuracies exceeding 91% and Kappa coefficients above 0.89 for both wetlands. A key innovation of this research is the introduction of a novel index—the wetland change-based drought severity index (WCDI)—derived from the land cover change matrix. The WCDI quantifies drought severity by analyzing transitions between moisture-related (e.g., water, vegetation) and aridity-related classes (e.g., barren land, salt flats). Independent validation against the 12-month Standardized Precipitation Index (SPI-12) demonstrated moderate to strong correlations for both WCDI formulations across the two wetlands. For Gavkhouni, correlation coefficients were r = 0.81 (WCDI1) and r = 0.79 (WCDI2) (p < 0.05); for Mighan, they were r = 0.74 (WCDI1) and r = 0.71 (WCDI2) (p < 0.05). These results confirm that both indices respond predictably to climatic water deficits. Drought severity thresholds were empirically derived using a percentile-based approach applied separately to each wetland’s historical WCDI time series and are therefore site-specific rather than universally transferable. The change detection results reveal an alarming ecological crisis, marked by a drastic decline in water bodies and vegetation cover, alongside a significant expansion of barren land and salt flats. The application of the WCDI revealed persistent “Very Severe Drought” conditions during 2017–2021, with a slight improvement in 2021–2023 that does not indicate full recovery. These transformations are primarily attributed to prolonged drought, unsustainable water management practices, and increasing anthropogenic pressures. This research underscores the urgent need for targeted conservation strategies and offers a scalable, semi-automated framework—enhanced by the novel WCDI—for high-precision wetland monitoring (requiring site-specific calibration) and drought assessment in arid and semi-arid regions.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - MTFormer: Joint Uncertainty-Aware Abrupt-Change distance Transform and multi-task Transformer for agricultural parcel segmentation
AU  - Fang K.
JO  - Expert Systems with Applications
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-07-05
VL  - 319
DO  - 10.1016/j.eswa.2026.132068
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.eswa.2026.132068
AB  - The accurate extraction of Agricultural Parcels (AP) from high-resolution remote sensing images is pivotal for advancing agricultural digitization. However, existing deep learning methods encounter significant challenges when dealing with multi-source and heterogeneous agricultural landscapes. Specifically, the substantial variations in parcel scales and interference from complex background textures compromise model generalization. Furthermore, current boundary regression approaches lack effective uncertainty modeling mechanisms to address label noise and blurred boundaries. Moreover, while emerging geospatial foundation models demonstrate superior performance, their prohibitive computational costs and deficiency in domain-specific semantic constraints hinder their scalable application in agricultural monitoring. To address these issues, we first constructed the Multi-Regional Agricultural Parcels (MR-AP) benchmark dataset, covering seven typical agricultural regions in China. We then proposed MTFormer, an AP extraction model that unites the Uncertainty-Aware Abrupt-Change Distance Transform (UADT) with a Multi-task Transformer. Specifically, MTFormer employs a multi-scale Transformer fusion module to parallelly capture global semantic dependencies and local features. Innovatively, we replaced the standard Euclidean distance with UADT, transforming the regression task into interpretable soft-label. supervision to mitigate label noise and blurred boundaries. Additionally, a multi-task joint deep supervision strategy was introduced to collaboratively optimize mask, boundary, and regression tasks. Experimental results demonstrate that MTFormer outperforms 10 mainstream segmentation models on the MR-AP dataset, achieving a Precision, Completeness, and R2 of 94.08%, 83.93%, and 0.772, respectively. In zero-shot transfer tests across six public datasets, its Precision consistently remained above 90%, verifying the model’s robust cross-scene generalization capability. Further quantitative analysis of landscape heterogeneity reveals that, when confronted with highly complex and fragmented landscapes, geospatial foundation models are highly prone to severe over-segmentation due to a lack of AP priors. In contrast, MTFormer, leveraging the anti-interference capability of its proprietary architecture, consistently maintains highly stable extraction accuracy. Additionally, MTFormer achieves a superior trade-off between accuracy and efficiency, making it highly suitable for practical deployment and scalable application in agricultural monitoring.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - EuroMineNet: A multitemporal Sentinel-2 benchmark for spatiotemporal mining footprint analysis in the European Union (2015–2024)
AU  - Yu W.
JO  - ISPRS Journal of Photogrammetry and Remote Sensing
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-07-01
VL  - 237
SP  - 409-425
DO  - 10.1016/j.isprsjprs.2026.04.046
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.isprsjprs.2026.04.046
AB  - Mining activities play a critical role in industrial and economic development but are also a major driver of land surface degradation, including deforestation, soil erosion, and water contamination. Effective environmental governance therefore requires consistent, long-term monitoring of mining-induced land surface changes, yet existing remote sensing datasets are often limited either in temporal depth or in their focus on mining activities. In this paper, we present EuroMineNet, a multiyear benchmark for mining footprint mapping and change detection based on Sentinel-2 multispectral imagery. EuroMineNet covers 133 mining sites across the European Union and provides annual expert-verified mining footprint annotations from 2015 to 2024, enabling year-by-year analysis of mining activity dynamics at regional scale. The benchmark supports two complementary tasks: (1) multitemporal footprint mapping, which aims to generate temporally consistent annual maps of mining areas, evaluated using a proposed Change-Aware Temporal Intersection-over-Union (CA-TIoU) metric; and (2) cross-temporal change detection, which targets the identification of mining-induced changes across arbitrary temporal intervals. We benchmark 31 state-of-the-art deep learning models on these tasks. Our results show that while current GeoAI methods reliably capture long-term mining expansion, they continue to struggle with detecting subtle short-term changes. By providing a decade-long, consistently annotated dataset and a standardized evaluation protocol, EuroMineNet establishes a reference benchmark for advancing robust and temporally consistent mining footprint mapping and change detection using Earth observation data. The dataset and code are released following FAIR and open science principles at https://github.com/EricYu97/EuroMineNet.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Hyperspectral image classification via efficient global spectral supertoken clustering
AU  - Liu P.
JO  - ISPRS Journal of Photogrammetry and Remote Sensing
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-07-01
VL  - 237
SP  - 545-556
DO  - 10.1016/j.isprsjprs.2026.04.041
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.isprsjprs.2026.04.041
AB  - Hyperspectral image classification demands spatially coherent predictions and precise boundary delineation. Yet prevailing superpixel-based methods face an inherent contradiction: clustering aggregates similar pixels into regions, but the subsequent classifier operates pixel-wise, undermining regional consistency. Consequently, existing approaches do not guarantee region-level, boundary-aligned classification. To address this limitation, we propose the Dual-stage Spectrum-Constrained Clustering-based Classifier (DSCC), an end-to-end framework that explicitly decouples clustering from classification by first grouping spectral similar and spatially proximate pixels into spectral supertokens and then performing token-level prediction. At its core, DSCC computes an image-level multi-criteria feature distance between pixels and centers, followed by a locality-aware assignment regularization, enabling the generation of boundary-preserving spectral supertokens. A density-isolation based center selection further yields representative, well-separated centers, reducing redundancy and improving robustness to scale variation. To accommodate mixed land-cover compositions within each token, we introduce a soft-label scheme that encodes class proportions and improves robustness for mixed-class tokens. DSCC attains a CF1 of 0.728 at 197.75 FPS on the WHU-OHS dataset, offering a superior accuracy–efficiency trade-off compared with state-of-the-art methods. Extensive experiments further validate the effectiveness and generality of the proposed dual-stage paradigm for hyperspectral image classification. The source code is available at https://github.com/laprf/DSCC.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Physically-aware deep learning for reconstructing gap-free sea surface temperature in the South China Sea
AU  - Su C.
JO  - ISPRS Journal of Photogrammetry and Remote Sensing
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-07-01
VL  - 237
SP  - 253-270
DO  - 10.1016/j.isprsjprs.2026.04.016
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.isprsjprs.2026.04.016
AB  - Missing data caused by cloud cover, sensor limitations, and orbital gaps remain a fundamental challenge for many satellite-derived geophysical products, limiting their utility in environmental monitoring and climate research. To address this issue, we propose a physically informed deep learning framework, the Peripheral Feature-assisted Fourier-transform Convolutional Bidirectional LSTM (PFTCBiLSTM), for reconstructing spatiotemporally coherent fields from incomplete remote sensing imagery. The model combines Fourier-domain convolutional layers, which capture spatial frequency characteristics, with bidirectional recurrent units that represent nonlinear temporal dependencies, while auxiliary geophysical drivers provide physical context for air–sea interactions. Using geostationary satellite observations over the South China Sea as a test case, PFTCBiLSTM demonstrated substantial improvements over advanced baseline models. The final configuration reduced the mean absolute error (MAE) from 0.33 °C for FTC-LSTM to 0.21 °C and the root mean square error (RMSE) from 0.51 °C to 0.35 °C, while increasing the structural similarity index (SSIM) to 0.98 and maintaining R2 = 0.98. Independent validation against NOAA iQuam in situ observations confirmed robust performance across both deep-basin and coastal regimes, particularly in cloud-prone and dynamically complex regions. Sensitivity experiments, Shapley Additive Explanations (SHAP), and adversarial robustness tests using projected gradient descent (PGD) further highlighted the model’s stability and interpretability. Although demonstrated here for an oceanographic application, the proposed framework offers a transferable solution for remote sensing image reconstruction. Its design can be readily extended to other satellite-derived variables, including chlorophyll-a concentration, aerosol optical depth, and salinity, providing a versatile methodology for advancing spatiotemporal data recovery across diverse Earth observation domains.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Improving perceptual quality in low-bitrate remote sensing image compression via codebook priors and adversarial learning
AU  - Li J.
JO  - Displays
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-07-01
VL  - 93
DO  - 10.1016/j.displa.2026.103445
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.displa.2026.103445
AB  - Existing image compression algorithms have achieved significant advancements in preserving high fidelity. However, attaining superior perceptual quality often requires retraining a compression model specifically optimized for this objective, which incurs additional training costs and hampers rapid deployment. To address this limitation, we propose CodeIC, an innovative framework that leverages codebook priors and adversarial learning to enhance perceptual quality without the need for retraining existing high-fidelity compression models. CodeIC operates through three meticulously designed stages. First, we pretrain a high-quality discrete codebook using a redesigned vector quantized generative adversarial network (VQGAN), which serves as a robust generative prior. Second, we introduce a Transformer-based prediction model to align the decoded image features from an existing compression algorithm with the frozen high-quality codebook. Finally, we design a hierarchical prior integration network (HPIN), which integrates Swin Transformer blocks (STBs) and multi-head cross-attention modules (MCMs) to query hierarchical priors from the codebook, enhanced by adversarial learning. This approach significantly improves the decoding of texture-rich images, ensuring both perceptual quality and fidelity. Extensive experiments demonstrate that CodeIC achieves a favorable trade-off between perception and fidelity, delivering superior perceptual quality than high-fidelity compressors and higher fidelity than perception-oriented models. More importantly, it substantially boosts downstream task performance (e.g., salient object detection) over baseline methods, proving its preservation of critical semantic information.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Flood inundation monitoring with multi-source satellite imagery based on deep learning and explainable frameworks
AU  - Yu H.
JO  - Journal of Hydrology
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-07-01
VL  - 673
DO  - 10.1016/j.jhydrol.2026.135409
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jhydrol.2026.135409
AB  - Flood inundation monitoring using satellite data plays a crucial role in emergency response. To harness the advantages of Multispectral (MS) and Synthetic Aperture Radar (SAR) sensors while mitigating their limitations, an integration of multi-source satellite imagery is crucial. In this study, we introduced U-Net to extract water bodies from scenes captured by Sentinel-1 and Sentinel-2 satellites. The performance of the U-Net model was evaluated using both a standard dataset and real-world cases. Different combinations of multi-source satellite imagery were then compared to investigate the variations across images and identify the most effective inputs. Finally, an explainable framework called Grad-CAM was employed to gain insights into the active regions within the deep learning models’ inner layers. The U-Net model achieved strong segmentation performance on Sentinel-1 imagery, with an overall accuracy of 0.95 and an intersection over union (IoU) of 0.33. The model produced a 1.41% false-water rate and a 34.62% false-dry rate, both outperforming the baseline. When Sentinel-1 and Sentinel-2 data were jointly used as inputs, overall accuracy increased to 0.97 and IoU increased to 0.33, while the false-positive and false-negative rates decreased to 0.86% and 27.85% respectively, surpassing models trained on either data source alone. Additionally, the incorporation of auxiliary information such as DEM further enhanced the model’s performance. The analysis of the shallow layers revealed their remarkable transparency and emphasized the necessity of introducing deep learning architecture in multi-source satellite imagery applications. This study can help enhance the utilization efficiency of multi-source satellite data and improve the understanding of deep learning models used for monitoring flood inundation.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Field-scale mapping of soil available phosphorus in cropland via interpretable machine learning and multispectral remote sensing
AU  - Qiu J.
JO  - Soil and Tillage Research
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-07-01
VL  - 259
DO  - 10.1016/j.still.2026.107091
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.still.2026.107091
AB  - AbstractSoil available phosphorus (SAP) is a key indicator for assessing crop fertilization requirements. Accurately and quickly mapping the spatial distribution of SAP in cropland is crucial for guiding variable fertilization decisions. To explore a suitable model for estimating SAP at the field scale and enhance its credibility and interpretability, this study focused on multiple croplands at the Jianshan Farm in Heilongjiang Province. Soil samples were collected during the autumn bare soil period, and synchronous Sentinel-2 satellite imagery was acquired. A dataset was constructed by integrating spectral remote sensing factors with topographic variables. Random Forest Regression (RFR), Categorical Boosting (CatBoost), and Back Propagation Neural Network (BPNN) were employed to establish models for estimating SAP content in cropland. The zebra optimization algorithm (ZOA) was used to perform hyperparameter optimization and performance analysis on the best-performing model. On this basis, the Shapley additive explanations (SHAP) method was adopted to quantitatively analyze the contribution and impact of environmental variables on model predictions. Spatial distribution mapping of SAP content was conducted for both the experimental and newly added plots to evaluate the model's applicability. The results indicated that the ZOA-BPNN-G model, developed using raw bands, soil indices, and topographic variables, achieved the highest accuracy in estimating SAP (R2=0.6229, r = 0.8385, RMSE=8.4359 mg/kg, MAE=5.9816 mg/kg, MAPE=15.5123 %). Compared with the estimation model before optimization, R2 increased by 9.55 %age points, and r increased by 7.91 %age points, while the values of other evaluation indicators decreased. The raw bands and soil indices are the primary explanatory variables for estimating SAP content, with key features including NDSI, B8A, BI, and B3. Interpretability analysis and uncertainty analysis improved the transparency and credibility of the model. The proposed estimation model achieved good SAP mapping results on the experimental plots and had a strong correspondence with the SAP measured data. The MAPE for the newly added plot reached 13.1288 %, confirming the application feasibility of the proposed method to the autumn bare soil in the study area. It can provide theoretical and technical support for the rapid detection of SAP content and the generation of variable fertilization prescription maps.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Disentangling representation and cross-domain adaptation for unsupervised change detection in multimodal remote sensing images
AU  - Zhang E.
JO  - ISPRS Journal of Photogrammetry and Remote Sensing
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-07-01
VL  - 237
SP  - 22-33
DO  - 10.1016/j.isprsjprs.2026.04.011
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.isprsjprs.2026.04.011
AB  - Change detection in multimodal remote sensing images presents significant challenges, particularly for abrupt damage assessment. Multimodal images (e.g., SAR vs. optical) have different physical characteristics, making it difficult to detect land cover changes through direct comparison. Existing approaches typically rely on supervised learning (requiring costly labeled data), one-to-one modality mappings (which struggle with complex real-world modality variations), or structure similarities of modality-specific features (limiting generalizability). In this paper, we propose a novel unsupervised Disentangled Representation with Unpaired cross-domain Adaptation (DRUA) framework to address these issues. DRUA disentangles raw images into style representations (modality-specific attributes) and content (land cover features). It uses style filters to enable flexible many-to-one/one-to-many mappings, capturing modality-specific knowledge without paired data. By aligning content domains via maximal projection optimization of the raw image, DRUA facilitates direct comparison for change identification. This approach eliminates reliance on unchanged regions. Extensive experiments on five multimodal remote sensing datasets have demonstrated that DRUA can achieve superior change detection performance against ten state-of-the-art methods. By effectively disentangling both the content and style information of heterogeneous images, DRUA has attained a mean overall accuracy (OA) of 96%, an F1 score of 0.79, and an average Kappa coefficient (KC) of 0.77, all outperforming the second-best method by about 2% in OA, 0.18 in F1 score, and 0.19 in KC. The datasets used in this study and related code resources are available online at: https://github.com/sighdn/DRUA.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - MHamba: Morphological State Space Module and heterogeneous feature learning for ship detection
AU  - Wu W.
JO  - Displays
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-07-01
VL  - 93
DO  - 10.1016/j.displa.2026.103440
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.displa.2026.103440
AB  - Ship detection from remote sensing imagery faces two critical challenges: inadequate capture of elongated ship morphologies in complex nearshore environments and severe scale imbalance when small and large vessels coexist. Existing CNN-ViT hybrid methods struggle with quadratic complexity in modeling long-range dependencies and rely on fixed multi-scale aggregation strategies that lack adaptability. To address these limitations, we propose MHamba, a Marine ship Structure-Aware Vision Mamba Network that achieves rotation-robust morphological feature learning and heterogeneous multi-scale fusion. Our key contribution is the Morphological State Space Module (MSSM), which employs Zero-in Convolution for efficient local shape refinement and introduces directional consistency regularization over structure-aware scanning routes, transforming discrete multi-directional perception into continuous manifold representations. For scale-adaptive fusion, the Heterogeneous Feature Fusion Module (HFFM) dynamically reweights feature contributions through heterogeneous kernel selection and consistency-aware learnable fusion. Extensive experiments demonstrate that MHamba achieves state-of-the-art performance on HRSC2016 (91.1% AP50, 72.8% AP75, 61.8% AP50:95[jls-end-space/]) while maintaining better generalization on Seaships7000 and ShipRSImageNet benchmarks.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Hydro-environmental dynamics of Kaptai Lake using satellite derived biophysical metrics and an ensemble Machine Learning Framework
AU  - Rafi K.R.
JO  - Journal of Contaminant Hydrology
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-07-01
VL  - 281
DO  - 10.1016/j.jconhyd.2026.104989
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jconhyd.2026.104989
AB  - Bangladesh is a land of numerous fluvial waters bodies traversing across the whole landscape which has the complex waterbodies form the hydrological and ecological regime. Prediction of watershed change intrinsically imposes challenges due to complex hydro-climatic reasons and water quality processes. The Kaptai lake manifests fluctuations for its seasonal precipitation and changes in vegetation condition. The remote sensing-based watershed behavior analysis using Machine learning (ML) architecture particularly Stacked Regression (SR) for Kaptai boundary is seen to have a potential to address the variability that is absent in the current literature. This study has been conducted using the Kaptai watershed, which is one of the largest artificial lake of South-East Asia, with the objective for developing a data driven methodology for a sustainable forecasting of watershed behavior. Thus, water Quality Index, meteorological variables, integrative synthesis of remote sensing indices along with combination of Machine Learning Architecture have been applied. For capturing the spatial and temporal dynamics, spatial maps spanning the 1990–2025, a period of 35 years have been generated using ArcMap (ArcGIS) for salient biophysical and water quality parameters. The study reveals that the steep slope of the Chittagong Hill Tracts (CHT) is the root cause of high TDS and Turbidity (NDTI). The heavy rains and land-use changes, namely deforestation and shifting cultivation further assist in transport of large sediment load into the reservoir. The indication of a positive non-linear water quality dynamics, periodic eutrophication and turbidity peaks (NDCI up to 0.99) and spikes, and significant vegetation loss and significant increase in LST are seen to play the role. A LightGBM model for Time Series prediction and a Stacked Regression model comprising of XGBoost, Random Forest and Multi-Layer Perceptron has been integrated through RidgeCV meta-Learner. The derived analytical outcomes give an R2 = 0.977 from Stacked Regression modelling. Moreover, by utilizing Light GBM and ANN an R2 value of 0.9402 and 0.99 have been obtained, respectively. This framework shows substantive functionality for forecasting in a scalable approach by involving hydrological, and biophysical processes within the watershed under complex variability. The findings here are a pathway towards an empirically grounded water management and climate resilient behaviors of Kaptai lake, that in turn help in holistic decision making.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Early detection and firmness prediction of apple fruit infested by Bactrocera dorsalis using hyperspectral imaging combined with 1D convolutional neural network
AU  - Shao Y.
JO  - Vibrational Spectroscopy
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-07-01
VL  - 145
DO  - 10.1016/j.vibspec.2026.103935
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.vibspec.2026.103935
AB  - To achieve accurate quality assessment of B. dorsalis-infested apples, this study proposes an early detection and firmness prediction method based on hyperspectral imaging (HSI) integrated with a one-dimensional convolutional neural network (1D-CNN). First, a hyperspectral imaging system (400–1000 nm) was used to acquire images over two consecutive years for three categories of apple samples: healthy, B. dorsalis-infested, and superficially similar scarred apples, while the firmness of each fruit was measured simultaneously. Subsequently, the successive projections algorithm (SPA) and competitive adaptive reweighted sampling (CARS) were applied to select characteristic bands related to fruit categories and firmness, respectively. Finally, early detection and firmness prediction models were developed by integrating partial least squares-discriminant analysis (PLS-DA), back-propagation neural network (BP), 1D-CNN, random forest (RF), support vector machine (SVM), and 1D-CNN. The results showed that the SPA–1D-CNN detection model with 10 selected characteristic bands achieved satisfactory performance, with an overall classification accuracy of 94%, precision of 0.94, recall of 0.94, and an F1-score of 0.94. For firmness prediction, the SPA–1D-CNN model also performed well, For the calibration set, Rc2 was 0.87, RMSEC was 0.76. For the prediction set, Rp2 was 0.86, RMSEP was 0.79. Moreover, pixel-wise firmness prediction was implemented on the spectral images, enabling the visualization of apple firmness distribution. This study demonstrates that HSI combined with 1D-CNN provides an effective approach for early quality assessment of B. dorsalis-infested apples, providing theoretical support for the development of relevant detection devices.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Hydro-meteorological drivers of spatiotemporal variability in lake water storage across China: Evidence from ICESat-2
AU  - Xu H.
JO  - Journal of Hydrology
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-07-01
VL  - 673
DO  - 10.1016/j.jhydrol.2026.135449
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jhydrol.2026.135449
AB  - Lakes are crucial indicators and regulators of climate change, moderators of regional climate dynamics, and pointers of global environmental shifts. However, the underlying meteorological and hydrological mechanisms shaping lake water levels remain inadequately understood. This study leveraged ICESat-2 satellite data to measure the water level of 237 medium and large lakes across China’s five lake regions in 2019‒2024. Four machine learning models were employed to fill missing water-level data, facilitating a detailed examination of the spatiotemporal dynamics in seasonal variations of lake water level (LWLsv) and lake water storage (LWSsv). Sixteen feature climatic, hydrological and surface-physical variables were selected for modeling. Correlation and trend analyses assessed the impact of drought factors, as measured by meteorological and hydrological drought indices, on LWSsv changes under compound conditions. The findings reveal that: (1) ICESat-2 satellites provide effective monitoring of LWL changes despite data gaps for some lakes due to size and shape limitations; (2) Among the tested models, XGBoost demonstrated superior performance, highlighting significant spatial variability in LWLsv across China’s lakes, discovering a macro-scale nationwide pattern of “higher levels in the east, lower levels in the west, stable in plateaus, and large fluctuations in plains”; (3) From 2019 to 2024, the total LWSsv for Chinese lakes reached 53.80 Gt, mirroring considerable inter-regional spatial heterogeneity in LWLsv patterns. Lakes in the middle and lower Yangtze River accounted for approximately 50% of the national LWSsv, while the Qiangtang Plateau’s inland lakes ranked second due to their abundance and size; (4) Water storage fluctuations displayed distinct responses to meteorological, hydrological, and compound conditions, with around 140 lakes influenced jointly by climatic and hydrological factors. The dominant drivers showed notable regional variations, reflecting the intricate interactions between meteorological and hydrological processes in lake ecosystems. The findings offer key scientific insights for water hydrological management and biodiversity conservation.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Support fully-connected tensor networks machine and its safe screening rule
AU  - Wang H.
JO  - Expert Systems with Applications
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-06-25
VL  - 317
DO  - 10.1016/j.eswa.2026.131865
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.eswa.2026.131865
AB  - Tensors as multidimensional data representations have garnered significant research attention in machine learning. While recent advances in support tensor machines using tensor train (TT) and tensor ring (TR) decompositions have shown promising results, they are limited to modeling interactions only between adjacent modes and exhibit sensitivity to tensor mode ordering, which restricts their expressive power and robustness. To overcome these fundamental limitations, we propose a novel support fully-connected tensor networks machine (FCTN-STM). At its core, FCTN-STM employs a fully-connected tensor network (FCTN) decomposition to model the weight tensor, thereby capturing intrinsic correlations between any two modes of the tensor, which leads to stronger model expressiveness and structural flexibility. Furthermore, to enhance computational efficiency without compromising accuracy, we introduce a safe screening rule (SSR-FCTN-STM) and integrate it into an alternating projection solution framework. This rule reduces each projection sub-problem by identifying and removing redundant variables during training. Its key advantage is safety, i.e., it guarantees that the removed variables are indeed redundant, ensuring the solution remains identical to the original problem. Experiments on seven real-world tensor datasets, including face (ORL), hyperspectral (HSI), functional magnetic resonance imaging (fMRI), and electroencephalogram (EEG) signal datasets, demonstrate that: 1) our SSR-FCTN-STM significantly outperforms existing methods in classification accuracy; 2) the SSR mechanism substantially reduces training time while fully preserving model accuracy. This work provides a novel and practical solution for efficient and powerful tensor learning.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Deep feature learning framework embedded with latent relationship penalty term for remote sensing image classification
AU  - Huang Y.
JO  - Advances in Space Research
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-06-15
VL  - 77
IS  - 12
SP  - 11801-11817
DO  - 10.1016/j.asr.2026.04.016
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.asr.2026.04.016
AB  - Due to spectral mixing and the high similarity of spectral signatures among different land-cover categories, optical remote sensing image classification remains a challenging task. Most existing methods implicitly assign identical attribute scores to all images, failing to capture the unique characteristics of individual sample, which fundamentally limits classification performance. To address this issue, we propose a novel deep feature learning framework embedded with latent relationship penalty term (DFLRPSFS), which explicitly exploits image-specific uniqueness. Specifically, a ResMLP module consisting of multi-layer perceptron (MLP) and residual connections (Res) is integrated into the fine-tuned VGG16 model to enhance the feature representations. Furthermore, we propose latent relationship-preserving sparse feature selection (LRPSFS), which introduces a latent relationship penalty term. The penalty term exploits image-specific uniqueness as prior knowledge to guide the selection process. Finally, the support vector machine (SVM) is employed to generate the classification results. Experimental results demonstrate that the proposed DFLRPSFS outperforms state-of-the-art methods, achieving accuracy improvements of 7.54% on the UCM dataset, 8.93% and 8.64% on 20% and 50% AID30 subsets, and 9.97% and 11.44% on 10% and 20% NWPU-RESISC45 subsets, respectively, validating its effectiveness and superiority.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Best practice of land use land cover classification using machine learning techniques and future land use prediction through artificial neural network
AU  - Doğruyol P.G.
JO  - Advances in Space Research
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-06-15
VL  - 77
IS  - 12
SP  - 11766-11783
DO  - 10.1016/j.asr.2026.04.014
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.asr.2026.04.014
AB  - Assessing changes in land use/land cover in urban areas, primarily driven by human needs, is critical in planning and managing future land use. Within this context, machine learning techniques can be effectively employed. In this study, machine learning techniques were used to classify the land use/land cover of the central settlements and surrounding areas of Yalova Province, which lost a significant portion of its built-up areas due to the 7.4-magnitude earthquake in 1999. Classification was carried out using the Classification and Regression Tree, Random Forest, and Support Vector Machine algorithms on the Google Earth Engine platform and Convolutional Neural Network model on Google Colaboratory based on Sentinel-2 satellite imagery from 2018 and 2024, to determine the most suitable algorithm. The integration of machine learning algorithms with an ensemble classification approach was tested to enhance classification accuracy, resulting in highly reliable land use maps (kappa: 0.984 for 2018; 0.990 for 2024). Over the six years, a 10.74% increase was observed, primarily in built-up areas. The future land use/land cover for 2036 was simulated using the Artificial Neural Network method. The accuracy of the simulation was evaluated using the Receiver Operating Characteristic curve, yielding a high reliability with an Area Under the Curve value of 0.813. The analysis revealed that the land-use change trend observed between 2018 and 2024 is projected to persist, with grasslands, water bodies, and areas of dense vegetation transitioning into built-up areas. This finding suggests a progressive decline in natural vegetation over time, with the potential for irreversible loss unless effective, ecologically grounded management strategies are implemented.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Modeling social unrest: The geographical influence on social unrest prediction
AU  - Bedi S.
JO  - Expert Systems with Applications
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-06-15
VL  - 316
DO  - 10.1016/j.eswa.2026.131640
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.eswa.2026.131640
AB  - Social unrest, a collective expression of discontent, is a salient indicator of deeper societal problems. Its prediction, especially at higher spatial resolutions, is challenging due to its complex dependence on socioeconomic, political, and environmental factors. Accurately forecasting social unrest will provide a better understanding of its drivers and help develop mitigation strategies. This study evaluates the role of geographic context in predicting social unrest by proposing two different and novel approaches to predict social unrest using machine learning techniques: (a) a geographic context-independent model using eXtreme Gradient Boosting (XGBoost), which predicts unrest based on features at a single point, and (b) a geographic context-dependent model using a Convolutional Neural Network (CNN), which incorporates features from the surrounding geospatial neighborhood. The models use remote sensing imagery and rasters of geographic, socio-demographic, and economic variables (and their proxies). In addition, the study presents a detailed and unique analysis to identify the significance of the features in predicting social unrest using Shapley values. Another key innovation of this research is the estimation of the confidence in the models’ predictions using a Monte Carlo dropout technique. This provides valuable insights into the model’s reliability and uncertainty in prediction as a function of geographic space. Results show that VIIRS nighttime light and land use are among the significant predictors of social unrest. Furthermore, prediction performance comparison shows that context-dependent CNNs outperform the context-independent XGBoost model in accuracy and F1 scores in both binary and multiclass classification tasks. The findings demonstrate the potential of machine learning in accurately understanding complex social science processes.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Machine learning-based estimation and model transferability of soil organic carbon using ground, airborne and satellite hyperspectral remote sensing
AU  - Kaushik M.
JO  - Advances in Space Research
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-06-15
VL  - 77
IS  - 12
SP  - 11614-11628
DO  - 10.1016/j.asr.2026.03.093
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.asr.2026.03.093
AB  - Soil organic carbon (SOC) is crucial for preserving soil health, plant development, and assisting with sustainable farming methods. Developing a transferable method that can be applied across different regions for SOC estimation is vital for achieving reliable and precise measurements in various regions. There are several works on estimating SOC using multispectral or hyperspectral imagery employing various statistical or machine learning algorithms. The major limitation is the lack of studies on the modelling from a transferability perspective to apply on a realistic operational basis. A transferable model for SOC enables rapid and field-based strategies to manage soil heterogeneity. The objective of this research is to develop a site-independent, machine-learning transferable method for estimating SOC from hyperspectral data. As part of a larger perspective on soil spectroscopy, in situ, airborne, and satellite hyperspectral data were acquired at two sites in India and Germany. In situ spectral measurements and soil samples taken over the German site were used to build a reference SOC model. The hyperspectral datasets processed for retrieving spectral reflectance were used for the estimation of SOC using four machine-learning algorithms: Partial Least Square Regression (PLSR), Random Forest Regression (RFR), Elastic Net Regression (ENR), and Support Vector Regression (SVR). The quality of results was assessed using different validation metrics with reference ground truth SOC. The better-performing PLSR-based model has exhibited good retrieval with a coefficient of determination ((Formula presented) ) of 74% and a ratio of performance to deviation (RPD) of 1.66 across platforms and sites. The results suggest broad prospects of transferable ML-based models for field-level SOC mapping using hyperspectral data across regions and data acquisition platforms or sensors.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - A physically constrained and interpretable deep learning framework for PM2.5 inversion under sparse monitoring conditions in arid regions
AU  - Zhang X.
JO  - Environmental Pollution
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-06-15
VL  - 399
DO  - 10.1016/j.envpol.2026.128233
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.envpol.2026.128233
AB  - Monitoring fine particulate matter (PM2.5) in arid regions remains difficult due to sparse ground-based networks and frequent extreme pollution episodes. Conventional inversion models often fail to capture these sudden concentration spikes. To address this gap, we developed ResNet-DFL, a physically constrained and interpretable deep learning framework aimed at satellite-based PM2.5 estimation in data-scarce areas. We applied a distribution-aware learning strategy to reduce long-tail bias and integrated Shapley Additive Explanations (SHAP) with two-dimensional Partial Dependence Plots (2D-PDPs) to quantify key drivers and their nonlinear interactions. Validation using independent sparse-station scenarios showed that ResNet-DFL achieves a spatial R2 of 0.79 and a temporal R2 of 0.68. It consistently outperforms benchmark baseline models like Random Forest and Geographically and Temporally Weighted Regression, particularly during heavy pollution events. Our interpretability analysis shows that relative humidity, temperature, and NDVI are the dominant factors governing local pollution. Furthermore, 2D-PDPs confirm that low temperature combined with high humidity primarily triggers PM2.5 accumulation in this region. We also found that a vertically normalized aerosol optical depth (RAOD) is critical to link column-integrated satellite observations with near-surface PM2.5 by adjusting for boundary layer dynamics. Ultimately, ResNet-DFL provides a reliable tool for PM2.5 estimation in data-scarce arid regions, providing practical data support for local air quality management and targeted pollution control.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Integrating satellite embeddings and national forest inventory data to map above-ground biomass of temperate deciduous old-growth forests
AU  - Sohrabi H.
JO  - Advances in Space Research
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-06-15
VL  - 77
IS  - 12
SP  - 11837-11850
DO  - 10.1016/j.asr.2026.04.018
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.asr.2026.04.018
AB  - Accurate estimation of above-ground biomass (AGB) is fundamental for understanding carbon dynamics, assessing forest health, and guiding conservation strategies. However, mapping AGB in structurally complex old-growth forests remains challenging due to limitations in remote sensing data and modeling approaches. This study presents the first evaluation of the newly released AlphaEarth Foundation (AEF) embeddings (a global, analysis-ready dataset derived from multimodal Earth observation sources) for AGB estimation. Using 2543 field plots from the Hyrcanian forests of northern Iran, six machine learning algorithms (MLs), Random Forest (RF), Extreme Gradient Boosting (XGBoost), Support Vector Regression (SVR), k-Nearest Neighbors (KNN), Linear Regression (LR), and Artificial Neural Networks (ANN) were evaluated, together with a weighted ensemble approach. The correction of plot locations using a canopy height model derived from Meta AI models significantly reduced geolocation bias, underscoring the importance of spatial alignment between field and satellite data. Feature importance analysis showed that a subset of layers strongly contributed to predictive power. Across algorithms, non-linear models slightly outperformed linear methods, with XGBoost and ANN achieving the highest accuracies (R2 ≈ 0.63 after geolocation correction). Ensemble modeling further improved prediction robustness (R2 ≈ 0.71), reducing error metrics and producing spatially consistent AGB maps at 10 m resolution. These findings highlight the value of AEF embeddings as a novel feature set for AGB mapping in complex forest ecosystems. This work demonstrates how foundation models, combined with ensemble learning, can support high-resolution, scalable AGB monitoring and provide essential tools for forest management, carbon accounting, and biodiversity conservation.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Direction-aware frequency domain network for fine-grained cloud removal in remote sensing images
AU  - Jia J.
JO  - Expert Systems with Applications
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-06-10
VL  - 315
DO  - 10.1016/j.eswa.2026.131681
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.eswa.2026.131681
AB  - Optical remote sensing images (RSIs) are often subject to cloud contamination during acquisition, resulting in substantial quality degradation and severely constraining their practical applications. We performed wavelet decomposition on cloud-contaminated images and found that thick clouds are mainly concentrated in the low-frequency components, while thin clouds cause disturbances in the high-frequency components. Some methods currently attempt to perform cloud removal in the frequency domain. However, directional information as a key characteristic of the frequency domain is often neglected. To address this, we propose a Direction-Aware Frequency Network (DAF-Net), comprising a directional classifier and a Direction-Aware Frequency Module (DAFM). The classifier determines the dominant direction based on high-frequency features in a lightweight manner, and the DAFM adaptively integrates multi-directional components to suppress thin cloud interference. The refined high-frequency features are subsequently injected into the low-frequency components, thereby enhancing reconstruction with improved structural coherence and enriched textural detail. The thick cloud dataset typically consists of multi-temporal data, while the thin cloud dataset is usually mono-temporal. Experiments on the multi-temporal Sen2_MTC dataset as well as the mono-temporal NUAA-CRL8/9 and CUHK-CR datasets demonstrate that DAF-Net outperforms comparison representative methods across evaluation metrics, including PSNR, SSIM, LPIPS, and RMSE. Furthermore, when processing multi-temporal images, DAF-Net is four times faster than the excellent diffusion model (DiffCR). It effectively improves cloud removal performance and provides a new perspective for the reconstruction of RSIs. The source code of DAF-Net model will be accessible at https://github.com/JJH0923/DAF-Net.
ER  -

