TI  - Adapting vision foundation models with lightweight trident decoder for remote sensing change detection
AU  - Ye W.
JO  - Expert Systems with Applications
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-06-01
VL  - 313
DO  - 10.1016/j.eswa.2026.131472
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.eswa.2026.131472
AB  - The field of remote sensing image change detection has attracted significant interest. However, prevailing methods tend to concentrate on the basic extraction of change features, neglecting the crucial role of stable semantic patterns (e.g., ”features that remain consistent across time”) in bi-temporal images (image pairs taken at different times) for enhancing change feature extraction precision. Moreover, existing change detection networks often grapple with striking an optimal balance between accuracy and complexity. To surmount these challenges, we leverage the advanced feature extraction capabilities of pre-trained Vision Foundation Models (FastSAM), introducing an innovative lightweight Trident Decoder framework for remote sensing change detection, termed LTCD. Our framework encompasses three pioneering fully convolutional modules: the Attention-CDut Module, Trident Decoder Module, and Segment-Head Module. The Attention-CD Module enriches the semantic content of bi-temporal image features via cross-attention, which precedes the decoding phase, effectively decoupling change features. The Trident Decoder Module intensifies the influence of stable semantic patterns on change features within the feature pyramid, resulting in a distinctive trident-shaped feature output. Furthermore, the Segment-Head Module integrates shallow auxiliary tasks under a deep supervision learning scheme to bolster feature interaction. Experimental results demonstrate that our work outperforms state-of-the-art (SOTA) approaches, including both CNN-based and Transformer-based methods, across five challenging benchmark datasets, achieving improved accuracy with a model size 10  ×  smaller than previous SOTA models.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Mapping glacial lakes with glacial-lake compact efficient neural network (GCE-Net): perspectives on balancing model size and performance
AU  - Wang Q.
JO  - Journal of Hydrology
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-06-01
VL  - 672
DO  - 10.1016/j.jhydrol.2026.135318
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jhydrol.2026.135318
AB  - Rapid glacier retreat in the Himalayas is accelerating glacial lake formation and expansion, increasing potential GLOF risk. Frequent, large-area mapping is needed to track rapidly changing lakes, but the computational cost of deep learning models limits high-frequency updates in resource-constrained mountain regions. To address this limitation, we propose GCE-Net (Glacial-lake Compact Efficient Neural Network), a compact segmentation framework designed to improve efficiency while maintaining accuracy (F1 score = 94.43%). GCE-Net uses a ResNet-34 encoder and a UNet++ decoder improved with Ghost modules and Efficient Channel Attention (ECA). After training, we apply a depth-guided structured pruning strategy to the full architecture without additional fine-tuning. Results show that the pruning level L3 sub-network (L3) provides a favorable balance between accuracy and efficiency, reducing parameters by 62.7% and computation by 30.6%, while incurring only a minor change in accuracy (F1 decrease < 0.3%). The trained GCE-Net was applied to the Southeastern Tibetan Plateau (SETP) to generate a 2023 glacial lake inventory, including 6,455 lakes with a total area of 400.18 km2. Comparison with published 1990 and 2018 inventories indicates faster lake growth after 2018, with recent increases dominated by small-to-medium lakes. For lakes that persisted from 1990 to 2023, net area change was positive within 0–2 km of glaciers but negative beyond 2 km, and the average per-lake change generally weakened with distance.
