DO  - 10.1016/j.ijagro.2026.100095
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ijagro.2026.100095
AB  - This study explores how hyperspectral sensors mounted on unmanned aerial vehicles (UAVs) may assist in maize nitrogen status monitoring and yield prediction using traditional regression and machine learning models. Three field experiments were conducted with three soil types (alluvial soil, black soil, and aeolian sand soil) and various nitrogen (N) fertilizer treatments (0, 168, 240, 270 and 312 kg N/ha) in Lishu County, Northeast China. Hyperspectral images obtained from sensors mounted on UAVs were collected to monitor the nitrogen nutrition index (NNI) at the jointing, silking, and maturity stages and the grain yield of maize in 2019 and 2020. In comparison to the prediction performance of the partial least squares regression (PLSR) and random forest (RF) regression models, 13 narrowband vegetation indices (VIs) and N application rates were employed as predictors to determine N status at the field scale. The results revealed that most VIs were significantly correlated with the NNI and yield at different stages and that the Maccioni index was the most influential predictor for both the NNI and yield estimation based on the relative importance calculation results of the different predictors. Compared with the PLSR model, the NNI and yield were better estimated by the RF model, except for yield estimation at the jointing and silking stages. The best performance for maize NNI and yield estimation was achieved at the silking stage and maturity stage, respectively. Based on the relationships between the NNI and yield, the NNI intervals were 0.91–1.30 for alluvial and black soil and 0.67–0.72 for aeolian sandy soil, with the goal of achieving the target yield. This study highlights the significance of introducing UAV technologies in providing a field-scale data-driven approach for crop nitrogen monitoring and yield prediction information to farmers and policy-makers for better precision crop management.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Machine learning-based detection of fusarium wilt in tomato using ground-based and satellite hyperspectral imagery
AU  - Chaitra H.
JO  - Advances in Space Research
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-05-01
VL  - 77
IS  - 9
SP  - 8679-8694
DO  - 10.1016/j.asr.2026.03.018
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.asr.2026.03.018
AB  - Hyperspectral remote sensing has been evolving as a multi-purpose spectral data sensing tool for various applications in agriculture. Asymptomatic disease detection in crops is vital for sensing technology-blended agronomic practices. Tomato, which forms a major portion of the global vegetable production, is susceptible to many diseases and pests. Fusarium wilt is one of the devastating diseases affecting tomato crops, with the risk of crop damage exceeding 70%. Various studies have attempted spectral discrimination of the tomato leaves affected by various diseases using hyperspectral data. However, the existence and continuity of the features of spectral discrimination observed at the in situ or laboratory level to the operationally useful natural farms at the regional level are yet to be understood. The objective of this research is the spectral detection and mapping of Fusarium wilt in tomatoes at plant and regional levels using hyperspectral data acquired from in situ, terrestrial, and satellite platforms. We acquired hyperspectral data from the ground (in situ and terrestrial) and the PRISMA satellite representing leaf, canopy, and field levels over a tomato growing region, Tumakur, India, in 2022. Identifying optimal spectral bands appropriate from leaf-level spectral measurements to field-level satellite-based hyperspectral imagery, we have calibrated and applied four different ML models for the detection and mapping of Fusarium wilt at five levels of disease severity (mildly infected with symptoms invisible to the human eye, infected but requiring expert inspection to localize, visibly infected, infected and extensively spread, and fully wilted) over a region of 263 sq. km. The results suggest model convergence of the disease detection at different levels of severity. At the ground level, all the four ML models used detect the Fusarium wilt at all the levels of disease intensity with accuracy varying from 85% to 88%. Upscaling the ML models to detect disease at the regional level, the quality of results indicates different degrees of success, ranging from 60% (invisible symptoms) to 93% (fully wilted). With an appropriate combination of spectral bands and ML algorithms, it is possible to detect and map Fusarium wilt in tomato with good prediction accuracy when the disease is at a mild stage.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Multi-granularity Prompt Learning with vision-language model for few-shot remote sensing image scene classification
AU  - Zhang T.
JO  - ISPRS Journal of Photogrammetry and Remote Sensing
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-05-01
VL  - 235
SP  - 349-362
DO  - 10.1016/j.isprsjprs.2026.03.013
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.isprsjprs.2026.03.013
AB  - Few-shot remote sensing image scene classification (FS-RSI-SC) has emerged as a promising approach to alleviate the large-scale data reliance in the scene classification task and has garnered increasing attention. However, most existing FS-RSI-SC methods require a base training stage with large amounts of relevant remote sensing scene images before adapting to the few-shot novel scene category. Recently, Visual-Language Models (VLMs) have demonstrated remarkable cross-domain transferability and enabled rapid adaptation to downstream tasks with only a few annotated samples, providing a novel insight into the FS-RSI-SC task. Therefore, in this paper, we concentrate on effectively adapting the pre-trained VLMs to the FS-RSI-SC task and propose a novel Multi-granularity Prompt Learning Network (MPLNet). Considering that scene categories contain multi-granularity information about ground objects, we propose a Multi-granularity Visual Representations (MVR) module to capture scene information from global, regional, and local granularities. Next, we design a Multi-granularity Textual Representations (MTR) module that learns diverse textual representations tailored to different granularity visual representations. Furthermore, an Attention-guided Similarity Mechanism (ASM) is presented to highlight the scene-relevant semantic information and alleviate the background noise interference. Comprehensive experiments on four FS-RSI-SC datasets demonstrate that our proposed MPLNet achieves the state-of-the-art classification accuracy across all few-shot settings. The code is available at https://github.com/Trinstar/MPLNet.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - A novel near real-time approach for salient object detection in hyperspectral images using distinctiveness and spatial cohesion
AU  - Lone Z.A.
JO  - Advances in Space Research
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-05-01
VL  - 77
IS  - 9
SP  - 8926-8938
DO  - 10.1016/j.asr.2026.02.091
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.asr.2026.02.091
AB  - Salient Object Detection (SOD) in Hyperspectral images (HSI) aims to identify the most spectrally distinctive regions in an image and is widely used in applications such as remote sensing, environmental monitoring, and precision agriculture. Existing methods often rely on location priors, making them sensitive to object position in the image, or depend on similarity metrics, making them computationally complex. Deep learning (DL) based approaches have also been explored, but they are generally data-intensive, and the limited availability of large-scale HSOD datasets constrain their applicability. To address these limitations, we propose a location-independent and similarity-metric-agnostic framework that jointly exploits spectral and spatial information. The Isolation Forest (IF), a machine learning algorithm, is used to identify pixels with anomalous spectral signatures, followed by weighted connected component analysis on the intermediate binary saliency map to obtain a coherent salient object. The Minimum Noise Fraction (MNF) transform is further applied to enhance anomalous spectral behavior while reducing noise. Experiments on the HS-SOD dataset show that the proposed method achieves a precision of 0.69, recall of 0.49, and F1-score of 0.54 at a 12% pixel threshold, outperforming state-of-the-art approaches. The method is also computationally efficient, with sensitivity analysis indicating nearly a threefold improvement in runtime, requiring only 0.38 s compared to 1.40 s for existing methods.
