TY  - JOUR
TI  - Future land use change and carbon storage dynamics: a machine learning-based spatial scenario approach
AU  - Roradeh H.
JO  - Advances in Space Research
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-05-01
VL  - 77
IS  - 9
SP  - 8782-8803
DO  - 10.1016/j.asr.2026.02.099
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.asr.2026.02.099
AB  - Land-use and land-cover change (LULC) is a critical driver of terrestrial carbon dynamics and climate change, particularly in regions undergoing rapid urbanization. This study integrates satellite imagery, land-use maps, and the Integrated Valuation of Ecosystem Services and Tradeoffs (InVEST) model to examine the spatiotemporal interactions between LULC trajectories and carbon storage in Sari County, northern Iran. Landsat data for 1995, 2005, and 2025 were classified using the Random Forest algorithm, while future projections to 2060 were generated through the MOLUSCE framework combining Artificial Neural Networks (ANN) and Markov Chains. The results reveal profound transformations characterized by a sixfold expansion of built-up areas, primarily at the expense of cropland (−19%) and forests (−4.3%). This land conversion translated into substantial carbon sequestration losses: areas with negligible carbon stocks (0 Mg C/ha) expanded from ∼7700 ha in 1995 to >26,500 ha by 2060, while high-density carbon pools (235 Mg C/ha) associated with forests declined by more than 7600 ha. These findings highlight urban expansion as the dominant threat to ecosystem carbon balance and climate regulation. Without integrated land-use planning and conservation strategies, the region faces significant reductions in ecosystem services, underscoring the urgent need for sustainable resource management and climate mitigation policies.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Unifying semantic segmentation and change detection in urban villages: The ZY-UV benchmark and MutualTemp framework
AU  - Zhang X.
JO  - ISPRS Journal of Photogrammetry and Remote Sensing
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-05-01
VL  - 235
SP  - 470-487
DO  - 10.1016/j.isprsjprs.2026.03.028
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.isprsjprs.2026.03.028
AB  - AbstractUrban village (UV) detection, which aims to identify informal settlements with distinctive village-like morphology embedded within urban fabrics, is fundamental for precision urban renewal and sustainable city management. Existing datasets and detection methods have made progress in semantic segmentation of UVs, but they predominantly emphasize single-temporal mapping and neglect cross-temporal change analysis, leaving the dynamic evolution of UVs insufficiently explored. To fill this gap, we introduce ZY-UV, the first large-scale, high-resolution multi-temporal benchmark dedicated to joint semantic segmentation and change detection of UVs. Constructed from 2.1 m ZiYuan-3 satellite imagery, ZY-UV covers six geographically diverse Chinese cities and provides carefully annotated multi-city UV boundaries and paired bi-temporal image sequences. This design enables systematic evaluation of both spatial semantics and temporal dynamics under heterogeneous urban contexts and varying imaging conditions. On top of this dataset, we propose MutualTemp, a multitask learning framework that harmonizes semantic segmentation and change detection through a shared Siamese encoder and mutually regulated temperature parameters. By dynamically modulating task-specific feature distributions and balancing gradient flows, MutualTemp effectively mitigates task interference, adapts to relative task difficulty, and improves cross-domain generalization. Together, ZY-UV and MutualTemp form a comprehensive platform for advancing urban village monitoring, providing not only a challenging benchmark for evaluating multi-task and domain adaptation strategies but also a practical foundation for future studies in large-scale urban dynamics, cross-city knowledge transfer, and robust geospatial intelligence in rapidly evolving urban environments.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Cloud removal in remote sensing images using the G2R framework and SSMamba
AU  - Zhang C.
JO  - Remote Sensing of Environment
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-05-01
VL  - 337
DO  - 10.1016/j.rse.2026.115321
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.rse.2026.115321
AB  - Developing a practical and robust cloud removal method has long been a crucial research objective in the field of remote sensing. Existing multi-temporal approaches typically adopt cloud-free images from other acquisition dates as training targets. However, the inherent temporal heterogeneity of optical observations introduces intrinsic limitations to this paradigm. To overcome these issues, we propose a novel cloud removal strategy based on a Generate-to-Remove (G2R) self-supervised framework. Unlike conventional methods, G2R employs the cloudy image itself as the supervision target, thereby introducing temporal constraints into the reconstruction process and enabling faithful recovery of the original surface features beneath cloud-covered regions. Furthermore, to address the insufficient capability of existing multi-temporal networks in modeling spatio-spectral dynamics and cross-dimensional interactions, we design the Spectral–Spatial Mamba network (SSMamba). Built upon a structured state space model, SSMamba adopts dual spectral and spatial branches tailored for multi-temporal inputs, achieving coordinated representation learning and efficient feature interaction. This design allows for fully exploiting spectral consistency and spatial dependencies across temporal observations, leading to improved detail restoration and stronger generalization. Experimental results on multiple real-image scenes demonstrate that the proposed framework not only achieves accurate reconstruction of cloud-covered regions but also delivers substantial advantages over state-of-the-art approaches, highlighting its strong potential for operational applications.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Meta-learning with limited samples for mangrove monitoring across complex coastal spectral domains
AU  - Zhang R.
JO  - Ocean and Coastal Management
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-05-01
VL  - 276
DO  - 10.1016/j.ocecoaman.2026.108173
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ocecoaman.2026.108173
AB  - Mangroves are critical coastal ecosystems that provide shoreline stabilization, storm protection, and carbon sequestration. Yet their ecological functions have been increasingly degraded by invasive species, typhoons, and human activities. Accurate mapping of mangrove distribution is essential for effective conservation, but conventional remote sensing approaches rely heavily on large annotated datasets and often fail in data-scarce or spectrally complex conditions. To address this challenge, we develop MetaSpecFSNet, a meta-learning-based framework designed for few-shot mangrove detection. The model is first pretrained on hyperspectral annotated data from general remote sensing scenes and then fine-tuned using samples from five representative mangrove sites, thereby reducing dependence on extensive labeled datasets. Sentinel-2 imagery from mangrove reserves in Hainan and Guangxi, China, was used for evaluation. Experimental results demonstrate that MetaSpecFSNet consistently outperforms mainstream deep learning models, including Convolutional Neural Network (CNN), Deep Neural Network (DNN), and Transformers. Leveraging the few-shot meta-learning strategy, the model achieved an average classification accuracy above 90% and an F1 score exceeding 65%. These findings highlight the potential of meta-learning to enhance ecological monitoring in environments where data are limited and spectral domains are highly complex, and provide valuable insights for evidence-based mangrove conservation and coastal management.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Feature-model strategies for remapping 1:1,000,000 vegetation map: application to the Tibetan Plateau
AU  - Wu F.
JO  - Advances in Space Research
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-05-01
VL  - 77
IS  - 9
SP  - 8982-8996
DO  - 10.1016/j.asr.2026.02.087
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.asr.2026.02.087
AB  - Obtaining high-precision vegetation maps is essential for ecological monitoring and natural resource management. However, the existing 1:1,000,000 vegetation maps are manually delineated, which introduces uncertainty and subjectivity in defining boundaries, limiting their utility for detailed management and research. This study demonstrates a reproducible method for reconstructing historical vegetation boundaries with enhanced spatial precision, with a case study on the Tibetan Plateau (TP). Landsat imagery was used as the primary data source, and recursive feature elimination (RFE) was applied to optimize feature sets. Based on selected features and three machine learning models: support vector machine (SVM), gradient boosting tree (GBT), and random forest (RF), 15 classification strategies were designed to evaluate the impact of different feature-model combinations. The results show that: (1) the strategy combining the RF classifier with remote sensing, topography, and climate features optimized through RFE achieved the highest accuracy; (2) the overall accuracy of the remapped vegetation map based on this optimal strategy is 89.03%, with a Kappa coefficient of 0.88; (3) the vegetation map produced in this study has high accuracy and improves delineation accuracy compared with existing maps, showing strong consistency with actual land surface conditions as observed in remote sensing imagery. These findings provide a reliable technical approach for remapping large-scale vegetation maps, and the final vegetation map also offers valuable basic data for vegetation change research on the TP.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Advanced machine learning based gold prospectivity mapping in the Dharwar Craton, India: A hybrid knowledge-data driven paradigm integrating ensemble and deep learning
AU  - Mitra S.
JO  - Geosystems and Geoenvironment
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-05-01
VL  - 5
IS  - 2
DO  - 10.1016/j.geogeo.2025.100473
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.geogeo.2025.100473
AB  - Developing more sophisticated as well as efficient exploration methods to identify the hidden ore bodies are necessary to meet the world wide increasing demand of mineral resources. In this regard, mineral prospectivity mapping (MPM) is crucial. This study undertakes a pioneering effort to apply and compare four machine learning (ML) models—random forest (RF), XGBoost (XGB), support vector classifier (SVC) and artificial neural network (ANN)—for gold prospectivity mapping within the Archean Dharwar Craton, India. The primary goals included the development and evaluation of these models, a systematic assessment of their comparative performance through cross-validation, feature important analysis and ultimately, production of prospectivity map. The culmination of this work is a high-resolution, combined prospectivity map, designed to produce a new prospectivity areas. Diverse geospatial data was meticulously integrated as per mineral system of the area, including geological maps, structural lineaments, geochemical, geophysical and ASTER remote sensing imagery. For model training, 79 known gold occurrences were carefully collected alongside an equal number of selected non-occurrence locations, framing the task as a supervised binary classification problem. Rigorous evaluation, employing 5-fold cross-validation and a 70:30 train-test split, confirmed the exceptional capabilities of these models. XGB and RF consistently emerged as top performers, with impressive AUC-ROC values of 0.9992 and 0.9965, respectively, coupled with high precision, recall and F1-scores and few false positives or negatives. While ANN also showed excellent performance, SVC, though strong, yielded comparatively lower metrics. A detailed feature importance analysis exhibits the positive role of Meta-Basalt, geochemical principal component 1 and Bouguer gravity anomaly and its derivative maps. Success-rate curves vividly illustrated the models' efficiency capturing over 76% of known occurrences within just 20% of the highest-ranked areas shows targeting precision. The generated combined prospectivity map, a robust synthesis from XGB, RF and ANN, based on a stringent consensus criterion validates existing knowledge and precisely delineates high-priority exploration targets, fundamentally reshaping the approach to future mineral exploration.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Flood susceptibility prediction in the Indo-China Peninsula using 21 years of inundation occurrence data and machine learning
AU  - Huang G.
JO  - Journal of Hydrology
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-05-01
VL  - 670
DO  - 10.1016/j.jhydrol.2026.135176
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jhydrol.2026.135176
AB  - Accurate flood susceptibility prediction is essential for identifying high-risk areas, supporting disaster preparedness, and mitigating socioeconomic losses. However, traditional hydrological models rely on complex physical parameters and large amounts of data, and although they have been used in small-scale flood event simulations, there are still significant research gaps in large-area flood prediction with strong spatial heterogeneity. These challenges are particularly pronounced in data-scarce and hydrologically complex regions such as the Indo-China Peninsula, where historical flood records are sparse and flood events exhibit strong spatial discontinuity. To address these challenges, this study develops a scalable, interpretable machine learning framework for flood susceptibility prediction across transboundary river basins, integrating remote sensing data with environmental variables grounded in water cycle theory. Using 21 years (2003–2023) of daily MODIS imagery at 250 m resolution, we developed a dynamic flood extraction method based on an adaptive Otsu algorithm with temporal aggregation, generating a spatially continuous inundation occurrence dataset. This dataset was used to train an Extreme Gradient Boosting (XGBoost) model incorporating twelve climate–land surface predictors, including Normalized Difference Vegetation Index (NDVI), land use, temperature, elevation, and groundwater anomalies. Model evaluation showed high predictive accuracy (R2 = 0.82, RMSE = 0.02), outperforming other common machine learning and deep learning methods in both performance and efficiency. Explainable artificial intelligence (AI) techniques, SHapley Additive exPlanations (SHAP) and partial dependence plots (PDPs), revealed NDVI, land use and temperature as dominant predictors, highlighting the significance of vegetation–climate interactions in regional flood susceptibility. Partial dependence analysis revealed a nonlinear “heat suppression” threshold. Cropland and urban areas exhibited greater sensitivity to temperature extremes, underscoring the influence of land surface changes on flood dynamics. The 21 years daily average inundation occurrence rate maps produced in this study support a range of applications, including early warning systems, infrastructure planning, and climate adaptation strategies, particularly in data-limited transboundary contexts.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - A novel non-destructive testing method for the uniaxial compressive strength of cemented paste backfill based on hyperspectral imaging and artificial intelligence
AU  - Na Q.
JO  - NDT and E International
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-05-01
VL  - 160
DO  - 10.1016/j.ndteint.2026.103654
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ndteint.2026.103654
AB  - Accurate and rapid evaluation of the uniaxial compressive strength (UCS) of cemented paste backfill (CPB) is crucial for ensuring safe and efficient underground mining. Traditional UCS tests, however, are contact-based, destructive and time-consuming, which severely limits real-time UCS monitoring and rapid feedback for in-situ backfill quality control. To overcome these limitations, this study innovatively introduces hyperspectral imaging (HSI) technology into the real-time, in-situ UCS monitoring, establishing a non-destructive testing (NDT) method by integrating HSI with artificial intelligence. A total of 120 CPB groups with five mass concentrations (61–73 %) and eight curing ages (3–28 d) were tested to obtain both UCS and corresponding hyperspectral data. The spectral response characteristics of the CPB under varying concentrations were analyzed, revealing that the reflectance gradually increased with concentration, and two distinct absorption peaks were observed near 1400 nm and 1950 nm. Two-dimensional correlation spectroscopy indicated that, under concentration interference, the spectral sensitivity was highest at 1900 nm and lowest at 1600 nm. Subsequently, the effect of various spectral preprocessing techniques and feature extraction algorithms on UCS prediction accuracy was investigated using the PSO-SVM-Bagging algorithm. The results demonstrated that the 2nd D-SG-Nor + UVE model exhibited the best performance, with R p 2 = 0.9487 and RPD = 4.4132. The three most important bands for UCS prediciton were identified as 1855.24 nm, 1240.29 nm, and 1589.91 nm respectively. Finally, comparison between the PSO-SVM-Bagging and CNN-LSTM algorithms revealed that the PSO-SVM-Bagging approach presented superior accuracy and generalization ability. This study validates the feasibility and scientific merit of applying HSI-based intelligent modeling as a NDT method for the UCS of CPB, providing a practical pathway for real-time monitoring, on-site feedback, and intelligent regulation of backfill performance in underground mining.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Urban expansion and climate variability drive hypoxia: A Landsat-based 1986–2024 dissolved oxygen climatology in China’s Greater Bay area
AU  - Zhu X.
JO  - Journal of Hydrology
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-05-01
VL  - 671
DO  - 10.1016/j.jhydrol.2026.135307
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jhydrol.2026.135307
AB  - Dissolved oxygen (DO) serves as a fundamental parameter for assessing the ecological well-being of coastal waters and is vital for sustaining the equilibrium of aquatic ecosystems. However, the trends and drivers of DO in coastal waters influenced by climate change and extensive human activities are still not well understood across vast spatial and temporal extents. Using the Greater Bay Area (GBA) as a representative urbanized coastal region, this study developed a robust ensemble machine learning (EML) algorithm to estimate DO concentrations and reconstruct a continuous DO time series from 1986 to 2024 based on Landsat satellite data. Thus, the reconstructed DO time series in the GBA were correlated with land-use and climate and factors to explore the underlying drivers of DO fluctuations. The results demonstrated that EML reliably estimated DO from Landsat Rrs, with Error, Bias, Slope, and mean absolute error of 8.31%, −0.1%, 0.30 and 0.75 mg L−1 on validation set, respectively. The DO concentrations remained relatively stable overall with an average of 6.04 ± 0.51 mg L−1, while hypoxic areas declined significantly from 1,445.2 km2 in 1986 to 271.4 km2 in 2024. The lower DO concentrations occurred in summer, with persistent hypoxia in estuarine nearshore zones. Nevertheless, historical trends indicated significant increases in DO concentrations and reductions in hypoxic extent. The SHAP-driven factor analysis indicated that these improvements were linked to land-use changes, including a reduction in cropland and urban expansion. This suggests that targeted policy interventions have mitigated the adverse effects of urbanization on coastal oxygen dynamics. These findings not only illustrate the applicability of remote sensing for reconstructing spatiotemporal DO patterns, but also offer valuable insights into the complex mechanisms underlying DO variability in coastal environments.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Lithological mapping in semi-arid shallow-covered areas using a multi-scale temporal dual attention network
AU  - Lu J.
JO  - International Journal of Applied Earth Observation and Geoinformation
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-05-01
VL  - 149
DO  - 10.1016/j.jag.2026.105292
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jag.2026.105292
AB  - Vegetation and surface deposits in semi-arid shallow-covered areas strongly mask bedrock spectral signatures, challenging conventional remote sensing lithological classification. This work proposed a deep learning framework named Multi-scale Temporal Dual-Attention Network (MSTDA-Net), which was designed to extract implicit lithology-vegetation relationships from multi-source time–series data. MSTDA-Net integrates three core modules: multi-scale temporal convolution, temporal attention (which weights the importance of different time phases), and channel attention (which weights the importance of different input features). These modules jointly capture local phenological patterns, emphasize key phenological phases, and enhance discriminative features. Using 12-phase (one phase per month) Sentinel-2 multispectral, Sentinel-1 SAR, and derived vegetation indices, a 25-dimensional monthly time–series dataset was constructed. Experiments show that MSTDA-Net achieves an overall accuracy of 97.14% and a Kappa coefficient of 0.970, outperforming all benchmark models. Ablation studies confirm the necessity and synergy of the three core modules, and feature importance analysis reveals that the model focuses on peak-growing-season signals and vegetation-sensitive features, aligning with geological principles. This work demonstrates that deep learning-based mining of vegetation phenology from multi-source time–series data can effectively infer lithology under shallow cover.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Iceberg detection from global open ocean sentinel-1 wave mode SAR data with YOLO deep learning
AU  - Xie T.
JO  - Remote Sensing of Environment
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-05-01
VL  - 337
DO  - 10.1016/j.rse.2026.115344
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.rse.2026.115344
AB  - Sentinel-1 wave mode (WV) synthetic aperture radar (SAR) routinely acquires high-resolution vignettes over the Southern Ocean, but its potential for automated and statistically oriented iceberg study has been underutilized. This work presents IByolo, a deep learning model adapted from the YOLOv11 architecture, for the detection and localization of km-sized icebergs in open ocean WV SAR data. The model was trained on a manually curated dataset of 13,686 annotated icebergs across 2062 vignettes. Sensitivity experiments over multiple architectural configurations identified the HGNetV2+C2PSA-CAA+P2 combination as optimal with AP50:95 of 0.41. IByolo generates reliable bounding boxes that enable efficient surface area estimations for icebergs within 0.1-5 km2. Application to 19,025 vignettes acquired south of 40∘[jls-end-space/]S during 2016–2018 yields 80,245 iceberg detections. Most vignettes contain fewer than ten icebergs with total surface areas below 5 km2. Icebergs are concentrated near major calving fronts in the northeastern Weddell Sea, Amundsen–Ross Seas, and coastal East Antarctica. Their population shows a pronounced seasonal cycle, with maxima in the austral summer and near-zero values in winter. This aligns with the Altimeter-based analysis for km-sized icebergs, and together they complement global observational records for further understanding of the Antarctic iceberg drift and changes. The retrieved size distribution follows a power-law scaling with an exponent of –1.42, which is consistent with prior satellite-based estimates. These findings confirm the viability of combining WV SAR data with advanced object detection frameworks for scalable iceberg monitoring. The resulting dataset would be a valuable resource for investigating Antarctic iceberg dynamics, freshwater fluxes, and their broader climatic impacts.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - 3D-OceanNet: A remote sensing data–driven model for predicting three–dimensional ocean temperature and salinity fields
AU  - Cao X.
JO  - Ocean Engineering
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-05-01
VL  - 354
DO  - 10.1016/j.oceaneng.2026.125016
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.oceaneng.2026.125016
AB  - AbstractRemote sensing provides indispensable observations for ocean monitoring, and fully exploiting these data for temperature-salinity (T-S) research is scientifically crucial. Most studies focus on predicting sea-surface variables or inverting subsurface properties, yet oceanic energy transfer and material exchange are governed by the dynamics of the full three-dimensional (3D) field. To bridge this gap, we introduce 3D-OceanNet, a deep learning framework that infers the evolution of 3D T-S structures from continuous surface observations. The model ingests multi-day satellite SST and SSHA sequences and predicts subsequent 3D T-S states. Its end-to-end architecture couples a 2D encoder with a 3D decoder, while a long-range spatio-temporal attention module links surface signals to subsurface structure, improving mapping fidelity and forecast skill. In reanalysis-based evaluations, 3D-OceanNet more faithfully reproduces the reanalysis fields than competing models, achieving lower RMSE and MAE and reduced MAPE for 1-8-day forecasts in the South China Sea and the Northwest Pacific. Independent validation with Argo profiles shows RMSE gaps relative to GLORYS reanalysis data of 0.1-0.2 °C (temperature) and 0.04-0.07 PSU (salinity), supporting accuracy, reliability, and generalization. Together, these results demonstrate that surface observations can underpin credible forecasts of 3D oceanic T-S fields and offer practical pathways for remote-sensing applications.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Downscaling GRACE(−FO) with mass-conserving XGBoost approach reveals high-resolution patterns and drivers of hydrometeorological-induced mass changes in High Mountain Asia
AU  - Wu H.
JO  - Journal of Hydrology
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-05-01
VL  - 671
DO  - 10.1016/j.jhydrol.2026.135235
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jhydrol.2026.135235
AB  - High Mountain Asia (HMA), the largest extra-polar repository of glaciers, critically regulates water resources for nearly two billion people and regional climate systems. Understanding climate impacts and regional water security requires quantifying regional mass changes at fine spatial scales, but the coarse resolution of GRACE(−FO) satellite data impedes this quantification. To address these limitations, this study proposes an XGBoost downscaling method integrated with mass-conservation correction. After obtaining preliminary downscaling results with low uncertainty and RMSE using the XGBoost model, the weighted forward modeling approach is further applied to enforce mass-conservation correction, thereby enhancing physical consistency. This correction reduces the RMSE of the regional mean time series from 0.85 cm to 0.08 cm, demonstrating that mass conservation effectively improves the downscaled solution. The downscaled solution was validated against the public ASTER-derived global digital elevation model, yielding a mean absolute trend error of only 0.53 Gt/yr, and outperforms existing Mascon solutions. Analysis identifies a pronounced atmospheric oscillation over the Caspian–Black Sea region, which induces interannual variations in moisture transport along the northern branch of the westerlies. This modulation of moisture pathways leads to oscillatory precipitation variability across Central Asia, ultimately giving rise to a distinct 6–7 year interannual mass oscillation in the Tien Shan–Pamir region. The strong correlation (greater than 0.7) between this mass variability and detrended cumulative precipitation highlights the dominant role of large-scale atmospheric circulation in shaping fine-scale hydrometeorological-induced mass changes across HMA.
