TI  - HCKNet: Hypergraph convolution and Kolmogorov-Arnold hybrid network for hyperspectral and LiDAR data classification
AU  - Chen Y.
JO  - Expert Systems with Applications
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-04-25
VL  - 307
DO  - 10.1016/j.eswa.2025.131028
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.eswa.2025.131028
AB  - Fusing hyperspectral image (HSI) with auxiliary elevation information (AEI), such as light detection and ranging (LiDAR) or digital surface models (DSM), offers substantial potential for improving land cover classification performance. However, learning rich and discriminative features from each modality and effectively integrating them remains a challenging task in multimodal remote sensing image classification. Most existing graph convolutional network (GCN)-based multimodal methods rely on simple MLP feature projections and pairwise relational modeling, which limits their ability to capture the nonlinear and higher-order intra-modality dependencies required for effective multimodal learning. To address these limitations, we propose HCKNet, a multimodal fusion network that, for the first time, extends hypergraph convolutional networks (HGCNs) to multimodal classification scenarios. To further enhance feature learning, each modality branch incorporates Kolmogorov–Arnold Networks (KANs), and a multi-feature score-weighted fusion (MFSWF) module adaptively integrates predictions from both branches. Experimental results reveal that HCKNet significantly improves overall accuracy (OA), average accuracy (AA), and Kappa on three benchmark datasets, outperforming other state-of-the-art (SOTA) methods. The code used in this study is publicly available and can be accessed at the following GitHub repository:https://github.com/xsxah/HSI-classification/tree/master.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Prototype similarity-constraint enhancement network: A few-Shot class-Incremental learning for hyperspectral image classification
AU  - Yang L.
JO  - Expert Systems with Applications
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-04-15
VL  - 306
DO  - 10.1016/j.eswa.2025.130911
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.eswa.2025.130911
AB  - In hyperspectral image incremental classification, few-shot class-incremental learning methods require multiple trainings during the incremental stage to adapt to new tasks. This makes the practical deployment of hyperspectral image incremental classification methods in real-world applications time-consuming and challenging to adapt to dynamically changing environments. Although few-shot class-incremental learning enable models to adapt to new-class while retaining old knowledge, limited labeled samples can easily lead to feature representation bias, resulting in estimation bias of classification results and exacerbating catastrophic forgetting. To address these challenges, we propose a training-free PSCEN. During the incremental stage, PSCEN does not require additional training to update the model and can be deployed with minimal resources, making it especially advantageous for real-world hyperspectral imaging applications. Furthermore, PSCEN prevents overfitting on few-shot samples and maximizes the retention of old knowledge. PSCEN mainly improves classification performance by enhancing the prototype representations of base-class and new-class. Specifically, we develop a prototype global similarity enhancement mechanism to adaptively enhance the robust representation of the base-class prototype by using the global central prototype. Additionally, the representation bias of new-class will lead to misclassification of new-class. Therefore, we propose a prototype similarity constraint calibration mechanism to improve the adaptability of new-class by constructing similarity constraint prototype to calibrate the representation bias of new-class prototypes. Numerous experiments conducted on Indian Pines, Kennedy Space Center, and Salinas have demonstrated that PSCEN offers superior efficiency and accuracy, showcasing excellent overall performance.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Cos-UMamba: Optimizing salient object detection with cosine scanning and bias-corrected feature fusion in optical remote sensing images
AU  - Wang Z.
JO  - Expert Systems with Applications
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-04-15
VL  - 306
DO  - 10.1016/j.eswa.2025.130863
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.eswa.2025.130863
AB  - Salient object detection in optical remote sensing images (ORSI-SOD) is a critical task with wide-ranging applications, including environmental monitoring, urban planning, and disaster management. However, the effective fusion of local and global features remains a fundamental challenge in this field. While existing methods attempt to achieve feature complementarity through architectural innovations, the quadratic complexity of Transformers hinders their scalability, and traditional Mamba architectures suffer from static scanning limitations and lack dynamic adaptability. Moreover, representational bias in heterogeneous feature fusion is frequently overlooked, reducing the reliability of detection outcomes. To address these challenges, we propose Cos-UMamba, a novel hybrid framework that integrates bias correction mechanisms with a dynamic omni-directional cosine scanning strategy. This approach enables global long-range modeling of complex topological structures while effectively mitigating feature fusion bias through a K-nearest neighbor (KNN)-based graph construction. By eliminating interference from non-salient regions, the proposed model significantly enhances feature representation. Extensive evaluations conducted on standard ORSI-SOD datasets, including ORSSD, EORSSD, and ORSI-4199, demonstrate the superior performance of Cos-UMamba across multiple metrics such as mean absolute error (MAE) and F-measure. These results validate its capability to advance the accuracy and robustness of salient object detection in diverse remote sensing scenarios, offering a robust tool for tackling real-world challenges in the field. The source code and dataset will be available onhttps://github.com/darkseid-arch/Cos-UMamba.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Whale identification and size estimation in satellite imagery via intelligent subtle perception
AU  - Wang S.
JO  - Expert Systems with Applications
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-04-05
VL  - 305
DO  - 10.1016/j.eswa.2025.130778
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.eswa.2025.130778
AB  - Protecting whales is vital for preserving ecological balance because they are essential to marine ecosystems. Conventional detection techniques, such as acoustic technology and visual observation, are expensive, ineffective, and susceptible to environmental influences. Although satellite remote sensing provides a more comprehensive view of whale monitoring, difficulties still exist due to the complexity of the marine environment and the scarcity of datasets. Deep learning, fortunately, is becoming a powerful tool that can accelerate the optimization of subtle perception for a variety of multidisciplinary applications that enable high-precision and accurate real-time ocean observation. Here, a comprehensive methodology is proposed to implement whale detection and size estimation in satellite images, leveraging advanced artificial intelligence and data augmentation strategies to overcome the challenges. First, several techniques, including grayscale processing, Gaussian noise addition, random flipping and cropping, histogram equalization, image overlay, and Clustering Generative Adversarial Networks, are employed to generate synthetic images to augment the whale satellite dataset. Second, a custom detection model is extended with TripletAttention (TA) modules for accurate feature extraction and detection performance in complex marine environments. The expanded dataset is then used to train the model, achieving an mAP0.5 score of 0.89, indicating high accuracy in whale detection. Next, the spatial resolution of the images is used to estimate the size of the discovered whales, providing valuable data for biological studies. Finally, the proposed method is extended to monitor whale activity in specific regions, such as Hawaii, confirming peak activity levels from December to April. This research supports the effective application of artificial intelligence in the detection of large marine species.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Predictive modeling of mangrove vegetation using spatiotemporal-PINNs approach
AU  - Chrisnanto Y.H.
JO  - Expert Systems with Applications
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-04-05
VL  - 305
DO  - 10.1016/j.eswa.2025.130766
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.eswa.2025.130766
AB  - Mangrove ecosystems are critical for coastal protection, yet their rapid loss poses significant challenges. We develop a novel hybrid forecasting framework integrating Physics-Informed Neural Networks (PINNs) with CNN-LSTM architectures to predict mangrove presence in East Luwu, South Sulawesi, Indonesia. Our key innovation lies in embedding governing partial differential equations-incorporating advection, diffusion, and logistic growth-directly into the neural network loss function, ensuring predictions satisfy fundamental conservation laws while leveraging deep learning’s pattern recognition capabilities. Using Landsat and Global Mangrove Watch data (1996–2020), we construct spatiotemporal sequences processed through a CNN-LSTM encoder that extracts latent features, which are then fused with geographic coordinates in a PINN decoder. Training over 4000 epochs with Adam (learning rate 0.001) followed by LBFGS optimization achieved loss reduction from 1.10 to 0.25, with diffusion coefficient D and growth rate r converging to (Formula presented) m2/s and (Formula presented) year−1[jls-end-space/], respectively, indicating ecosystem equilibrium. Comparative evaluation demonstrates our approach outperforms baseline models: validation metrics show IoU  ≈  0.015 and Dice  ≈  0.030 (17 % improvement over pure CNN-LSTM), with MSE  ≈  0.65 and RMSE  ≈  0.81. Physical validation confirms model adherence to conservation principles, with continuity residuals (Formula presented) s−1 and momentum residuals (Formula presented) N/m3, while predicted drag coefficients (0.85-1.38) align with empirical ranges (0.8-1.4). Ablation studies reveal the physics-informed component contributes 25 % reduction in prediction error compared to data-driven approaches alone. This work advances physics-informed machine learning for ecological forecasting and provides an interpretable, scientifically consistent tool for coastal ecosystem management.
ER  -

