TI  - Mapping snow algae in West Antarctic Peninsula from Sentinel-2 images using a machine learning based approach
AU  - Li Y.
JO  - ISPRS Journal of Photogrammetry and Remote Sensing
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-04-01
VL  - 234
SP  - 275-290
DO  - 10.1016/j.isprsjprs.2026.02.026
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.isprsjprs.2026.02.026
AB  - Snow algae are crucial terrestrial carbon sinks and light-absorbing impurities in Antarctica. However, the distribution of snow algae across the West Antarctic Peninsula (WAP) remains poorly understood due to their high spatiotemporal variability and complex polar imaging conditions. This study develops a robust, large-scale mapping framework for green and red snow algae using machine learning. Our approach outperformed traditional spectral-index and spectral-unmixing methods across multiple challenging scenes, with the Kappa score improving from 0.51 and 0.49 to 0.82. By synthesizing 2676 Sentinel-2 images, the 2019–2024 composite distribution of WAP snow algae and the interannual variability of bloom intensity were assessed. Results reveal a total bloom area of 54.34 km2, and uncover significant negative association between bloom intensity and the Antarctic Oscillation index. This research establishes a novel, transferable tool for mapping snow algae in cryosphere systems and provides a baseline for evaluating their ecological implications in a changing climate.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - A big data ensemble machine learning framework for robust land use/land cover classification with landsat time-series
AU  - Mensah E.
JO  - Remote Sensing Applications Society and Environment
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-04-01
VL  - 42
DO  - 10.1016/j.rsase.2026.102095
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.rsase.2026.102095
AB  - Accurate mapping of land use and land cover (LULC) is essential for monitoring environmental changes, particularly in mining-impacted semi-arid landscapes where anthropogenic disturbances significantly alter ecosystem structure and function. This study assesses the performance of six machine learning classifiers Random Forest (RF), Gradient Tree Boost (GTB), Support Vector Machine (SVM), k-Nearest Neighbours (KNN), Minimum Distance (MinDist), Classification and Regression Tree (CART), and three ensemble strategies (Stacked, Hybrid Stacked, and Weighted Voting) for LULC classification using Landsat 7 and 8 imageries in the Acland region, Queens-land, Australia between 2000 and 2024. Spectral bands and derived indices (NDVI, NDWI, NDBI, SAVI, EVI, BSI) were used as input features within a Google Earth Engine framework. The results indicate that RF and GTB consistently outperformed other base classifiers, while SVM showed a substantial improvement with higher-quality Landsat 8 data. Ensemble strategies, particularly hybrid stacked ensembles, further enhanced accuracy, producing smoother thematic maps, and improving separability in spectrally overlapping classes such as bare soil, mining areas, and light vegetation. Sensitivity analysis showed that classifier performance is affected by the initialization of pseudo-random number generators (seeds). Hybrid ensembles exhibited reduced variability and increased robustness between different seeds, outperforming standard stacking. Using identical seeds for both base and ensemble classifiers reduced diversity and degraded performance, underscoring the critical importance of randomization in ensemble learning. Comparisons between Landsat 7 and 8 highlight the benefits of improved radiometric quality, which reduces variability and enhances classifier stability. Overall, this study demonstrates that ensemble classification strategies provide a robust approach to long-term LULC monitoring in heterogeneous, mining-disturbed landscapes with the ability to integrate complementary classifier strengths, accommodate sensor differences, and maintain stability across random initializations.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Time cumulative effects and machine learning–driven forecasting of cyanobacterial blooms in data-sparse eutrophic lakes
AU  - Zhang K.
JO  - Journal of Hydrology
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-04-01
VL  - 668
DO  - 10.1016/j.jhydrol.2026.134966
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jhydrol.2026.134966
AB  - Cyanobacterial harmful algal blooms (CyanoHABs) critically threaten global freshwater security in lakes. Timely prediction of their magnitude and spatially explicit distribution is essential for mitigating ecological risk. Conventional mechanistic models demand massive data inputs, well-defined boundary conditions, and intensive computation, rendering them impractical for most lakes. Our proposed machine learning framework overcomes these constraints by intelligently integrating sparse monitoring stations and satellite data, and by constructing time cumulative effect variables, which are defined as the average or cumulative values of environmental variables over preceding days. Using 23 years of observations from Lake Chaohu, China, we evaluated four algorithms—Random Forest (RF), XGBoost, Artificial Neural Network (ANN), and Ridge regression, for forecasting CyanoHABs at 1/3/7-day lead times. RF achieved optimal 1-day predictions for CyanoHABs percentage (CBP) (root mean square error (RMSE) = 4.18 %, coefficient of determination (R2) = 0.81) through strategic feature selection from cumulative variables. RF outperformed other models in temporal pattern recognition and sustained robust performance across all forecast horizons (RMSE: 4.18 %–6.26 %, R2: 0.59–0.81). For spatially explicit predictions, the RF model achieved superior 1-day accuracy (RMSE = 5.05 %, F1 score = 0.84) through dynamic interpolation of sparse station data, outperforming 3/7-day forecasts. By leveraging sparse monitoring data, this computationally efficient approach enables reliable 1–7-day forecasting of spatially explicit distributions. It thus offers a transferable solution for proactive CyanoHABs management in data-sparse eutrophic lakes worldwide.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Spectral contrast polarity inversion as a transfer failure mechanism in deep learning sand dune segmentation: A cross-desert evaluation using Sentinel-1 and Sentinel-2
AU  - El Bouchti N.
JO  - Remote Sensing Applications Society and Environment
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-04-01
VL  - 42
DO  - 10.1016/j.rsase.2026.102060
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.rsase.2026.102060
AB  - Deep learning segmentation models achieve strong source-domain performance for sand dune mapping, yet their transferability across mineralogically distinct desert environments remains insufficiently characterized. This study evaluates four U-Net architectures — Optical, SAR, Early Fusion, and Attention Fusion — trained on Sentinel-1 and Sentinel-2 imagery from a Moroccan source domain under zero-shot transfer, unsupervised domain adaptation, and few-shot fine-tuning across three target environments: Morocco East, Oman, and Lyndon River West, Australia. Under zero-shot transfer, optical-informed architectures collapsed to near-zero IoU at mineralogically inverted sites (Morocco East: 0.000–0.110; Australia: 0.003–0.029), whilst the SAR U-Net achieved the sole above-baseline result at Morocco East (IoU = 0.625; +36.0%). Scalar distributional distance did not predict transfer degradation (r = −0.168, p > 0.05), whereas the sign of the normalized Sentinel-2 B12 mean was associated with all transfer failure outcomes. Unsupervised polarity inversion recovered optical model performance to the class ratio boundary without labelled data. Five-shot fine-tuning elevated optical-informed architectures above source-domain performance at Morocco East (mean IoU = 0.627–0.729 at N = 5), whilst no architecture exceeded the class ratio at Australia at any shot count, confirming a persistent performance ceiling driven by compound spectral and morphological domain shift. The proposed B12 polarity diagnostic correctly identifies transfer failure risk at a normalized mean threshold of −1.5, providing a zero-label pre-deployment screening tool. Monte Carlo Dropout entropy–error correlations inverted from positive at Oman to negative at Australia; few-shot fine-tuning partially restored calibration at Australia (Spearman ρ = +0.145 to +0.319 at N = 20).
ER  -

