TY  - JOUR
TI  - Physics-guided deep learning for geostationary satellite-based estimation of dead fuel moisture content in Southwest China
AU  - Fan C.
JO  - Remote Sensing of Environment
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-03-15
VL  - 335
DO  - 10.1016/j.rse.2026.115259
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.rse.2026.115259
AB  - Accurate large-scale estimation of forest surface Dead Fuel Moisture Content (DFMC) is critical for wildfire risk warning and scientific decision-making. While existing process-based and empirical models leveraging satellite data show utility at local scales, they exhibit inherent limitations: process-based models suffer from physical simplifications in numerical simulations, while empirical approaches lack mechanistic integration due to shallow learning architectures. This persistent gap necessitates—yet lacks—integrative frameworks that synergize physical realism with deep learning flexibility. To address this challenge, we propose a physics-guided deep learning framework that synergistically integrates geostationary meteorological satellite data and reanalysis data for regional-scale forest surface DFMC estimation. Our methodology fuses Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) neural network features with physical features derived from the process-based Fuel Stick Moisture Model (FSMM). Critically, the physical feature fuel surface relative humidity (RHsurf) is incorporated into the loss function to constrain model weights, yielding our final Physics-guided LSTM (PyLSTM) model. Validation using single-site 3079 h DFMC data from Chengdu, Sichuan, China, demonstrated PyLSTM's superior temporal performance (R2 = 0.70, RMSE = 10.60%). Spatial validation across 241 sites in Xizang, Yunnan, Guizhou, and Sichuan provinces confirmed its robust spatial accuracy (R2 = 0.71, RMSE = 16.96%), outperforming both standalone FSMM and LSTM models. PyLSTM successfully captured the declining DFMC trend preceding the Yajiang fire event, with significantly lower estimated DFMC in the burned area compared to surrounding pixels in Yajiang County. These results demonstrate PyLSTM's capability to enhance wildfire risk early warning and identify high-risk areas. Therefore, this study serves as a foundational step toward estimating hourly regional-scale DFMC dynamics—a vital factor in assessing fire danger and behavior.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Detection and visualization of water content distribution in Chuju based on hyperspectral imaging technology and machine learning
AU  - Yang Y.W.
JO  - Spectrochimica Acta Part A Molecular and Biomolecular Spectroscopy
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-03-15
VL  - 349
DO  - 10.1016/j.saa.2025.127302
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.saa.2025.127302
AB  - During the processing of dried Chuju ( Chrysanthemum morifolium Ramat ), rapid and non-destructive moisture content detection is crucial for quality control. Due to its complex structure, moisture distribution becomes highly non-uniform during drying process. However, few reports are available regarding the detection method, thus failing to meet practical production requirements. To address this challenge, this study employed hyperspectral imaging (HSI), combined with machine learning algorithms, to facilitate rapid moisture content detection and visualize its spatial distribution. Spectral data were collected from both the front and back sides of Chuju samples to develop a moisture content predictive model. Six preprocessing techniques and three machine learning algorithms were evaluated based on their performance. The results demonstrated that the combination of standard normal variate (SNV) with support vector regression (SVR) yielded optimal performance, achieving excellent prediction accuracy for both front and back side, with determination coefficient (R2p) reaching 0.986. Subsequently, pixelwise spectral analysis was performed to realize moisture distribution visualization. The findings indicated that during the drying process, moisture distribution was non-uniform; notably, the central region exhibited a significantly slower dehydration rate compared to the outer region. To ensure accurate prediction regardless of sample facing, four classification models were implemented to differentiate between the front and back sides. Among these models, linear discriminant analysis (LDA) achieved the highest classification accuracy at 98.74 %. Thus, this study provides a non-destructive and rapid method for effectively detecting moisture content in Chuju with high precision ensuring product quality, with the potential to be applied in other agricultural products.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Application of hyperspectral imaging to the automatic assessment of polyphenol oxidase and peroxidase enzymatic activity levels in bell pepper cultivars
AU  - Amoghin M.L.
JO  - Spectrochimica Acta Part A Molecular and Biomolecular Spectroscopy
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-03-15
VL  - 349
DO  - 10.1016/j.saa.2025.127279
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.saa.2025.127279
AB  - We explored the application of hyperspectral imaging (400–1100 nm) for non-destructive evaluation of peroxidase (POD) and polyphenol oxidase (PPO) enzymes responsible for browning processes in bell pepper ( Capsicum annuum L. ) cultivars. Several preprocessing techniques, including Standard Normal Variate (SNV), were applied to spectral data to enhance signal quality. Analysis using Partial Least Squares Regression (PLSR) showed that raw spectral data provided stronger correlations and lower prediction errors compared to processed. Discriminant spectral bands were identified using Support Vector Machine (SVM) combined with metaheuristic optimization, with SVM–Learning Automata (LA) resulting as the most effective wavelength selection strategy. Enzyme activities were then predicted using selected wavelengths with Artificial Neural Network (ANN) and PLSR models. Model performance was evaluated using the coefficient of determination (R2), Root Mean Square Error (RMSE), and Ratio of Performance to Deviation (RPD) on independent validation sets. ANN consistently outperformed PLSR, achieving high cultivar-specific R2 values for POD of 0.86, 0.93, and 0.98, for Orange, Yellow, and Red pepper varieties, respectively and PPO R2 values of 0.91, 0.97, and 0.99, for the same pepper cultivars. A combined “Total Model” integrating data from all cultivars further demonstrated robust generalization, with R2 values of 0.9082 for POD and 0.9604 for PPO. Findings confirm that hyperspectral imaging, coupled with an effective wavelength selection technique and ANN modeling provides a rapid, reliable, and robust approach for industrial evaluation of enzymatic activity in bell peppers. The proposed methodology offers significant potential for quality monitoring, process optimization, and large-scale application in industrial environments.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - DCIBCD: A dual-branch cooperative interaction method for interference-resistant binary change detection
AU  - Yuan Q.
JO  - Expert Systems with Applications
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-03-10
VL  - 301
DO  - 10.1016/j.eswa.2025.130210
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.eswa.2025.130210
AB  - Remote sensing binary change detection facilitates large-scale land surface change analysis and supports risk prevention tasks. Current methodologies employ multi-source image fusion to address spatiotemporal inconsistencies. However, these approaches frequently exhibit bidirectional interference issues, leading to erroneous detections and the oversight of actual changes. To address these challenges, this paper proposes a dual-branch collaborative interaction binary change detection (DCIBCD) model, which employs bidirectional interactive fine-tuning to mitigate interference effects. The DCIBCD architecture incorporates two fundamental elements: a bidirectional fine-tuned Siamese feature extractor (BFSFE) and a multi-source dfference-sensitive feature fusion (MDFF) component. The BFSFE utilizes a bidirectional refinement process to enhance localized multi-scale features, successfully capturing 1D spatial relationships through its integrated a large-scale axis mixed attention (LSAMA) mechanism. The MDFF executes simultaneous cross-branch and single-branch feature refinement to improve change identification accuracy. Comprehensive evaluations show DCIBCD’s outstanding performance on four standard datasets, attaining F1-scores of 90.44 % (LEVIR-CD), 90.52 % (WHU-CD), 96.09 % (DSIFN-CD), and 94.23 % (CDD), validating its effectiveness in resisting interference in multi-source data interaction scenarios. The code of our paper is accessible athttps://github.com/AIBox-IMU/Computer-Vision/tree/main/DCIBCD.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Machine learning for non-destructive nutrient diagnosis in citrus: comparing spectral analysis and hyperspectral imaging with CNNs
AU  - Blanco-Alvarez I.
JO  - Expert Systems with Applications
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-03-10
VL  - 301
DO  - 10.1016/j.eswa.2025.130517
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.eswa.2025.130517
AB  - Accurate nutrient assessment is critical for sustainable citrus production. This study advances non-destructive nutrient diagnosis by integrating Vis/NIR hyperspectral imaging with machine learning to predict macro- and micronutrient concentrations in ’Clementina de Nules’ mandarin leaves in commercial crops along the season. Two approaches were compared: (1) analysing mean leaf reflectance spectra using several machine learning approaches, and (2) processing full hyperspectral images with convolutional neural networks (CNNs). Models were trained using the full spectral range and a subset of wavelengths selected via a novel expert committee-based Sequential Forward Selection (SFS) algorithm to mitigate the curse of dimensionality. The mean spectral approach with a multilayer perceptron (MLP) achieved superior performance (R2 > 0.60 for most nutrients, up to 0.85 for Fe and 0.77 for B), while CNNs excelled only with selected bands (e.g., R2 = 0.82 for Fe). Traditional vegetation indices performed poorly (R2 < 0.48), highlighting the limitations of conventional methods. The modified SFS algorithm enhanced model efficiency by reducing redundant spectral data, demonstrating the potential of tailored machine-learning architectures for spectral-based nutrient diagnostics. These findings advance precision agriculture by offering robust, scalable solutions for real-time nutrient monitoring, addressing computational challenges in high-dimensional data and paving the way for optimised fertilisation management in citrus crops.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Fast contamination recognition network: a knowledge distillation based hyperspectral outdoor insulator contamination level recognition network
AU  - Zhou J.
JO  - Expert Systems with Applications
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-03-05
VL  - 300
DO  - 10.1016/j.eswa.2025.130401
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.eswa.2025.130401
AB  - Hyperspectral imaging (HSI) technology has significant engineering application potential as an innovative method for detecting contamination levels on high-voltage insulators. However, current research primarily focuses on laboratory conditions, and there is a notable lack of studies addressing the direct and effective outdoor detection of insulator contamination. Additionally, practical engineering application demands lightweight detection models that balance high accuracy with rapid inference speed and low computational complexity. Motivated by these challenges, this paper first investigates and confirms the feasibility of directly detecting outdoor contamination levels on insulators using hyperspectral imagery. Then, a novel Fast Contamination Recognition Network (FCRN) is proposed, capable of rapidly and accurately identifying the contamination levels from hyperspectral images collected under realistic outdoor conditions. The FCRN leverages an advanced knowledge distillation framework, compressing the powerful yet complex Spatial-Spectral Pyramid Network (SSPN), initially designed for hyperspectral classification, into a lightweight convolutional neural network. This compression significantly reduces computational overhead while preserving the superior spectral-spatial feature extraction capability of the larger model. Experimental results demonstrate that most tested models achieved over 80% accuracy across outdoor contamination insulators datasets, strongly confirming the practicality and robustness of outdoor hyperspectral-based contamination detection. Notably, the FCRN not only dramatically reduces the computational overhead of the model (more than 90% reduction in the number of parameters, memory, and FLOPs), but also effectively improves the accuracy of the lightweight model by 1–2%, successfully narrowing the performance gap with the much larger SSPN model. The code and dataset will be released athttps://github.com/IIMARSS/FCRNafter acceptance of the paper.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - AFSNet: Remote sensing change detection with progressive difference feature aggregation and full-scale connection
AU  - Fu Y.
JO  - Expert Systems with Applications
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-03-05
VL  - 300
DO  - 10.1016/j.eswa.2025.129994
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.eswa.2025.129994
AB  - Remote sensing change detection (RSCD) aims to identify and analyze surface changes by comparing remote sensing (RS) images captured at different time points. Achieving precise detection remains a significant challenge in all the involved evaluation measurements. To reduce missed and false detection in change regions, We propose AFSNet, a high-performance change detection model extracting features with mobile backbone networks through progressive aggregation and full-scale connection using full-scale deep supervision for enhanced feature learning. Given the computational efficiency and parameter economy of mobile backbones outperforming mainstream architectures, we introduce a residual swin transformer module (RSTM) in the backbone to effectively capture and integrate both global and local semantic information. We then design a dual residual module (DRM) to extract bitemporal difference features across multiple levels and a convolutional attention block (CAB) to adaptively reweight multi-scale encoder features for improved detection by integrating attention modules into a full-scale fusion framework. Two RS public datasets, BCDD and LEVIR-CD, are used to validate the proposed AFSNet, and the results show that the F1-score of AFSNet reaches 94.51 % and 91.68 % respectively. All code as well as detailed instructions are available athttps://github.com/tawneydaylily/AFSNet.
