TY  - JOUR
TI  - Unbiased representation learning via feature decoupling network for cross-scene hyperspectral image classification
AU  - Zhao H.
JO  - Expert Systems with Applications
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-03-01
VL  - 298
DO  - 10.1016/j.eswa.2025.129690
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.eswa.2025.129690
AB  - For hyperspectral cross-scene classification (HSIC) tasks, the model is trained on the source domains (SDs) and applied directly to the unseen target domains (TDs). For this domain generalization (DG) challenge, a significant issue is the contradiction between the overparameterized model and the limited training domain, which results in the absorption of spurious correlations from environmental features. To alleviate this contradiction, this study proposes a domain extension generator with a feature decoupling network (FDNet). The generator initially decouples the SD into reflectance and shading components, treating them as causal and environmental features, respectively. Considering possible causal residues in environmental features, the shading components are shuffled by style to eliminate undesired correlations. Then, the reflectance and sparsified shading are reconstructed for extension, enriching the training diversity without environmental interference. In addition, to enhance the stability of class-level causal representation, a supervised aggregation strategy is designed to minimize the intra-class distance of the reflectance domain, and supervised contrastive learning is employed to enhance the class-domain semantic consistency information. Comparative analysis with advanced domain generalization and adaptation approaches on three HSI datasets validates the superiority in accuracy and Kappa coefficient metrics of the proposed method.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - HiF2-FSLF: Hierarchical frequency fusion few-Shot learning framework for hyperspectral and lidar classification
AU  - Wang X.
JO  - Expert Systems with Applications
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-03-01
VL  - 299
DO  - 10.1016/j.eswa.2025.130164
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.eswa.2025.130164
AB  - Joint ground observation of hyperspectral image (HSI) and light detection and ranging (LiDAR) remote sensing offers the possibility of obtaining more fine-grained feature interpretation. However, practical applications often encounter a situation where one HSI and LiDAR region has only a few labeled samples, and sometimes none, whereas another HSI and LiDAR region with similar land cover classes has a sufficiently large number of labeled samples. This presents great challenges to traditional multimodal deep learning fusion classification. Therefore we attempt to classify an HSI scene with only a few samples with the help of a similar scene with a large sample size of both HSI and LiDAR, and propose a few-shot learning (FSL) framework based dual-source hierarchical frequency domain fusion for HSI and LiDAR classification (HiF2-FSLF). Unlike existing methods that either rely solely on HSI or perform simple feature-level fusion, HiF2-FSLF introduces a frequency-domain perspective to explicitly model the complementary information between HSI and LiDAR. Specifically, A multiscale collaborative block (MSCB) is proposed to extract shallow features of the dual-source data, and enhanced sensitivity to local information while enabling the coexistence of global large-scale information and local detailed information. Then, a feature intertwine invertible module (FIIM) is developed to capture high frequencies by adopting a step-by-step decomposition strategy. During the fusion phase, a hierarchical frequency fusion attention method (H2FAM) is designed to refine the effective features through the three-branch attentions to realize the complementary fusion between HSI and LiDAR. After extensive experiments on the publicly four datasets with only five training samples per class, the classification performance of the proposed method outperforms other state-of-the-art methods, reaching 97.83 % OA in Houston2013, 94.57 % OA in Trento, 85.20 % OA in MUUFL, 88.33 % in Augsburg.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Complementary information-guided interactive fusion network for HSI and LiDAR data joint classification
AU  - Xu S.
JO  - Expert Systems with Applications
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-03-01
VL  - 298
DO  - 10.1016/j.eswa.2025.129549
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.eswa.2025.129549
AB  - The classification of land remote sensing using single-modal data has reached a bottleneck, which has spurred significant interest in the joint utilization of multimodal remote sensing data to enhance classification performance. However, existing methods exhibit limitations in extracting intricate local and global features. Furthermore, achieving effective information interaction and deep fusion between multimodal datasets remains an unresolved challenge. To address these issues, we propose a Complementary Information-Guided Interactive Fusion Network (CIGIF-Net) for the classification of hyperspectral image (HSI) and light detection and ranging (LiDAR) data. The core idea of our approach leverages the capability of Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs) to extract local spatial features while utilizing the strengths of Transformers in modeling long-range dependencies. Furthermore, our method facilitates deep fusion by designing mechanisms for the interactive integration of multimodal local spatial features, complemented by guidance from multimodal data during long-range dependency modeling, thereby improving overall classification performance. Specifically, CIGIF-Net incorporates multiscale feature learning, interactive feature fusion, and the complementary information-guided attention mechanism. Initially, CNNs are used to learn multiscale local spatial features. Subsequently, we perform an interactive fusion of multimodal spatial information based on channel attention techniques. Finally, the complementary information-guided attention mechanism dynamically utilizes complementary insights to inform deeper attention distributions, which guide global feature construction and enable efficient information aggregation. This methodology allows for the comprehensive extraction and synergistic utilization of complementary information across multimodal datasets. Extensive experiments conducted on three widely recognized HSI and LiDAR datasets demonstrate that the proposed CIGIF-Net achieves superior classification performance.
ER  -

