TY  - JOUR
TI  - IMFF: A dual-space optimization network via multi-level feature fusion and boundary-aware learning for high-resolution remote sensing scene classification
AU  - Yuan J.
JO  - Expert Systems with Applications
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-01-15
VL  - 296
DO  - 10.1016/j.eswa.2025.129163
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.eswa.2025.129163
AB  - High-resolution remote sensing classification has emerged as a critical research frontier in geospatial analysis, particularly for urban planning and agricultural monitoring applications. Despite deep learning architectures have significantly advanced classification accuracy, two critical challenges remain: significant intra-class variance (e.g., seasonal changes in agricultural fields) and ambiguous inter-class distinctions (e.g., agricultural fields and forests exhibit similar visual characteristics). To address these issues, this study proposes a novel remote sensing image scene classification network, IMFF, a dual-space optimization architecture via multi-level feature fusion and boundary-aware learning with entropy-guided localization. Specifically, the framework is implemented through a coordinated three-pathway strategy: 1) a multi-scale context aggregation pathway used to capture hierarchical patterns through multi-level discriminative fusion structure (MDFS); 2) an information-guided refinement pathway, which is proposed to simultaneously enhance boundary discrimination via optimized laplacian-gaussian edge magnifier (LOGM) and dynamically localize semantically critical regions through high-entropy region contextualization module (HECM); 3) a discriminative feature distillation pathway effectively extracts high-order semantic information through a deep information miner (DIM). This unified optimization mechanism simultaneously enhances inter-class separability through boundary-aware learning while reducing intra-class variance through entropy-guided localization. Moreover, extensive experiments on three benchmarks demonstrate the excellent performance of the proposed model: it achieves 99.88 % accuracy on UC Merced, 97.65 % on AID, and 94.96 % on NWPU-RESISC45, outperforming existing methods while maintaining competitive efficiency (0.002s/image inference). The code is available at https://github.com/Codeater1/-IMFF-Net-.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Nationwide mapping and characterization of land subsidence in the United States using InSAR
AU  - Xiong Z.
JO  - Remote Sensing of Environment
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-01-15
VL  - 333
DO  - 10.1016/j.rse.2025.115134
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.rse.2025.115134
AB  - Land subsidence (LS) threatens public safety and sustainable socioeconomic development across the United States (US). Wide-area LS mapping plays a crucial role in improving the characterization of LS, thus contributing to a better understanding of its impacts beyond national borders. However, a nationwide LS map for the US is still lacking, and mapping LS at this scale faces challenges such as atmospheric artifacts, computational limitations, and data calibration issues. We develop a strategy for mapping and identifying LS on a nationwide scale. This strategy includes average deformation calculation, mosaicking of Interferometric Synthetic Aperture Radar (InSAR) results, and LS areas extraction using deep learning. Using this strategy and extensive SAR data, we present the first high-resolution, nationwide LS map for the contiguous U. S. (CONUS), derived from 23,391 ascending Sentinel-1 images acquired between 2019 and 2021. Our analysis reveals that LS affects all 48 states and the District of Columbia, covering ∼45,666 km2 . Approximately 73 % of LS occurs in cultivated lands, 10 % in wetlands, with groundwater overexploitation being the predominant driver of subsidence. These subsiding areas expose 6.26 million people, 160,071 buildings, and critical infrastructure, including thousands of kilometers of railways and roads. Although some measures have been implemented to mitigate LS, existing cases suggest that long-term groundwater management is essential, and significant challenges remain in addressing severe LS. This national-scale assessment provides critical data for targeted management and informs future monitoring and mitigation strategies.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Remote sensing-based bathymetry mapping in shallow lakes: comparative analysis of Sentinel-2 and Landsat-8 imagery integrated with machine learning techniques
AU  - Karimi N.
JO  - Advances in Space Research
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-01-15
VL  - 77
IS  - 2
SP  - 1439-1463
DO  - 10.1016/j.asr.2025.10.028
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.asr.2025.10.028
AB  - Accurate bathymetric mapping of shallow inland waters is critical for water resource management, sediment budgeting, and hydrodynamic modeling, yet repeated in-situ surveys are costly and often impractical. This study assesses the potential of medium-resolution multispectral satellite imagery (Sentinel-2 and Landsat-8) integrated with machine learning to generate high-quality bathymetry for Chah Nimeh No. 3 reservoir in southeastern Iran. Four widely used algorithms (Multi-Layer Perceptron (MLP), Random Forest (RF), Support Vector Regression (SVR), and XGBoost) were trained and evaluated using 52,000 in-situ water depth measurements collected via a remotely operated hydrographic survey vehicle. The MLP model achieved the highest accuracy, with Landsat-8 inputs yielding RMSE ≈ 0.82 m (R2 ≈ 0.97) and Sentinel-2 inputs RMSE ≈ 1.10 m (R2≈ 0.95), and was selected for sensor performance comparison. Hyperparameters, including the number of hidden layers, neurons per layer, and learning rate, were optimized via systematic grid search, identifying sensor-specific configurations (Sentinel-2: 9 layers, 16 neurons/layer, LR = 1e−3; Landsat-8: 11 layers, 16 neurons/layer, LR = 1e−4).Both sensors demonstrated strong predictive capability; however, the Landsat-8 model incorporating all optical and thermal infrared bands outperformed Sentinel-2. Landsat-8 achieved MAD = 0.50 m, RMSE = 0.78 m, and MAPE = 9.6 %, while Sentinel-2 produced MAD = 0.58 m, RMSE = 0.88 m, and MAPE = 10.4 % (test data: 30 % of total in-situ points). Direct spatial comparison of final bathymetric products showed a high coefficient of determination (R2 = 0.90) and minimal mean difference (+0.11 m, STD = 1.7 m), indicating strong agreement between the two datasets. Correlation analysis revealed unexpectedly strong associations between Landsat-8 thermal bands and depth (Band 10: R2 = 0.52; Band 11: R2 = 0.53). To quantify their contribution, two MLP models were compared: one using all Landsat-8 bands and another excluding thermal bands. Thermal inclusion improved performance substantially (R2 = 0.98, RMSE = 0.78 m) relative to the thermal-excluded model (R2 = 0.93, RMSE = 1.30 m), representing a 67 % increase in RMSE without thermal data. This enhancement likely reflects depth-linked thermal patterns driven by differential heat capacity, sediment heating in shallow zones, and depth-dependent sediment–water heat exchanges.These findings demonstrate that integrating freely available medium-resolution multispectral and thermal imagery with MLP can yield sub-metre bathymetric products (<5% error relative to an 18 m depth range in Chah Nimeh reservoir) in optically clear shallow lakes, providing a cost-effective and operationally valuable tool for monitoring and managing data-sparse inland reservoirs.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Advanced predictive modelling of urban expansion and land surface temperature dynamics using multi-scale machine learning approaches
AU  - Bindajam A.A.
JO  - Advances in Space Research
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-01-15
VL  - 77
IS  - 2
SP  - 1679-1702
DO  - 10.1016/j.asr.2025.10.080
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.asr.2025.10.080
AB  - Rapid urbanization alters land use and land cover (LULC), increasing landscape fragmentation and intensifying land surface temperature (LST), thereby posing significant challenges for sustainable urban planning. This study aims to systematically quantify urban expansion, assess its morphological and thermal impacts, and forecast future LST dynamics in Lucknow, India, from 1991 to 2021. Using multi-decadal Landsat data, we developed year-specific Random Forest (RF) classifiers trained on 17 features including six spectral bands (Blue, Green, Red, NIR, SWIR1, SWIR2), three indices (NDVI, NDBI, NDWI), and eight GLCM texture metrics extracted from NIR and SWIR1 bands. Spatial autocorrelation of training samples was evaluated using Moran’s I (0.35–0.48), and mitigated through spatial separation and cross-validated grid search optimization. LULC models achieved high accuracy with test accuracies of 88.00 % (1991), 86.12 % (2001), 88.13 % (2011), and 87.67 % (2021), and kappa values ranging from 0.8562 to 0.8741. LST was retrieved using the Mono-Window Algorithm (MWA), revealing a mean LST increase of ∼2.5 °C per decade, with 55 % of the region exceeding 30 °C by 2021. Morphological Spatial Pattern Analysis (MSPA) identified growing fragmentation in agricultural and open land (e.g., loss of core and bridge elements) and densification in built-up zones. Polynomial regression models (degrees 2–4) were used to forecast LST for 2031 across MSPA classes, predicting highest values in built-up cores (34.30 °C) and bridges (32.51 °C), and lowest in core agriculture (25.77 °C). These findings confirm that landscape form and composition strongly regulate urban thermal patterns. The study presents an integrated modeling pipeline linking machine learning classification, thermal retrieval, and morphological analysis to anticipate urban heat trajectories, providing critical insights for climate-adaptive land use planning and resilient urban design.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - ISFE-Net: An important spatial feature extraction network for UAV-Based hyperspectral water quality monitoring in rivers
AU  - Chen J.
JO  - Journal of Hydrology
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-01-01
VL  - 664
DO  - 10.1016/j.jhydrol.2025.134542
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jhydrol.2025.134542
AB  - Rivers are integral components of ecosystems, and monitoring nitrogen and phosphorus is critical for effective riverine water quality management. Although satellite remote sensing has been widely employed for large-scale monitoring of lakes and coastal zones, narrow river reaches occupy only a few pixels in satellite images, severely limiting its applicability. Moreover, the weak spectral responses of nitrogen and phosphorus constrain the accuracy of traditional empirical and machine learning retrieval methods. Existing deep learning approaches often overlook the extraction of essential spatial context around sampling locations, resulting in limited model generalizability and predictive performance. In this study, we introduced the Important Spatial Features Extraction Network (ISFE-Net), which leverages high spatial resolution UAV hyperspectral imagery to retrieve water quality parameters across the full Juzhang river basin as well as its upper, middle, and lower reaches. The ISFE-Net introduced a spatial feature extraction module that identifies the most important image information and filters out irrelevant noise, thereby substantially enhancing retrieval accuracy. On the testing set, ISFE-Net achieved R2 values of 0.689, 0.614, and 0.799 for NH3 -N, TN, and TP across the entire basin. The model achieved its best performance in the middle and lower reaches, where NH3 -N, TN, and TP yielded testing R2 values of 0.785, 0.787, and 0.829, with RMSEs of 0.006, 0.165, and 0.003 mg/L and MAPEs of 11.7 %, 9.2 %, and 6.9 %, respectively. These results demonstrated ISFE-Net’s strong generalizability across varied riverine environments and offer a new approach for high precision remote sensing retrieval of non-optically active water quality parameters.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Hyperspectral remote sensing and machine learning techniques for assessing soil contamination by potentially toxic elements: A case study from the Zeïda mining area, Morocco
AU  - Jamal M.
JO  - Journal of African Earth Sciences
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-01-01
VL  - 234
DO  - 10.1016/j.jafrearsci.2025.105912
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jafrearsci.2025.105912
AB  - This study integrates hyperspectral remote sensing (HRS) and machine learning (ML) techniques for assessing soil heavy metal contamination in the Zeïda mining area of Morocco, a region with a history of significant mining activity, particularly lead extraction. The study focuses on six potentially toxic elements (PTEs)—copper (Cu), chromium (Cr), manganese (Mn), cadmium (Cd), lead (Pb), and zinc (Zn)—which are prevalent in the area due to mining operations. A total of 77 soil samples were collected across various zones of the mining area, and spectral data were obtained using an ASD FieldSpec® 3 spectroradiometer. To enhance the spectral feature sensitivity, five spectral transformations were applied, with the second derivative (SD) and asymmetric second derivative (ASD) proving the most effective in improving correlations with heavy metal concentrations. Machine learning models, including Sparse Multinomial Logistic Regression (SMLR), Regression, and Backpropagation (BP), were utilized to develop inversion models for predicting the concentration of the studied metals. Among these models, the Regression model using ASD-transformed spectra showed the highest performance, with R2 values of 0.827 for Cu, 0.921 for Cr, 0.998 for Mn, and strong results for Pb, Cd, and Zn as well. These models exhibited low root mean square errors (RMSE), indicating reliable predictive power. The study demonstrates the utility of HRS and ML for large-scale, non-invasive environmental monitoring, offering a practical framework for mapping and assessing the spatial distribution of heavy metal pollution in mining-impacted regions. The findings contribute to environmental risk assessment and provide valuable information for soil remediation planning in areas with historical and ongoing mining activities.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - DSENet: dynamic sparse enhancement network for road extraction from high resolution remote sensing images
AU  - Li H.
JO  - Advances in Space Research
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-01-01
DO  - 10.1016/j.asr.2026.08.087
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.asr.2026.08.087
AB  - Road extraction from high resolution remote sensing images is a significant research area in computer vision. However, the sparse pixel distribution of fine road structures often leads to severe degradation of local features during the successive downsampling operations in conventional convolutional neural networks, making it challenging to preserve the pixel-level continuity and completeness of roads. To address these issues, we propose a Dynamic Sparse Enhancement Network (DSENet) that effectively focuses on fine road segments. Specifically, the Dynamic Sparse Enhancement Module (DSEM) is proposed to mitigate the discontinuous extraction of fine roads by estimating multi-scale spatial scores, selecting grid-constrained Top-K localized windows, and enhancing sparse fine-road features within these windows. Furthermore, leveraging the elongated geometric prior of roads, we introduce orthogonal strip convolution in the encoder to better capture long-range contextual information along horizontal and vertical directions, thereby improving road recognition accuracy. Extensive experiments on DeepGlobe and CHN6-CUG datasets demonstrate that DSENet achieves IoU scores of 65.91% and 67.65%, respectively, and outperforms the compared representative road extraction methods, achieving superior accuracy, completeness, and pixel-level continuity in road extraction.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - IACF-Net:Interactive adaptive cross fusion network for collaborative classification of hyperspectral and LiDAR data
AU  - Zhao Y.
JO  - Infrared Physics and Technology
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-01-01
VL  - 153
DO  - 10.1016/j.infrared.2025.106318
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.infrared.2025.106318
AB  - At present, the co-classification of hyperspectral images (HSI) and LiDAR data (LiDAR) has become a popular research direction for remote sensing tasks. However, Some existing methods ignore the nonlinear coupling and correlation between different bands, spatial positions, etc. in HSI data when processing it. Moreover, during the process of multimodal data fusion, some methods often lose the locally effective information among multimodal features. Addressing the above questions, this paper designs a interactive adaptive cross-fusion network(IACF-Net) based on the combination of CNN and transformer. The network first adds weight constraints to the HSI data, and then multi-layer convolutional neural network (CNN) was used to extract features from HSI data with different weights and LiDAR data. Secondly, designed a co-attention module(CAM) to realize the information exchange between HSI data and capture correlations between their features, complete the adaptive decoupling. Finally, the cross-attention fusion module (CAFM) is used to fuse HSI and LiDAR features to realize the information exchange between heterogeneous features, while retaining the local useful information. We conducted experiments on IACF-Net on three benchmark HSI and LiDAR datasets. The experimental results show that the overall accuracy (OA) of IACF-Net is higher than other advanced network models, verifying the effectiveness of IACF-Net in the collaborative classification problem of HSI and LiDAR data. This study confirms that the proposed IACF-Net, with its unique ”weighted-interactive-cross fusion” architecture, can effectively overcome the challenges of strong coupling of HSI data and loss of multimodal fusion information, providing a superior performance solution for collaborative classification of HSI and LiDAR data.
ER  -

