TY  - JOUR
TI  - STARNet deep learning model for precision irrigation management support: UAV hyperspectral monitoring of spatial heterogeneity of soil moisture content in arid apple orchards
AU  - Guo F.
JO  - Soil and Tillage Research
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-01-01
VL  - 265
DO  - 10.1016/j.still.2026.107442
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.still.2026.107442
AB  - The pronounced spatial heterogeneity of soil moisture content (SMC) in arid-region apple orchards presents significant challenges for monitoring. Furthermore, current remote sensing models are limited by their poor generalization capabilities across diverse agroecological conditions. To overcome these limitations, this study proposes an SMC prediction framework, STARNet (Spectral-Transformation Attention Residual Network), based on unmanned aerial vehicle (UAV) hyperspectral remote sensing and a dual-branch deep learning architecture. The model integrates one-dimensional convolutional neural networks (1D CNN) to extract local correlated features from spectral band sequences and two-dimensional residual networks (2D ResNet) to extract structured features from restructured spectral feature maps. Additionally, a lightweight channel attention mechanism (SE module) is incorporated to enhance the adaptive representation of moisture-sensitive bands. Using multi-date UAV hyperspectral datasets from flat irrigated and mountainous rain-fed apple orchard scenarios in Jingning County, Gansu Province, we employed a local-perturbation data augmentation strategy to improve training stability under limited field samples. Experimental results showed that STARNet maintained stable predictive performance on independent unaugmented field-measured validation sets. In the flat irrigated orchard validation set Sa2, the model achieved R², RMSE, and RPD values of 0.88, 1.52%, and 2.91, respectively; in the mountainous rain-fed orchard validation set Sb2, the corresponding values were 0.86, 1.73%, and 2.67, respectively. These results were superior to those of the best-performing traditional models under the same validation conditions. Ablation experiments indicated that the dual-branch structure and SE module improved the model’s representation of moisture-sensitive spectral information. High-resolution spatial mapping showed that near-surface SMC in the flat irrigated orchard in July had higher values near irrigation facilities, whereas the mountainous rain-fed orchard exhibited more pronounced patchy spatial differentiation. These results revealed differences in near-surface soil moisture spatial patterns across contrasting orchard scenarios. This study reduces the dependence of traditional hyperspectral modeling on manual feature selection and provides high-resolution diagnostic information for near-surface soil moisture monitoring, drought-risk identification, and precision irrigation management support in arid-region apple orchards.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - An enhanced feature fusion method for urban functional zone mapping with SDGSAT-1 day-night imagery and multi-dimensional geospatial data
AU  - Jiang H.
JO  - Remote Sensing of Environment
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-01-01
VL  - 332
DO  - 10.1016/j.rse.2025.115050
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.rse.2025.115050
AB  - Urban functional zones (UFZs) are well-planned spatial units characterized by distinct socioeconomic activities and composite land uses, such as residential areas, industrial zones, and blue-green spaces. Fine-grained UFZ mapping has played an increasingly crucial role in supporting targeted urban renewal and transformation of development mode in megacities, facilitating spatial structure optimization to enhance urban livability and sustainability. Prior UFZ mapping methods that focus on two-dimensional (2D) features of point of interest and multi-spectral imagery, pay little attention to three-dimensional (3D) features of building height and digital surface model, mostly with the absence or underutilization of emerging nighttime light imagery. Given the availability of high-quality day-night spectral signatures provided by the Sustainable Development Science Satellite 1 (SDGSAT-1) in a single sensor observing mode, it has become possible to effectively perform UFZ mapping with day-night feature enhancement. In this study, we proposed a progressive and cross-scale deep fusion architecture for generating UFZ maps at the block scale, enhancing spectral and spatial information through sequential refinement—from feature representation and relationship extraction to context modeling. To verify the effectiveness and generalizability of the proposed method, experiments were conducted in two Chinese megacities with distinct UFZ landscapes. Results demonstrated that the medium-resolution SDGSAT-1 imagery could be used as a reliable data source for deriving day-night features, enabling the generation of fine-grained UFZ maps when combined with 2D—3D features from other geospatial big data. Cross-method comparisons also showed that this approach could significantly improve both semantic segmentation and topological interpretation across different UFZ types. Notably, our method could not only achieve acceptable levels of mapping performance (overall accuracy &gt; 0.91 and average F1-score &gt; 0.91), but also realize the accurate extraction of purer UFZ blocks with a small sample size (training-testing ratio = 1:4), further indicating considerable potential in large-scale UFZ mapping. The source codes are available at:https://github.com/Sustainable-City-Lab/UFZ-data-fusion.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - MSAI-Path: Predicting Microsatellite Instability From Routine Histology Slides Without Reinventing the Wheel
AU  - Baumann E.
JO  - Modern Pathology
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-01-01
VL  - 39
IS  - 1
DO  - 10.1016/j.modpat.2025.100932
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.modpat.2025.100932
AB  - Microsatellite instability (MSI) is an important biomarker in colorectal cancer, influencing both patient prognosis and treatment decisions. Current approaches for MSI prediction from hematoxylin and eosin--stained whole-slide images (WSI) rely on end-to-end deep learning (“black-box”) models with limited interpretability, often relying on heatmaps for visualization. However, experienced pathologists can intuitively identify MSI through specific histologic features and have developed manual classification systems such as MS-Path for Lynch syndrome screening. We present a novel hybrid approach that combines computational and pathologist expertise to create an explainable and verifiable method for MSI prediction in colorectal cancer, applicable to resection and biopsy WSI. Our proposed method uses nuclei and tissue segmentation models to automatically quantify MSI-associated histologic features outlined in the Bethesda guidelines, including intraepithelial lymphocytes, grade of differentiation, mucinous components, and tertiary lymphoid structures. After validation on annotated data sets, these features are integrated with clinical data and used in logistic regression and random forest models to predict MSI status. We validated our approach using 3256 WSI from 2267 patients across 7 cohorts from 5 centers. The method achieved an area under the curve of up to 0.88 across all resection cohorts, and 0.90 on biopsies, performing on par with published black-box deep learning models. Importantly, the learned variable importances strongly correlated with manual scoring systems and aligned with manual pathologist assessments. We observed significant intrapatient heterogeneity in predicted scores, emphasizing the importance of whole-case analysis. Our approach also shows potential as a screening tool that could exclude 41% of patients from gold-standard MSI testing while maintaining 95% sensitivity. This study demonstrates that classifiers based on clinical and validated histologic information can predict MSI status as effectively as black-box models while providing complete interpretability. Our method offers an alternative pathway for understandable, explainable, and trustworthy biomarker prediction in computational pathology.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Optimal selection and fusion framework of multi-source soil moisture data based on climate zoning and cross attention
AU  - Wang E.
JO  - Journal of Hydrology
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-01-01
VL  - 664
DO  - 10.1016/j.jhydrol.2025.134350
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jhydrol.2025.134350
AB  - Soil moisture is crucial for understanding the interactions between soil, atmosphere, and hydrological processes. The limited availability of in-situ measurements, and uncertainties in satellite-based and reanalysis datasets pose significant challenges to soil moisture estimation. To enhance the accuracy of soil moisture estimation, this study proposes an optimal selection and fusion framework of multi-source soil moisture data based on climate zoning and cross attention mechanism, which allows to maximize the advantages of each soil moisture product in terms of coverage periods and quality. Specifically, the framework employs a CNN-LSTM architecture for spatiotemporal feature extraction and data fusion, where convolutional neural networks (CNN) initially capture spatial patterns from multi-source soil moisture data, and long short-term memory networks (LSTM) is followed to model their temporal dependencies. Considering different soil moisture products behave differently in different climate zones, the framework establishes several auxiliary data (ERA5-L skin temperature, precipitation and DEM)-guided cross attention weighting sub-modules for multi-source data fusion which can automatically learn the quality advantages of each product. To illustrate the framework, three types of soil moisture products (SMAP, ERA5-L and ESA CCI) and the in-situ data in the Yellow River basin are selected for data fusion. The results demonstrate that soil moisture generated by the proposed framework achieves an unbiased root mean square error (ubRMSE, m3/m3) of 0.043, showing 47%, 35%, and 17% reductions compared to the original SMAP, ERA5-L, and ESA CCI data, respectively. In addition, the proposed framework also shows robustness in climate zoning granularity, feature processing strategies, and spatial representativeness of stations. Finally, an improved soil moisture dataset (daily, 0.1°) ranging from 1950 to 2021 is produced for the Yellow River basin. This study provides a new practical framework for soil moisture estimation improvement, which will benefit basin hydrology and water resources management.
ER  -

